"use strict";(self.webpackChunkkiwi_wiki=self.webpackChunkkiwi_wiki||[]).push([["22671"],{44776(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"2026/09/08-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2026/09/08-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2026-09-08-01.md","title":"OCI Free Tier Terms Change","description":"This article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.","date":"2026-09-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud","permalink":"/en/tags/cloud"},{"inline":true,"label":"OCI","permalink":"/en/tags/oci"}],"readingTime":3.46,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026/09/08-01","title":"OCI Free Tier Terms Change","date":"2026-09-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Cloud","OCI"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Long Time No Update, Missed You All","permalink":"/en/2026/09/01-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n# OCI Changed Its Free Tier Terms\\n\\nWhile browsing around today, I noticed that OCI\'s Free Tier terms changed. Looking closer, they actually sent out a notice on June 15 this year.. but I never pay much attention to OCI\'s emails...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## OCI Always Free A1 Resources Before vs. After Update\\n\\n| Resource Item               | \u{1F504} Before Aug 2026 Update                        | \u{1F6A8} After Aug 2026 Update (Latest Policy)                        | \u{1F4A1} Account Restructuring & Ops Impact                                      |\\n| ------------------ | --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\\n| **Max OCPU per Account**  | **4 OCPU**                              | **2 OCPU** (Cut in half)                              | Excess instances will be **automatically terminated (Terminate)** by the system.                   |\\n| **Memory (RAM) per Account** | **24 GB**                               | **12 GB** (Cut in half)                               | Max quota per single VM drops to 12 GB; combined total across multiple VMs cannot exceed this either.                     |\\n| **Monthly Free Usage Limit**     | 3,000 OCPU hours / month  <br />18,000 GB hours / month | **1,500 OCPU hours / month**  <br />**9,000 GB hours / month** | **PAYG (Pay-As-You-Go) users** usually won\'t have VMs deleted for exceeding the limit, but overages will be **billed directly to the credit card**. |\\n| **Max VM Combination Examples**   | \u2022 1x 4C/24G large VM  <br />\u2022 4x 1C/6G micro VMs  | \u2022 **1x 2C/12G full-spec VM** <br />\u2022 **2x 1C/6G standalone VMs** | Resource reshuffle: ditch fragmented tiny VMs and consolidate compute to get the most value.                     |\\n| **Free Block Storage (Total Disk)**  | **200 GB**                              | **200 GB** (**Unchanged**)                          | Although disk wasn\'t shrunk, splitting across 2 VMs leaves very little space per VM\u2014still a storage pain point.             |\\n| **Free Outbound Public Traffic (Network)**   | **10 TB** / month                           | **10 TB** / month (**Unchanged**)                       | Bandwidth allowance remains super generous, great for acting as a high-bandwidth relay or sharing space.                    |\\n## Impact\\n\\nHonestly, even though my OCI setup looks super fancy right now\u2014with a WireGuard mesh and all that\u2014the only services I actually use are:\\n* Wireguard for BT download - this is to keep copyright trolls off my back\\n* V2Ray via 3X-UI - this is for friends still in <ruby><del>China</del><rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>the bandit zone</rt></ruby>\\nEverything else is just flashy fluff that makes no difference if wiped out.\\n\\nI checked carefully and haven\'t been charged yet, and it seems PAYG isn\'t being strictly enforced right now either, though with a trust shift like this it\'s best not to gamble on a corporation\'s goodwill....\\nBetter to proactively downgrade myself for peace of mind.\\n### New Plan\\n\\nHonestly I haven\'t thought it through completely yet, but my rough plan is as follows:\\nMy current three accounts will probably be split up like this:\\n\\nAccount A\\nA-1 1C6G running the current WireGuard role (basically keeping its current scale and purpose) joined to the mesh\\nA-2 1C6G for running 3X-UI and things provided to friends\\nThe nice thing about this is for security settings, only Account A needs ports `80`/`443` exposed externally, while the other accounts stay open only within the Mesh.\\n\\nAccounts B & C\\nB 2C12G I don\'t know what to do with it yet, but join it to the mesh\\nC 2C12G I don\'t know what to do with it yet, but join it to the mesh\\n\\nWith the specs cut in half, AI workloads that barely couldn\'t run before definitely can\'t run now.... so I have even less idea what to use them for....\\nStill, I\'ll probably reorganize things first.\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://img.shields.io/badge/Kiwi-%E6%89%80%E8%AC%82%2C%E9%9B%96%E7%84%B6%E6%88%91%E4%B8%8D%E7%9F%A5%E9%81%93%E5%8F%AF%E4%BB%A5%E5%B9%B9%E5%98%9B%2C%20%E4%BD%86%E6%98%AF%E6%88%91%E4%B8%8D%E8%83%BD%E6%B2%92%E6%9C%89%E5%B0%B1%E6%98%AF%E9%80%99%E5%80%8B%E6%84%8F%E5%A2%83-A8FF24?style=social&logo=kiwix&logoColor=EA7500\\" height=\'40\' />\\n### Bonus\\n\\nSince Watchtower has been acting weird lately, I plan to switch to a `systemd timer` triggering `docker compose up -d` to automatically update my Docker images.\\nOnce I get that set up, I\'ll update my wiki with it as well."},{"id":"2026/09/01-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2026/09/01-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2026-09-01-01.md","title":"Long Time No Update, Missed You All","description":"More random rambling","date":"2026-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/blog"}],"readingTime":6.88,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026/09/01-01","title":"Long Time No Update, Missed You All","date":"2026-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","description":"More random rambling","tags":["Life","Blog"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"OCI Free Tier Terms Change","permalink":"/en/2026/09/08-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"New Blog Launch","permalink":"/en/2026/04/20-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nEver since I started adjusting how the new Blog/Wiki is presented back in April, I haven\'t touched any of this for a long time.\\nPartly because I got sucked into vibe coding, and partly because work has been hectic.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Recent Status Report\\n\\nEven though nobody really cares about what a middle-aged guy has been up to, I still want to jot things down like a diary\u2014organizing what I\'ve been doing lately and what I plan to do next.\\n\\n## Vibe Coding\\n\\nTo cut to the chase: it failed. Not only did I fail to build anything useful, I even paid for a full year of `Gemini AI Pro`. Still, there are a few takeaways I want to leave behind.\\n\\nSimply put, the failure of this vibe coding experiment can\'t be blamed entirely on Google. Even though plenty of people online say Gemini is trash at coding, blah blah blah, objectively speaking, the main reasons for the failure came down to two things:\\n\\n* Not having a clear idea of what I actually wanted to build\\n\\tStrictly speaking, it\'s because I lack creativity. I came up with a bunch of ideas that seemed promising, only to realize in the end that the return wasn\'t worth the effort, there wasn\'t a real need, or someone had already written a great open-source project I could just use directly.\\n\\tThat killed my motivation to keep burning brain cells on it.\\n\\n* Knowing too little about programming concepts\u2014even when AI wrote a mountain of code, I couldn\'t read it or tell where problems might arise\\n\\tThis issue was glaringly obvious. From structuring a program\'s framework, planning architecture, choosing a language, and defining variables, to keeping the AI from going off the rails across multiple turns and designing for future maintenance and revisions from the start\u2014all of that was far beyond my ability to plan ahead. In other words, no matter how good AI is at writing code, if the human drafting the blueprint has an empty head, it\'s still useless (okay, this applies to plenty of other domains too, like governing a country, a company, or a project).\\n\\nSo for now, that whole effort is completely on hold. That said, strictly speaking, if I just ask AI to write simple shell scripts\u2014things I can actually understand\u2014it\'s still super handy.\\n\\n## Work-Related Stuff\\n\\n### Office Politics\\n\\nThought I was going to spill something here?? Nope. I\'m not dumb enough to leak work details in a place like this. Just making a note for myself: emotionally, this period at work left me with some pretty unpleasant memories, so I\'m leaving a footnote.\\n\\n### Slackin\' on the Clock\\n\\nStrictly speaking, this is about the company issuing me a new laptop. This machine is pretty interesting: a Lenovo ThinkPad P16 Gen 3 with an NVIDIA RTX PRO 1000 GPU and two SSDs. I took advantage of a loophole during the company\'s Windows installation\u2014one of the SSDs wasn\'t mounted, formatted, or brought under management\u2014and installed Arch Linux on it for my writing setup. Now I can have a working Arch Linux environment even at the office without relying solely on WSL all the time (though my work Windows still has WSL too).\\n\\nAs for the story of this Arch Linux setup, I might write more about it later if I feel like it\u2014or just skip it if I don\'t.\\n\\n### New Toy Acquired\\n\\nBasically, I picked up a retired company Dell Latitude 5411 on the cheap and brought it home. After thinking about it for a while, I decided to turn it into my very first personal Arch Linux laptop.\\n\\n## Main Time Sink\\n\\nEverything above was just trivial stuff over the past few months. What I\'ve really been thinking about and working on lately is the following.\\n\\n### Rebuilding the Home Entertainment Center\\n\\nFor a long time now, I\'ve wanted to completely overhaul my home gaming setup. Ideally, I\'d have a machine in the living room capable of streaming or running games up to a certain performance tier natively, while heavy-hitting PC AAA titles could either be played directly in the study or streamed out to the living room. So I took inventory of all the machines I already have (all old hardware) and started planning the revamp.\\n\\n#### NVIDIA SHIELD TV Pro\\n\\nThis is currently the main set-top box for watching videos in my living room. I once tried installing RetroArch and Moonlight on it, and here\'s what I found:\\n* Moonlight runs, but it depends heavily on a gaming streaming machine running Sunshine.\\n* RetroArch runs smoothly up through the SNES (SFC) era, though storage space is a major issue.\\n\\n#### Steam Deck\\n\\nAs my main handheld, I\'ve actually been thinking about switching it to another Steam account, setting up emulators properly, and moving the whole thing along with its dock into the living room to replace our current Switch (since the Switch Joy-Cons are broken... and the screen has cracks too).\\nHowever, this approach hits a couple of roadblocks:\\n* The Steam Deck\'s emulation capability starts struggling past the PS2 era\u2014though it really is my best living-room console option right now.\\n* It\'s my personal toy, and I really don\'t want the kids wrecking it.\\n\\n#### Dell Latitude 5411\\n\\nThis machine has zero value for gaming because it has no discrete GPU and only 8GB of RAM\u2014it contributes nothing meaningful to gaming. However, because it exists, it took over the Steam Deck\'s role as my casual Linux desktop.\\nThat\'s the only reason I can free up the Steam Deck in the first place.\\n\\n#### MSI GL73\\n\\nThis gaming laptop equipped with an NVIDIA RTX 2060 Mobile is currently my best-performing gaming machine, running Windows\u2014the most game-friendly OS.\\n\\n### The Catch\\n\\nThe vision sounds great, but reality is brutally harsh\u2014I ran into problems before even starting the work.\\n\\nThe biggest issue is that this home entertainment center isn\'t meant for just me alone. Almost all existing tools and approaches assume one big premise:\\n\\n`Owner of the games = Player = Controller of everything`\\n\\nThe moment you have to factor in kids playing too\u2014controlling their playtime and restricting which games they can play\u2014you immediately land on the solution of adding a Steam child account into a Family Group so you can manage kids\' playtime and game libraries via Steam\'s built-in features. As for emulators, since storage on a living-room device is limited, you simply put only curated games on it from the start. That sounds lovely, right? But here\'s the problem: if the living-room machine is powerful enough on its own and doesn\'t need streaming, everything is solved\u2014it\'s just a standalone console. What if you need a beefier streaming host to run games that demand high-end hardware?\\n\\nLong story short, I tested running games on the Steam Deck via Steam Link over my home network, and \\"utterly unplayable\\" doesn\'t even begin to describe it...\\nMeanwhile, the Moonlight/Sunshine approach has no account isolation whatsoever (since it streams the entire desktop). So if I want to include AAA titles, I\'d have to dedicate a streaming host for them, and on top of that figure out how to stream the games *I* want to play, how to separate accounts and isolate sessions... Things get way too complicated, and I\'d need to add more machines to pull it off.\\n\\n#### Implementation Progress So Far\\n\\nRight now, I\'ve been unifying my bare-metal Arch Linux setups and aligning their configurations closely with the Steam Deck\'s SteamOS + EmuDeck integration\u2014organizing my previously neglected emulator resources while also paving the way to eventually stop using Windows altogether...\\n\\n### Conclusion\\n\\nIf I give up on living-room streaming, I could repurpose the Steam Deck into an emulation box plus some kid-friendly / living-room-friendly Steam games. The only catch is I\'d effectively lose my handheld, or have to share the console with the kids...\\nUntil I buy a new gaming rig, this isn\'t a bad compromise\u2014assuming the kids\' mom doesn\'t make me kneel on a washboard and dismantle the whole thing for making it too convenient for the kids to play games..."},{"id":"2026/04/20-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2026/04/20-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2026-04-20-01.md","title":"New Blog Launch","description":"Actually just some rambling","date":"2026-04-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/blog"}],"readingTime":0.84,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2026/04/20-01","title":"New Blog Launch","date":"2026-04-20T00:00:00.000Z","description":"Actually just some rambling","tags":["Life","Blog"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Long Time No Update, Missed You All","permalink":"/en/2026/09/01-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Automated Multimedia Setup Adjustments","permalink":"/en/2026/02/04-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nA while ago I wanted to use Obsidian to manage my Wiki, and in the process of switching the Wiki platform, I ended up switching the Blog as well.\\n\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Thoughts\\n\\nHonestly, when it comes to the Blog theme, I personally preferred the original `Hexo+NeXT`, but alas, for backend integration I switched it over to the new `[Docusaurus](https://docusaurus.io/blog/releases/3.10)`. I had to heavily customize the CSS to get the UI somewhat close to the original. Still, the backend integration is pretty nice\u2014before, I had to write each document one by one in `Marktext`, whereas now I just open a new Note in `Obsidain`, write it up, and git push when it\'s done....\\n\\nA lot actually happened during this period of tweaking, but I\'m too lazy to note it all down.. I\'ll write about the details one by one later as they come up."},{"id":"2026/02/04-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2026/02/04-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2026-02-04-01.md","title":"Automated Multimedia Setup Adjustments","description":"Adjustments to my current Servarr configuration","date":"2026-02-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Multimedia","permalink":"/en/tags/multimedia"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Automated Multimedia Setup Adjustments","date":"2026-02-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Multimedia","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2026/02/04-01","description":"Adjustments to my current Servarr configuration"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"New Blog Launch","permalink":"/en/2026/04/20-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"UniFi Controller Broke Again (and Again) Because of an AUR MongoDB Upgrade~~~","permalink":"/en/2025/12/10-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nTo fix the issue that had been bothering me for a long time with `lidarr`\'s API connecting to `Musicbrainz`, I made some adjustments to my <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>automated free pirated media setup</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">automated multimedia collection setup</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Adjustments\\n\\n## Lidarr\\n\\n### Incident\\n\\nThe root cause was that `Musicbrainz` changed its API-related settings, causing `lidarr` to fail to retrieve metadata from `Musicbrainz` properly, which basically rendered `lidarr` useless.\\n\\n### Countermeasure\\n\\nSimply put, the ultimate solution was to <ruby><font color=\\"lightgreen\\" size=\\"+3\\">wait</font></ruby>. Once you waited long enough, the `lidarr` dev team actually released an updated version of `lidarr` to work with `Musicbrainz`\'s new settings (though the text search part is still beyond saving).\\n<ruby><font color=\\"red\\" size=\\"+3\\">BUT</font></ruby> since everyone couldn\'t wait earlier, before the dev team fixed it, people started using modified builds or adding mods. As a result, those who used a modified build found that upgrading directly to the new official version didn\'t work either\u2014which led to the painful ordeal that followed.\\n\\n### The Tortuous Process\\n\\nBecause this old guy <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>proactively adopted the best solution available at the time</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">couldn\'t wait six or seven months and got itchy fingers</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, after the update I had no choice but to <ruby><font color=\\"red\\" size=\\"+3\\">completely wipe</font></ruby> the entire `lidarr` config, then reconfigure everything and re-import the files... Yeah... that process really was long and exhausting...\\n\\n\\n\\n### Differences Between Old and New\\n\\nThe biggest difference is that the fixed `lidarr` still cannot use `text search` like before to search directly by artist name or album name and `add new`. Instead, you have to go to `Musicbrainz` first to look up the artist\'s `Artist ID` or the album\'s `Release group ID`.\\n\\nThen go into `lidarr`\'s `add new` and use:\\n\\n```bash\\nlidarr:<artist ID/Release group ID>\\n```\\n\\nAnd just like before, select the cover that pops up and add it into `lidarr`.\\n\\n## Subtitles\\n\\n### Reason\\nNothing special really\u2014just that the author of the `ChineseSubtitle` plugin I used previously got too busy and abandoned the project...\\n\\n### Replacement\\n\\nAfter looking around, I switched to [Bazarr](https://www.bazarr.media/). The setup was just as simple\u2014just spin it up with Docker.\\n\\n### Impressions\\n\\nBazarr is basically part of the *arr family too, so it integrates very well with the rest of my multimedia stack. However, with subtitles, it\'s like: if you can find them, you find them; if you can\'t, you can\'t\u2014just like \\"whoever gets more votes wins, whoever gets fewer votes loses,\\" a truism that\'s still basically stating the obvious...\\n\\n## Networking\\n\\n### Automedia Setup Adjustments\\n\\nOriginally, based on reference guides, I wrapped the entire automedia stack (including `radarr`, `sonarr`, `lidarr`, `jackett`...) inside `Gluten`. However, `Gluten`\'s VPN has been acting up lately (honestly, I\'m not even sure how long it\'s been broken).\\nFirst I noticed `jackett` + `flaresolverr` were acting weird, so I moved that pair out of `Gluten`.\\nThen while fixing `lidarr` (yep, the one mentioned above...), I found that `add new` search couldn\'t connect... After decisively moving `lidarr` out of `Gluten`, it worked immediately...\\nSo I moved everything in the entire automedia stack out of `Gluten` except `qbittorrent`. After all, that matches my ideal setup anyway\u2014only `qbittorrent` goes through the VPN to stay safe, while the other services don\'t waste VPN bandwidth...\\nAnd then I realized the issue mentioned in the reference guides\u2014that they wouldn\'t be able to talk to each other if not placed together\u2014had disappeared... (probably because I deliberately assigned static IPs to every container in automedia...)\\n\\n### VPN - ERROR [vpn] getting public IP address information:\\n\\nJust add `PUBLICIP_ENABLED=false` under `enviroment` in `docker-compose.yml` and it\'s fixed.\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nYou really shouldn\'t expect an open-source solution to run forever with the exact same config file without adjustments (even commercial software needs minor tweaks across version updates...).\\nFortunately, the changes were minor, so it should be good for another few years."},{"id":"2025/12/10-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2025/12/10-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2025-12-10-01.md","title":"UniFi Controller Broke Again (and Again) Because of an AUR MongoDB Upgrade~~~","description":"Chronicles of wrestling with UniFi Controller upgrades","date":"2025-12-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Network","permalink":"/en/tags/network"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.49,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"UniFi Controller Broke Again (and Again) Because of an AUR MongoDB Upgrade~~~","date":"2025-12-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Network","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2025/12/10-01","description":"Chronicles of wrestling with UniFi Controller upgrades"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Automated Multimedia Setup Adjustments","permalink":"/en/2026/02/04-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Plasma 6.4.. What Can I Say About You","permalink":"/en/2025/07/16-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nRecently I got a bit diligent about updating my system... and then my UniFi Controller blew up right under a single `yay -Syu --noconfirm`.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Incident Log\\n\\nAfter firing off `yay -Syu --noconfirm` and letting it run all the way through... the UniFi Controller Web UI became unreachable.\\nStarted investigating and found:\\n\\n* `systemctl status unifi.service` reported active and ok, yet `netstat -an` showed no listening port for UniFi Controller.\\n\\n* Checked `/var/log/unifi/server.log` and saw `exit code=62`.\\n\\n* Checked `systemctl status mongodb.service` and saw `exit code=62` there too; looking into it revealed MongoDB couldn\'t start normally because the database version was too old.\\n\\n* Checked `/var/log/mongodb/mongodb.log` and confirmed the MongoDB data version was 7.0, while the updated `mongodb-bin` package in the AUR was version 8.2\u2014too big of a version gap, so it refused to start.\\n\\n* Switched to installing the `mongodb80-bin` package, which allowed upgrading MongoDB\'s major version to 8.0 via commands.\\n\\nThe frustrating detour part:\\n\\n* MongoDB\'s database upgrade concept:\\n  \\n  * MongoDB cannot skip multiple versions in one jump when upgrading a database (yes, MongoDB\'s binary version and database version aren\'t automatically coupled\u2014they\'re separate and must be upgraded manually).\\n  \\n  * Going from 7.0 to 8.2 requires stepping 7.0 --\x3e 8.0 --\x3e 8.2. Generally a newer version is compatible with the previous version\'s database, whereas a newer database cannot work with an older binary.\\n\\n* The chaotic detour experience:\\n  \\n  * First I removed both the `unifi` and `mongodb-bin` packages, installed `mongodb80-bin` instead, and upgraded the admin database from 7.0 --\x3e 8.0 --\x3e 8.2.\\n  \\n  * Then I removed `mongodb80-bin` again and reinstalled `mongodb-bin` and `unifi`. `systemctl status mongodb.service` looked fine, and `systemctl status unifi.service` looked fine too, yet UniFi Controller still wouldn\'t come up\u2014checking `/var/log/unifi/server.log` still showed `exit code=62`.\\n  \\n  * No matter how much I looked around, I still couldn\'t figure out how to upgrade UniFi\'s own MongoDB database (this is probably because this old guy hadn\'t put skill points into the right tree... so I looked completely clueless here).\\n  \\n  * And so I got stuck with the main MongoDB admin database upgraded fine, while UniFi\'s MongoDB database couldn\'t be upgraded....\\n  \\n  * Finally I checked UniFi\'s `PKGBUILD` file and discovered... its dependency just requires *any* `mongodb`....\\n  \\n  * In the end, pairing `mongodb80-bin` + `unifi` worked because 8.0 is compatible with a 7.0 database, and it magically came right back to life.....\\n  \\n  \\n# Key Technical Takeaways\\n\\nThe two main things learned from this incident:\\n* MongoDB concepts and upgrades \u2014 I still need to look into how to upgrade MongoDB databases other than `admin` (honestly I couldn\'t even list the databases).\\n* I need to look into how to upgrade UniFi in the future without hitting landmines. I tried running it in Docker before, though I didn\'t stick with it due to networking issues\u2014maybe it\'s time to dig that back up."},{"id":"2025/07/16-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2025/07/16-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2025-07-16-01.md","title":"Plasma 6.4.. What Can I Say About You","description":"My struggles fighting with KDE","date":"2025-07-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.39,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Plasma 6.4.. What Can I Say About You","date":"2025-07-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2025/07/16-01","description":"My struggles fighting with KDE"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"UniFi Controller Broke Again (and Again) Because of an AUR MongoDB Upgrade~~~","permalink":"/en/2025/12/10-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Finally Ran Into It... Arch Linux Bricked...","permalink":"/en/2025/06/16-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nFollowing that previous Archlinux crash incident I thought had happened, I got trolled by KDE Plasma yet again....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Tragedy Strikes Again!?\\n\\nA great man haunted by a scumbag spirit once said, \\"People keep repeating the same mistakes... good grief!\\"\\nYes, once again I got trigger-happy with `yay -Syu --noconfirm` followed by a bunch of `sync` commands and `sudo reboot`.\\nAnd then my VNC couldn\'t connect again.... Checked it out and found that my `plasma-x11` option had vanished...\\n\\n# The Truth Is Always One!\\n\\nYep, the culprit was KDE Plasma... Digging through a bunch of posts, I discovered that starting from Plasma 6.4, the X11 session was removed from the default installation...\\nSo I started hunting for a `KDE + Wayland` remote desktop solution again...\\n\\nThen, right as I was trying out `krdp` and getting frustrated with its performance... I discovered:\\n\\n## `Turns out you can manually install the X11 session~~~~`\\n\\nSo without hesitation, I slapped the machine with a `yay -S  plasma-x11-session --noconfirm`.\\nAnd the problem is ~~solved~~ postponed until Plasma 7 comes around.\\n\\n# Ramblings\\n\\nAlthough I know the whole community has been shouting that Xorg is too old and pushing to gradually migrate to Wayland..\\nRight now, in practice, I really haven\'t found a remote desktop solution that feels more efficient than `vnc`.. Maybe in the not-too-distant future,\\nI\'ll switch everything over to `moonlight/sunshine`, because all the current `rdp` solutions on Linux feel pretty sluggish to use\u2014\\nnowhere near as smooth as Windows...."},{"id":"2025/06/16-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2025/06/16-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2025-06-16-01.md","title":"Finally Ran Into It... Arch Linux Bricked...","description":"Bad luck hitting a crash after an update","date":"2025-06-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Finally Ran Into It... Arch Linux Bricked...","date":"2025-06-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2025/06/16-01","description":"Bad luck hitting a crash after an update"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Plasma 6.4.. What Can I Say About You","permalink":"/en/2025/07/16-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Novels... I\'d Die Without Light Reading...","permalink":"/en/2025/04/08-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nActually, it\'s not really Arch Linux\'s fault... (Okay, okay, I know Arch Linux is true love...)\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# The Crash\\n\\nI used to see people say that if you use Arch Linux, you need to be mentally prepared for it to randomly die after an update. Having used it for nearly 10 years (Wait!! Am I really that old already? I started using it in 2015...), honestly, I had never once experienced a crash from an update, so I had a hard time understanding why anyone would refuse to update just to avoid breaking things....\\n\\nUntil last week, when I casually hit my remote desktop with a `yay -Syu --noconfirm` and then discovered... there was no \\"then\\". The machine looked powered on, yet remote connections failed. I went into ESXi to check the Console and it was a completely black screen.... It... bricked..\\n\\n# Root Cause\\n\\nHow would I know what the root cause is... I don\'t even know how to code..\\n\\nHowever, I ran the following tests:\\n\\n* Installed Manjaro with sddm + KDE plasma - Works normally\\n* Installed EndeavourOS with KDE - LiveDVD boots, but black screen after a fresh install\\n* Re-install\\n\\t* Tried options like `linux-6.14`, `linux-lts-6.12`, etc., and it still booted to a black screen after installation and reboot\\n    * Switched to gdm + KDE Plasma - Works, though inside ESXi only Wayland works normally\\n\\nAnd all of these issues also occurred when tested under VMWare Workstation 17 Pro....\\n\\n## Suspected Causes\\n\\n1. sddm does not get along with VMware\u2014aside from Manjaro, anything using sddm gives a black screen on boot\\n2. X11 does not get along with gdm\u2014even though using gdm shows a display, trying to log into a plasma-x11 session runs into issues\\n\\nBased on the two points above, ~~Broadcom forgot to pray to the gods~~ VMware has been disliked by everyone ever since its change of ownership...\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nMy current setup is `gdm+KDE Plasma`, but because wayland-plasma doesn\'t support VNC and this machine is mainly used as my `VDI`, I used `systemd` to run `vncserver@:1.service` so the machine starts a plasma-x11 session over VNC on boot.\\nSo far it hasn\'t had any issues, though it looks like I\'ll eventually have to migrate from ESXi to PVE.\\n\\nOn a side note, this incident didn\'t affect my other console-only Arch Linux machines, so strictly speaking it doesn\'t really count as Arch Linux bricking. Great!! My confidence in Arch Linux didn\'t drop\u2014it actually went up (wait, what??)"},{"id":"2025/04/08-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2025/04/08-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2025-04-08-01.md","title":"Novels... I\'d Die Without Light Reading...","description":"Finding and downloading novels","date":"2025-04-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Live","permalink":"/en/tags/live"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":5.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Novels... I\'d Die Without Light Reading...","date":"2025-04-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Live","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2025/04/08-01","description":"Finding and downloading novels"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Finally Ran Into It... Arch Linux Bricked...","permalink":"/en/2025/06/16-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bought It... Yet Human Desire Is Truly Bottomless...","permalink":"/en/2025/02/26-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Foreword\\n\\nNot having novels to read, just like not having manga to read, is going to kill me.....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Japanese Light Novels\\n\\nOriginally, I always went to [MobiNovels (\u9B54\u7B46\u5C0F\u8AAA)](https://mobinovels.com/) to grab the Japanese light novel e-books uploaded by the site owner, but later the site owner had a daughter and accidentally lost the site, so for a while I didn\'t update my own library...\\nUntil recently, when I discovered something great.\\n\\n## Tampermonkey - Greasy Fork - Wenku8 Downloader (\u8F7B\u5C0F\u8BF4\u6587\u5E93\u4E0B\u8F7D)\\n\\nThis is truly a blessing, because it can automatically convert novels on [Wenku8 (\u8F15\u5C0F\u8AAA\u6587\u5EAB)](https://www.wenku8.net)\u2014currently the site with the largest free collection\u2014into `epub` format and pack the illustrations and cover image inside before downloading...\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Screenshot_20250408_160310.png\\" alt=\\"Wenku8 download\\" style={{ width: \\"1000px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />\\n\\nNotes\\n\\n* Brave users need to turn off `Brave Shields`, otherwise the script will be blocked from running\\n* For those who want the filenames directly in Traditional Chinese, please click `CC\u958B\u555F` (Enable CC) first\u2014it will convert the filenames to Traditional Chinese\\n* Downloading all together merges all volumes into a single file for download\\n* If there is a problem downloading any image, it will throw an Error instead of giving you an `epub` with missing images\\n\\n# Chinese Power-Fantasy Web Novels\\n\\nChinese power-fantasy web novels are trickier\u2014basically you have to find websites offering `full-book txt` downloads. Currently, I go to [Free Novel Net (\u514D\u8CBB\u5C0F\u8AAA\u7DB2)](http://www.12z.cn/index.html) to download the full-book txt files.\\nBecause Chinese web novels easily run into millions of words and generally only contain text anyway, I don\'t expect covers or illustrations...\\n\\n## Converting to `epub`\\n\\nSince I always read on a `kindle`, I forcibly convert txt files to epub for archiving. Also, since I work in a Linux (penguin) environment most of the time now, the conversion tool naturally has to run on Linux.\\nYou ask why not use a web-based converter? Mainly because Chinese web novels have too much text, free online converters can\'t handle them, and I just want to stay a freeloader..\\n\\n### ~~txt2epub~~\\n\\n~~[txt2epub github](https://github.com/kevinboone/txt2epub) - There are many tools with the same name; I picked this one because it can be installed with `yay`, while most others are installed via `pip`.\\nOn `Archlinux`, I just run `yay -S txt2epub` and it\'s done...~~\\n\\n~~Basic syntax\\n\\n```bash\\n[Kiwi]#> txt2epub -o </path/filename.epub> --author <author Name> --title <book title> --cover-image <filename.jpg> chapter01.txt chapter02.txt chapter03.txt\\n```\\n\\n~~Notes~~\\n\\n* ~~This thing is purely a CLI command. If you want a GUI, use `pip` to install another Python txt2epub\u2014I saw [a GUI version](https://github.com/k4yt3x/txt2epub)~~\\n* ~~Make sure the text is `unicode` before converting, otherwise there will be issues~~\\n\\n### kaf-cli\\n\\nBefore this, I used `txt2epub` for conversion, but found the text formatting of the converted files was a complete mess, so I switched to [kaf-cli](https://github.com/ystyle/kaf-cli), which can also be installed directly with `yay`.\\nThe syntax is very similar to `txt2epub`.\\n\\n\\n\\nAn excerpt of the instructions from GitHub is as follows:\\n\\n> Command-line mode parameters\\n\\n```\\nUsage of kaf-cli:\\n  -align string\\n        \u6807\u9898\u5BF9\u9F50\u65B9\u5F0F: left\u3001center\u3001righ\u3002\u73AF\u5883\u53D8\u91CFKAF_CLI_ALIGN\u53EF\u4FEE\u6539\u9ED8\u8BA4\u503C (default \\"center\\")\\n  -author string\\n        \u4F5C\u8005 (default \\"YSTYLE\\")\\n  -bookname string\\n        \u4E66\u540D: \u9ED8\u8BA4\u4E3Atxt\u6587\u4EF6\u540D\\n  -bottom string\\n        \u6BB5\u843D\u95F4\u8DDD(\u5355\u4F4D\u53EF\u4EE5\u4E3Aem\u3001px) (default \\"1em\\")\\n  -cover string\\n        \u5C01\u9762\u56FE\u7247\u53EF\u4E3A: \u672C\u5730\u56FE\u7247, \u548Corly\u3002 \u8BBE\u7F6E\u4E3Aorly\u65F6\u751F\u6210orly\u98CE\u683C\u7684\u5C01\u9762, \u9700\u8981\u8FDE\u63A5\u7F51\u7EDC\u3002 (default \\"cover.png\\")\\n  -cover-orly-color string\\n        orly\u5C01\u9762\u7684\u4E3B\u9898\u8272, \u53EF\u4EE5\u4E3A1-16\u548Chex\u683C\u5F0F\u7684\u989C\u8272\u4EE3\u7801, \u4E0D\u586B\u65F6\u968F\u673A\\n  -cover-orly-idx int\\n        orly\u5C01\u9762\u7684\u52A8\u7269, \u53EF\u4EE5\u4E3A0-41, \u4E0D\u586B\u65F6\u968F\u673A, \u5177\u4F53\u56FE\u6848\u53EF\u4EE5\u67E5\u770B: https://orly.nanmu.me (default -1)\\n  -filename string\\n        txt \u6587\u4EF6\u540D\\n  -font string\\n        \u5D4C\u5165\u5B57\u4F53, \u4E4B\u540Eepub\u7684\u6B63\u6587\u90FD\u5C06\u4F7F\u7528\u8BE5\u5B57\u4F53\\n  -format string\\n        \u4E66\u7C4D\u683C\u5F0F: all\u3001epub\u3001mobi\u3001azw3\u3002\u73AF\u5883\u53D8\u91CFKAF_CLI_FORMAT\u53EF\u4FEE\u6539\u9ED8\u8BA4\u503C (default \\"all\\")\\n  -indent uint\\n        \u6BB5\u843D\u7F29\u8FDB\u5B57\u6570 (default 2)\\n  -lang string\\n        \u8BBE\u7F6E\u8BED\u8A00: en,de,fr,it,es,zh,ja,pt,ru,nl\u3002\u73AF\u5883\u53D8\u91CFKAF_CLI_LANG\u53EF\u4FEE\u6539\u9ED8\u8BA4\u503C (default \\"en\\")\\n  -line-height string\\n        \u884C\u9AD8(\u7528\u4E8E\u8BBE\u7F6E\u884C\u95F4\u8DDD, \u9ED8\u8BA4\u4E3A1.5rem)\\n  -match string\\n        \u5339\u914D\u6807\u9898\u7684\u6B63\u5219\u8868\u8FBE\u5F0F, \u4E0D\u5199\u53EF\u4EE5\u81EA\u52A8\u8BC6\u522B, \u5982\u679C\u6CA1\u751F\u6210\u7AE0\u8282\u5C31\u53C2\u8003\u6559\u7A0B\u3002\u4F8B: -match \u7B2C.{1,8}\u7AE0 \u8868\u793A\u7B2C\u548C\u7AE0\u5B57\u4E4B\u95F4\u53EF\u4EE5\u67091-8\u4E2A\u4EFB\u610F\u6587\u5B57\\n  -max uint\\n        \u6807\u9898\u6700\u5927\u5B57\u6570 (default 35)\\n  -out string\\n        \u8F93\u51FA\u6587\u4EF6\u540D\uFF0C\u4E0D\u9700\u8981\u5305\u542B\u683C\u5F0F\u540E\u7F00\\n  -tips\\n        \u6DFB\u52A0\u672C\u8F6F\u4EF6\u6559\u7A0B (default true)\\n  -unknow-title string\\n        \u672A\u77E5\u7AE0\u8282\u9ED8\u8BA4\u540D\u79F0 (default \\"\u7AE0\u8282\u6B63\u6587\\")\\n  -volume-match string\\n        \u5377\u5339\u914D\u89C4\u5219 (default \\"^\u7B2C[0-9\u4E00\u4E8C\u4E09\u56DB\u4E94\u516D\u4E03\u516B\u4E5D\u5341\u96F6\u3007\u767E\u5343\u4E24 ]+[\u5377\u90E8]\\")\\nPS: \u5728darwin(mac\u3001osx)\u4E0A-tips\u53C2\u6570\u8981\u8BBE\u7F6E\u4E3Afalse\u7684\u65B9\u6CD5 kaf-cli -filename \u5C0F\u8BF4.txt -tips=0\\n```\\n\\n> Command-line mode instructions\\n> Convert \u5168\u804C\u6CD5\u5E08.txt and set the author name to \u4E71\\n\\n```\\n# windows 10: win + s \u641C\u7D22powershell \\ncd d:/\\nd:/kaf-cli.exe -author \u4E71 -filename d:/\u5168\u804C\u6CD5\u5E08.txt\\n\\n# linux / mac\u4E0B, \u628Akaf-cli-linux/kaf-cli-darwin\u91CD\u547D\u540D\u4E3Akaf-cli, \u653E\u5230\u7528\u6237\u76EE\u5F55\\n# \u628A\u5C0F\u8BF4\u548Ckaf-cli\u653E\u5230\u7528\u6237\u76EE\u5F55\u4E0B,  \u6253\u5F00\u7EC8\u7AEF\u6267\u884C\\ncd ~\\n./kaf-cli -author \u4E71 -filename ./\u5168\u804C\u6CD5\u5E08.txt\\n\\n# \u5982\u679Ckaf-cli\u653E\u5230path\u91CC\u4E86, \u6216\u8005\u5728aur\u5B89\u88C5\u7684\u53EF\u4EE5\u6267\u884C:\\nkaf-cli -author \u4E71 -filename ~/\u5168\u804C\u6CD5\u5E08.txt\\n\\n# \u547D\u4EE4\u884C\u7684\u7B80\u5355\u6A21\u5F0F\uFF08\u529F\u80FD\u548C\u62D6\u62FD\u6A21\u5F0F\u4E00\u6837\uFF09\\nkaf-cli ~/\u5168\u804C\u6CD5\u5E08.txt\\n```\\n\\n# Update to kindle\\n\\nBasically, for Japanese light novels, I just throw them into my light novel library \u2014 `Calibre-Web` \u2014 and use `Calibre-Web`\'s built-in feature to send books to my Kindle, so there\'s no problem there. However, I haven\'t set up a Calibre-Web instance for Chinese web novels yet (maybe later if the collection grows, or I\'ll put them into my general books Calibre library).\\nRegardless of the future, right now if I want to manually push them to Kindle, I rely on\\nAmazon\'s official [send to kindle](https://www.amazon.com/sendtokindle), which has a web-based uploader.\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Screenshot_20250408_163222.png\\" alt=\\"send tokindle\\" style={{ width: \\"1000px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />\\n\\nOnce it\'s done, you can download it on your Kindle and enjoy reading non-stop..."},{"id":"2025/02/26-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2025/02/26-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2025-02-26-01.md","title":"Bought It... Yet Human Desire Is Truly Bottomless...","description":"A record of impulse spending","date":"2025-02-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Live","permalink":"/en/tags/live"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.89,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Bought It... Yet Human Desire Is Truly Bottomless...","date":"2025-02-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Live","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2025/02/26-01","description":"A record of impulse spending"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Novels... I\'d Die Without Light Reading...","permalink":"/en/2025/04/08-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"WSL Struggle Chronicles","permalink":"/en/2025/01/08-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nHaving no money while harboring deep desires is truly tormenting.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Desires\\n\\n## TV\\n\\nAt the end of last year, I finally decided to get a TV for the living room, because I really couldn\'t stand the whole family cramming into my study to watch movies together on a single 32\\" monitor...\\nAfter thinking it over again and again, I bought this cheap 86\\" TV.\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/000001_1737016623.webp)\\n\\n## TV BOX\\n\\nAfter using the TV\'s built-in Google TV for a while, I noticed that videos with external/separate subtitle files easily suffered from subtitle out-of-sync issues and the like. So the beast inside couldn\'t hold back anymore, and I bought what is currently hailed as the most powerful TV BOX \u2014 the `Nvidia Shield TV Pro 2019`.\\nYes, even though it\'s from 2019, it somehow still has the best performance on the market... Makes you wonder whether the hardware was overkill back then or other brands are just being stingy with specs...\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/000001_1739785749.webp\\" alt=\\"\\" style={{ width: \\"640px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />\\n\\n### Thoughts & Impressions\\n\\n* Damn expensive \u2014 It actually cost over 6k NTD. Even the latest Google TV Streamer 4K (a 2024 product) only costs around 3k+ NTD, yet this five-plus-year-old piece of hardware still sells for this much...\\n* The SoC is genuinely fast \u2014 I looked up the Nvidia Tegra X1+ (the same chip as the Nintendo Switch... roughly 5th-gen i5 level performance). It\'s about twice as fast as the MediaTek chip in the Google Streamer 4K (though power draw is triple at 15W).\\n* 4K AI Upscaling \u2014 Actually, this feature was the main reason I bought it, and it\'s also its biggest selling point. However, in initial use my untrained eyes can\'t tell a huge difference yet... Plus not every app supports it (for example, Duboku doesn\'t trigger Upscaling), though maybe I just haven\'t configured it right\u2014I\'ll try again soon.\\n* Gaming stream \u2014 Since I don\'t use `Geforce Now`, I can\'t comment on it, but I have used `moonlight` to stream my Steam games (Ace Combat 7). It looks pretty decent and pairs smoothly with a Bluetooth controller.\\n\\nThe next step will probably be gradually turning it into part of an entertainment center without messing up Android (besides Emby which I already use, maybe streaming games via moonlight... or running something like an emulator collection).\\n\\n## Gaming Server (Under Consideration)\\n\\nLately I\'ve been stuck in a `To be or Not to be` state...\\nIt started because what I really want is a laptop with a dedicated GPU to run a Linux host (since right now, outside of the Steam Deck\'s desktop mode, I don\'t have a physical Linux terminal to use), and I\'d really like to set up a penguin machine as a daily driver (a toy that can actually get work done).\\nConsidering that buying a brand-new laptop outright doesn\'t seem as cost-effective as building a new Gaming Server and freeing up the gaming laptop currently filling that role to repurpose it, I dove headfirst into researching desktop builds.\\nHere\'s what makes it tough:\\n\\n* Haven\'t followed desktop builds and specs in ages \u2014 Since 2011, I haven\'t bought any desktop that wasn\'t meant to be a server; I switched completely to laptops. My shopping habits also changed from going to Guanghua Digital Plaza to buy parts to browsing `PChome`, ordering online, and waiting for delivery at home... Going back to DIY feels really unfamiliar now, and I have no mental baseline left.\\n* Gap between budget and expectations \u2014 My mental model was still stuck in the era when laptops cost more than desktops. After looking around, I have to admit that the PMs behind every laptop spec actually worked hard to balance the configuration... Looking at desktops makes it way too easy to fall into the trap of \\"just spend a bit more for better specs,\\" only to blow past the budget by the end\u2014yet sticking to the budget leaves specs feeling slightly lacking and hard to swallow.\\n  After all that, it feels like only winning the lottery could solve it.... =_=!!\\n  \\nIt\'s gotten to the point where I\'m thinking I might as well put that money into stocks instead... That\'d probably give a better return...\\nBesides, the moment I turn on my computer, two kids pull chairs up on my left and right, squeezing into my 120cm-wide desk and asking \\"Daddy, what are you doing...\\" It really kills any urge to buy one..."},{"id":"2025/01/08-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2025/01/08-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2025-01-08-01.md","title":"WSL Struggle Chronicles","description":"Freeloading with WSL on a company PC","date":"2025-01-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/en/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.63,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"WSL Struggle Chronicles","date":"2025-01-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Windows","Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2025/01/08-01","description":"Freeloading with WSL on a company PC"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Bought It... Yet Human Desire Is Truly Bottomless...","permalink":"/en/2025/02/26-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Miscellany","permalink":"/en/2025/01/03-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAfter updating `qemu`, the default WSL kernel turned out to lack TC module support, which set me down the path of no return: compiling my own Linux kernel...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Story\\n\\nActually, the story is pretty much what the preface already said (so why am I still rambling?).\\nA few extra details are below:\\n\\n* My WSL runs on my `company` Windows machine, and my Local Administrator privileges were revoked. Plus, since I\'m not in an R&D department, getting them back is basically hopeless, meaning I can\'t update the WSL version via `wsl --update`.\\n* Even if I *could* run `wsl --update`, I checked the WSL-Kernel source on GitHub and the default config doesn\'t have TC or QoS kernel support anyway... (Wait, so my first point above... yeah, it was purely complaining about having no permissions...)\\n* The WSL kernel doesn\'t support dynamically loading modules (in other words, the `modporbe` command is completely useless in WSL...), so there\'s no way to load TC/QoS dynamically.\\n\\nSo... this old guy had no choice but to configure and compile the kernel himself to satisfy his needs.\\n\\n# Technical Details\\n\\nI\'ll put those in <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[my Wiki](https://kiwi0093.github.io/wiki/wiki/Linux/WSL/5-WSL_Kernel/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [WSL Custom Kernel](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs/HomeLab/Linux/WSL/w-0020))*\u2014feel free to check it out if you\'re interested.\\n\\n# Reflections\\n\\nA long, long time ago, back when I used `Gentoo`, I configured and compiled a Linux kernel (how could you use `Gentoo` without doing it yourself??), but after that I never bothered doing it manually again (just like long, long ago when I was diligent enough to configure and compile my own `FreeBSD` kernel, whereas now I just use `GENERIC`).\\n\\nHonestly, this hands-on attempt looked intimidating at first, though in practice it wasn\'t bad at all...\\nYou just have to get used to the fact that for a WSL kernel, everything is either enabled or disabled\u2014anything you want to use has to be built-in rather than built as a module. This is basically the same as early `FreeBSD` kernels, and conceptually different from how mainstream `Linux` kernels are used today.\\n\\nAnd under that model, the hard part\u2014aside from the specific features you know for sure you need (like `TC/QoS` for this time, plus `wireguard` support which I use a lot)\u2014is remembering which modules are essential for WSL itself. That\'s why every guide you see recommends running:\\n\\n`zcat /proc/config.gz > .config`\\n\\nor\\n\\n`cat /proc/config.gz | gunzip > .config`\\n\\nto dump the currently running WSL kernel config as your baseline, and then simply add whatever else you need on top. That approach simplifies away all the parts that would otherwise fry your brain.\\n\\nFinally... once everything is working properly, WSL is still a blast to use\u2014especially when you\'re slacking off at the office on a `company Windows PC` you aren\'t allowed to wipe, WSL really is the salvation of us wage slaves..."},{"id":"2025/01/03-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2025/01/03-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2025-01-03-01.md","title":"Miscellany","description":"Miscellany","date":"2025-01-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"V2ray","permalink":"/en/tags/v-2-ray"},{"inline":true,"label":"Network","permalink":"/en/tags/network"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":4.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Miscellany","date":"2025-01-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["V2ray","Network","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2025/01/03-01","description":"Miscellany"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"WSL Struggle Chronicles","permalink":"/en/2025/01/08-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Domain Migration","permalink":"/en/2024/12/04-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article is reprinted from old site archives.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nIt is already 2025. Although there isn\'t much to sigh about, I have indeed tweaked a few things recently.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# BT over VPN\\n\\n`A colleague was sued by a copyright troll and had to pay a settlement...`\\nThis incident more or less planted a seed of worry in my heart... So when I saw the introduction related to Gluetun mentioned in `Ivon\'s Blog`, I got eager to try it out.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\nI have updated it to <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[My Wiki](https://kiwi0093.github.io/wiki/wiki/VM&Container/Service%20Container/Home%20Lab/3-auto-chasing-drama/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [Automated Media Binge-Watching Wiki](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs/HomeLab/Docker/Media/ArrStack/w-0060))*! Interested folks can refer to my approach.\\n\\n## Thoughts\\n\\nIt seems to work for now, but qBittorrent\'s efficiency doesn\'t look very good. Plus, I haven\'t successfully set up `port forwarding` yet, so I am actually considering whether to pay for ProtonVPN\'s service...\\nTo be honest, ProtonVPN\'s plans and pricing (VPN Plus 2-year plan at 4.99 USD/month) actually seem quite viable...\\n\\n# V2Ray on Cloudflare\\n\\nAfter leaving China, I haven\'t spent much effort or skill points on anti-censorship wall-circumvention tools anymore...\\nHowever, while watching YouTube last time, I discovered that you can set up a serverless V2Ray server using free `Cloudflare Workers`, and it even comes with multiple IPs that can be refreshed and changed...\\nI can only say it is truly a blessing...\\n\\n## Edgetunnel\\n\\n[Edgetunnel from cmliu](https://github.com/cmliu/edgetunnel)\\n\\nActually, the moment I found this project, 90% of the setup was already done. The remaining part is having a `Cloudflare` account\u2014though you might not even need one since CF Workers automatically generate a URL like `worker_name.User_name.workers.dev` for you. However... as someone who has previously set up V2Ray WebSockets over TLS myself, hosting on a custom domain under CF can be considered a standard setup... so none of these should be an issue, unless someone starts completely from scratch with nothing at all, in which case they might really need to put in some effort to get a domain...\\n\\nNext, just create a free Worker project, paste the code from GitHub into the Worker editor, ~~go to an [online UUID generator](https://www.uuidgenerator.net/) to get a pleasing UUID~~ put any arbitrary string as a token into the 4th line `let userID = \'<uuid you like>\';`, and deploy it.\\n\\nThen go to the Worker\'s Settings -> Domains and Routes to configure your domain, or resign yourself to using the `workers.dev` link (though I only tested that it connects; not sure what might happen if actually used), and visit `https://your.domain.or.workers.dev/UUID` to see a screen similar to the one below:\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/\u87A2\u5E55\u64F7\u53D6\u756B\u9762%202025-01-03%20171436.png)\\n\\n## Client\\n\\n### Windows\\n\\nBasically `V2RayN` works. Just import the subscription group, refresh, and you\'re good to go.\\n\\n### Android\\n\\nUnfortunately, `V2RayNG` does not support group subscription features, so I had to test [ClashMeta for Android](https://github.com/MetaCubeX/ClashMetaForAndroid) instead. I installed it directly from GitHub using `Obtainium`...\\nDetailed configuration methods... too lazy to write them out. Basically anyone who knows how to use `V2RayNG` will know how to configure it themselves from a subscription URL, just by filling in `https://your.domain.or.workers.dev/UUID` into the URL field.....\\n\\n### iOS\\n\\n~~No hardware... so not tested. Even friends who have hardware are far away in China, so let\'s skip testing... too troublesome~~\\nUpdated: Tested `singbox` app using a colleague\'s iOS device. It looks pretty good, but you have to comment out `127.0.0.1` in the source code first, and all nodes must be online in order to use it. Not sure if it\'s due to my bad configuration or what, but overall the user experience was pretty poor...\\nMight need to spend more time looking into it.\\n\\n### MacOS\\n\\nDo you think someone who doesn\'t even have iOS hardware would have MacOS hardware??? (If I really had one, I probably would have used it to install Asahi Linux anyway...)\\n\\n## Reflections\\n\\n* ~~Indeed, being outside of China and lacking the pressure of the `GFW` makes it really hard to ignite my passion for testing a bunch of things~~ Without the network environment inside China, I can\'t optimize IPs specifically for Jiangsu...\\n* Same as above, so I actually ~~don\'t know how effective the created nodes really are... can only look at user feedback~~\\n  Based on feedback from friends physically located in China, it\'s much better than my previous nodes and the speed is fast enough...\\n* I used to feel that living in China required spending a bunch of skill points on tunneling tools. Although skill is skill... learning things always brings a sense of achievement, especially when they are easily applied practically. But looking back without absolute necessity, it really is a waste of skill points...\\n* Looking at the VPNs sold nowadays\u2014many using the WireGuard protocol while tunnels in China are almost all disguised as WebSocket-based SOCKS servers\u2014I feel quite wistful, especially since I need Wireguard every day to connect back home.\\n  Feeling wistful that WireGuard is so easy to set up and extremely efficient... I suppose one can only helplessly accept that one cannot choose where they are born."},{"id":"2024/12/04-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2024/12/04-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2024-12-04-01.md","title":"Domain Migration","description":"Record of migrating my domain","date":"2024-12-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Network","permalink":"/en/tags/network"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.41,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Domain Migration","date":"2024-12-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Network","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2024/12/04-01","description":"Record of migrating my domain"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Miscellany","permalink":"/en/2025/01/03-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Hooked on Ansible....","permalink":"/en/2024/11/14-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nGandi renewal fees got too expensive, so I jumped over to Cloudflare....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Background\\n\\nOriginally I bought my domain from [Freenom](https://www.freenom.com/en/index.html?lang=en), and later due to issues on their platform, their management rights for `.com` domains were revoked, so it was transferred directly to [Gandi](https://www.gandi.net/zh-Hant).\\nActually, Gandi is also a very large DNS registrar with pretty solid services, but their renewal fee is just too expensive\u2014about 37 USD per year... (my original price on Freenom was around 10 USD per year).\\nSo I migrated over to the much cheaper [Cloudflare](https://www.cloudflare.com/zh-tw/).\\n\\n# Cloudflare DNS Registrar\\n\\nCloudflare is a huge internet services company. A long time ago I had already pointed my DNS to Cloudflare for management; their free tier alone is already great to use.\\n\\n## Cloudflare Pricing\\n\\nCloudflare doesn\'t publish a public pricing list, so I found a reference table [online (Reddit)](https://www.reddit.com/r/webdev/comments/17lpxa6/cloudflare_domain_registrar_pricing_table/?rdt=46259).\\nAn excerpt is below:\\n\\n| TLD    | Base Purchase Price | Base Renewal Price | ICANN Fee | Total First Year Price | Total Renewal Price | Average Annual Cost Over 5 Years |\\n| ------ | ------------------- | ------------------ | --------- | ---------------------- | ------------------- | -------------------------------- |\\n| co.uk  | $4.71               | $4.71              | $-        | $4.71                  | $4.71               | $4.71                            |\\n| me.uk  | $4.71               | $4.71              | $-        | $4.71                  | $4.71               | $4.71                            |\\n| org.uk | $4.71               | $4.71              | $-        | $4.71                  | $4.71               | $4.71                            |\\n| uk     | $4.71               | $4.71              | $-        | $4.71                  | $4.71               | $4.71                            |\\n| win    | $3.98               | $4.98              | $0.18     | $4.16                  | $5.16               | $4.96                            |\\n| work   | $6.00               | $6.00              | $0.18     | $6.18                  | $6.18               | $6.18                            |\\n| us     | $6.50               | $6.50              | $-        | $6.50                  | $6.50               | $6.50                            |\\n| com.co | $8.00               | $8.00              | $-        | $8.00                  | $8.00               | $8.00                            |\\n| cc     | $8.00               | $8.00              | $-        | $8.00                  | $8.00               | $8.00                            |\\n| net.co | $8.00               | $8.00              | $-        | $8.00                  | $8.00               | $8.00                            |\\n| nom.co | $8.00               | $8.00              | $-        | $8.00                  | $8.00               | $8.00                            |\\n| com    | $9.59               | $9.59              | $0.18     | $9.77                  | $9.77               | $9.77                            |\\n| net    | $9.92               | $9.92              | $0.18     | $10.10                 | $10.10              | $10.10                           |\\n| org    | $9.93               | $9.93              | $0.18     | $10.11                 | $10.11              | $10.11                           |\\n| xyz    | $10.00              | $10.00             | $0.18     | $10.18                 | $10.18              | $10.18                           |\\n| vip    | $11.00              | $11.00             | $0.18     | $11.18                 | $11.18              | $11.18                           |\\n| app    | $12.00              | $12.00             | $0.18     | $12.18                 | $12.18              | $12.18                           |\\n| me     | $12.85              | $12.85             | $-        | $12.85                 | $12.85              | $12.85                           |\\n| cloud  | $15.00              | $15.00             | $0.18     | $15.18                 | $15.18              | $15.18                           |\\n| wiki   | $19.00              | $19.00             | $0.18     | $19.18                 | $19.18              | $19.18                           |\\n| blog   | $20.00              | $20.00             | $0.18     | $20.18                 | $20.18              | $20.18                           |\\n| games  | $20.00              | $20.00             | $0.18     | $20.18                 | $20.18              | $20.18                           |\\n| co     | $22.00              | $22.00             | $-        | $22.00                 | $22.00              | $22.00                           |\\n| tv     | $25.00              | $25.00             | $-        | $25.00                 | $25.00              | $25.00                           |\\n| plus   | $25.00              | $25.00             | $0.18     | $25.18                 | $25.18              | $25.18                           |\\n| zone   | $25.00              | $25.00             | $0.18     | $25.18                 | $25.18              | $25.18                           |\\n| market | $28.00              | $28.00             | $0.18     | $28.18                 | $28.18              | $28.18                           |\\n| media  | $28.00              | $28.00             | $0.18     | $28.18                 | $28.18              | $28.18                           |\\n| io     | $36.00              | $36.00             | $-        | $36.00                 | $36.00              | $36.00                           |\\n\\nThese prices are only for reference, because after transferring, the actual amount I paid was 10.XX USD (transferring forces a mandatory one-year renewal...).\\n\\nAlso, it looks like you definitely have to bind a credit card, though since the price is the same no matter how many years you buy at once... I might as well just leave it on auto-renew...\\n\\n## Mail Alias\\n\\nRight now, my domain\'s email uses Zoho\'s service, though honestly I don\'t have the greatest impression of it...\\nCloudflare actually lets you alias domain emails to Gmail; after some setup, besides receiving mail, you can also send mail using your own domain:\\n\\n* [HalisUnreal\'s Blog](https://blog.halisunreal.com/posts/use-your-own-domain-email/)\\n* [Configuring a Custom Domain Email on Gmail Using Cloudflare](https://mskclover.com/2024/07/31/custom-email-on-gmail-by-cloudflare/)\\n* [Sending and Receiving Custom Domain Email with Cloudflare + Gmail](https://www.waterwater.moe/posts/2024/2024-01-01_%E4%BD%BF%E7%94%A8-cloudflare--gmail-%E5%AE%8C%E6%88%90%E8%87%AA%E5%AE%9A%E4%B9%89%E5%9F%9F%E5%90%8D%E9%82%AE%E7%AE%B1%E6%94%B6%E5%8F%91/)\\n\\nLater on, I might still consider migrating email too just to cut down on having too many service platforms. Anyway, since it can map to any Gmail account, I can just register a random Gmail account and map it there.\\n\\nThat makes it easy to set up email addresses for family members as well.\\n\\n\\n\\n# Summary\\n\\nMoney is hard to earn, so it is more practical to spend a little effort organizing these `essential expenses` and keeping them to a minimum."},{"id":"2024/11/14-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2024/11/14-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2024-11-14-01.md","title":"Hooked on Ansible....","description":"A log of wrestling with Ansible","date":"2024-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.63,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Hooked on Ansible....","date":"2024-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2024/11/14-01","description":"A log of wrestling with Ansible"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Domain Migration","permalink":"/en/2024/12/04-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-10","permalink":"/en/2024/10/15-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Foreword\\n\\nOriginally, I wanted to pull myself together and write some `shell script`s... Then halfway through, I thought some steps might be simpler to write using `ansible`.\\nOnce I started writing... I became irretrievably hooked on it....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# What is Ansible?\\n\\nAnsible is an automation tool developed by RedHat. Through Python and SSH, it achieves \\"one `control machine` rules them all\\".\\nIt\'s not like I hadn\'t tried it before, but back then I just thought Ansible was slow, complicated, and hard to understand.... So I messed around with some beginner stuff and gave up.\\nThis time, something suddenly clicked in my head, and as I kept writing, it started covering more and more ground.\\n\\n## Related Tutorials\\n\\nHere are some references and videos for beginners who want to try using Ansible:\\n\\n### Documents\\n\\n* [Ansible Community Documentation](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/index.html)\\n* [Ansible Community Documentation (CN)](https://docs.ansible.org.cn/ansible/latest/index.html)\\n* [Ansible Authoritative Chinese Guide](http://www.ansible.com.cn/index.html)\\n\\n## YouTube Tutorial Videos\\n\\n[![Getting Started with Ansible - Learn Linux TV](http://img.youtube.com/vi/3RiVKs8GHYQ/0.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLT98CRl2KxKEUHie1m24-wkyHpEsa4Y70 \\"Playlist - Getting started with Ansible\\")\\n\\nJay from Learn Linux TV offers lots of useful Linux tutorial videos (though mostly focused on the basics). Besides Ansible, you can also browse his other topic playlists.\\n\\n## Previous Roadblocks\\n\\nPreviously, I mainly got stuck on two things when using Ansible:\\n\\n### Using Variables / Loops\\n\\nLoops in Ansible almost always go hand-in-hand with variables. If you don\'t know how to define the `\\"{{  }}\\"` Jinja2 variable syntax, you basically can\'t use loops.\\nAn Ansible playbook without loops becomes bloated\u2014the more machines and repetitive tasks you have, the more verbose and sluggish it runs.\\n\\n### Conditionals\\n\\nThis is also a huge point. Just like with `shell script`, if you don\'t know how to use `if ..else ..fi` to check states, you won\'t get very far writing a `shell script` either....\\n\\n## Other Addictive Parts\\n\\n## include_task, include_roles\\n\\nPreviously, I already liked Ansible\'s `roles` feature\u2014after all, it lets you break down a huge `playbook.yml` into smaller, easier-to-write `roles`, reducing the writing burden and enabling reuse.\\nHowever, `roles` are static: whatever you write there will definitely be called and executed. When combined with `include_task` and variable checks, though, you can inspect a `node`\'s properties (such as `os_family` to automatically detect the OS type) and `dynamically` load the corresponding `task.yml`. That expands the coverage of a single `playbook.yml` to almost every machine (especially for someone like me who likes using different `distribution`s for different types of servers/workstations).\\n\\n# Remaining Issues\\n\\n## Still Don\'t Understand Advanced Usage\\n\\nAnsible is a tool that\'s constantly evolving with tons of people diving deep into it, so there are still quite a few complex conditional checks and loop patterns I don\'t really understand (yeah... the kind where I don\'t even understand someone else\'s example...).\\nSo the `playbook.yml` I\'m writing right now still looks pretty rough, and its efficiency isn\'t optimized to the max, though at least it works.... Then again, my `shell script` writing level is about the same... so I guess they\'re six of one, half a dozen of the other...\\n\\n## Beautifying Output\\n\\nThis one is purely asking for extra trouble... Although you can set `display.skipped.host=false` in `ansible.cfg` to hide skipped items and make the output look nicer\u2014or even go further and set `display.ok.host=false` so nothing shows up except changed items....\\nOr I saw a trick using `callback` parameter tweaks so nothing displays unless explicitly defined (kind of like only showing text from `echo` inside a `shell script`).\\nTruth be told, I don\'t really need to spend effort on this anyway, since I have no intention of publishing my Ansible project externally.... Either way, I\'m the only one using it, so it doesn\'t matter if it\'s ugly\u2014good enough is good enough.\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nFirst off, since my Ansible progress has jumped way ahead compared to before, I now have a bunch of notes waiting to be updated into the wiki.... (Procrastination is about to strike...)\\nSo for the foreseeable future, I\'m going to have plenty of things to do... How exciting~~ (Wait, what?!)\\nI\'ve dug myself a hole... now let\'s see if I can fill it in later."},{"id":"2024/10/15-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2024/10/15-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2024-10-15-01.md","title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-10","description":"A bunch of random ramblings","date":"2024-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Docker","permalink":"/en/tags/docker"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-10","date":"2024-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Linux","Docker","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2024/10/15-01","description":"A bunch of random ramblings"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Hooked on Ansible....","permalink":"/en/2024/11/14-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-09","permalink":"/en/2024/09/05-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nA small, sad thing happened recently..\\n\\nMy favorite `manjaro-zsh-config` disappeared from the AUR because the original author felt it was a Manjaro-specific package and didn\'t want it hosted on the AUR...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Manjaro-zsh-config\\n\\nActually, I dug through my own <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[Wiki](https://kiwi0093.github.io/wiki/wiki/Misc/Zsh/1-Zsh&Powerlevel10k/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [Zsh Customization Notes](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs/HomeLab/Tools/Shell/w-0094))* and found that I did document a manual installation method\u2014I just hadn\'t turned it into an install script.\\n\\nEven though it was removed, it can still be installed, just doesn\'t feel great.\\n\\nI could try keeping the PKGBUILD file and using `makepkg -si` to see if it can still be installed the same way.\\n\\nThough writing a script for it would probably be better...\\n\\n# Generative AI\\n\\nAt a colleague\'s request, I also tried the `Ollama+WebUI` setup paired with the `LLaMA 3` LLM...\\n\\nTurns out it runs pretty slowly (I hosted it on my Oracle Free Tier - ARM VPS), and getting it to generate images via commands looks kind of convoluted...\\n\\nSo I deleted it...\\n\\nAnyone interested can check out the blog posts below:\\n\\n## References\\n\\n* [Ivon\'s Blog - Offline ChatGPT Installation Methods! List of Open-Source Software to Run Large Language Models Locally](https://ivonblog.com/posts/foss-local-llm/)\\n* [Ivon\'s Blog - Deploying Open WebUI + Ollama LLM Web Interface on Linux with docker-compose](https://ivonblog.com/posts/ollama-llm-docker/)\\n\\n## Nonsense\\n\\nLately I\'ve been browsing Ivon\'s Blog a lot, and I have to say I\'m really grateful for it\u2014so much of the content covers topics I\'m interested in.\\n\\nTo the point where I feel like: if they already wrote it so well, why bother testing it and writing it all over again myself...\\n\\n# Useful Tools - Android Related\\n\\nMost of these were also things I saw on Ivon\'s Blog and decided to try out... So I won\'t write too much, just list a few that I actually find useful.\\n\\n### Obtainium\\n\\nThis app that can automatically check and update APKs installed from GitHub is seriously awesome... If they ever release a Windows version I\'d definitely install it...\\n(Wait, don\'t you already use `Winget`?)\\n\\n### Innertune & Pipepipe\\n\\nBoth of these are basically ad-free YouTube clients\u2014one for watching videos, and the other for listening to YouTube Music...\\nAlthough my inner data hoarder usually tries to save everything somewhere I control and stream it myself, for the few things I want to listen to without hoarding a giant collection, this is super handy (for example, I really like Simyee\'s Cantonese cover of *Blue and White Porcelain*, even though I don\'t collect Chinese songs...).\\n\\n### Pairdrop\\n\\nThis one is genuinely great... It\'s basically an open-source AirDrop... I also tried self-hosting it `via docker` though I didn\'t get it working properly yet, so I\'m using the official service for now and will tinker with it again later.\\n\\n# Cooking...\\n\\nOkay... actually I\'ve spent most of my time lately focusing on cooking.\\n\\nI even installed `mealie` to record my recipes... Though because I\'m too lazy to take photos... I have no idea how long my patience for documenting these recipes will last...\\n\\n# Movie & Book Thoughts\\n\\nNone... Honestly, after having kids I realized I\'m becoming less and less of a geek... (still don\'t like going out though).\\n\\nForget anime or movies... I\'ve barely even been reading books.\\n\\nGuess I\'ll just wait until the kids get a bit older and see if I have a chance to get back into it... (sigh...)\\n\\n# Summary\\n\\nWrote a whole bunch of random stuff...\\n\\nThis blog originally started to document my notes on learning how to bypass firewalls... Now it has turned into a venting space for a frustrated middle-aged guy...\\n\\nThat\'s fine too, though\u2014since I\'ve been moving most of my technical notes over to the Wiki anyway... might as well leave this place as an old man\'s space to vent..."},{"id":"2024/09/05-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2024/09/05-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2024-09-05-01.md","title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-09","description":"Random ramblings","date":"2024-09-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Docker","permalink":"/en/tags/docker"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-09","date":"2024-09-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Linux","Docker","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2024/09/05-01","description":"Random ramblings"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-10","permalink":"/en/2024/10/15-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Random Ramblings Version 2024-03","permalink":"/en/2024/03/28-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nI\'ve been quiet for a long time\u2014not only because I\'ve been going to physical therapy continuously trying to relieve the sciatica that has bothered me for ages, but mostly because life has settled into calm, with no urge to tinker with new things.\\n\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\nSuddenly realizing I\'m becoming more and more like an idle retiree with nothing to do, yesterday I decided to make work for myself and started testing some things I don\'t actually need...\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nJumping straight to the conclusion: after spending a whole day testing, there were zero worthwhile results, so I deleted everything... Still, let me briefly share my thoughts on what I tried.\\n\\n# Research (!?) Topics\\n\\nMost of the tests involved multimedia-related services.\\n\\n## Xiaoya Alist\\n\\n[![Xiaoya Alist](http://img.youtube.com/vi/L5AbBdMBo8k/0.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L5AbBdMBo8k \\"Xiaoya Alist\\")\\n\\nI admit it\u2014after watching the showcase, I was tempted and wanted to set it up...\\nBut I died on Step 1 of Step 1 \u2014 creating an Aliyun Drive account... because I don\'t have a Chinese phone number...\\nSo I can only imagine that I probably *could* have gotten it running...\\n\\n## TubeArchivist\\n\\n[TubeArchivist](https://www.tubearchivist.com/) is an offline YouTube archiver. Its basic function is subscribing to a YouTube channel and downloading all the videos from that channel for offline viewing...\\n\\nMoreover, it can integrate and serve those files to `emby`/`plex`/`jellyfin` as library media for playback.\\n\\n## TubeArchivist-Plugin for metadata\\n\\nEven though TubeArchivist downloads the actual video files, the filenames are based on YouTube IDs rather than human-readable metadata.\\nSo people developed plugins to pull YouTube metadata and convert it for `emby/jellyfin` so that what you see looks proper.\\n\\n### [TubeArchivist-emby](https://github.com/xinmans/tubearchivist-emby)\\n\\nBecause `emby` doesn\'t have it implemented as a plugin, you have to attach an external Python service to convert it. I spent an entire morning on it and still couldn\'t get it working, and I didn\'t feel like debugging Python code... so I stamped it \\"Abandoned.\\"\\n\\n### [TubeArchivist-JF-Plugin](https://github.com/tubearchivist/tubearchivist-jf-plugin)\\n\\nJust to make it work, I even went and installed `jellyfin`, and it succeeded! The gotchas are:\\n\\n* After installing the plugin, remember to restart Jellyfin, otherwise the plugin won\'t initialize.\\n\\n* After configuring and saving the plugin settings, remember to restart Jellyfin again, otherwise the connection will keep failing... and it won\'t be able to update metadata.\\n\\n## IPTV\\n\\n### Jellyfin\\n\\nSince `Emby`\'s Live TV feature requires a paid unlock... and I happened to have Jellyfin up because of TubeArchivist, I set my sights on Jellyfin.\\n\\nIn the end, after struggling through a mountain of IPTV prerequisite configs (strictly speaking, still not really sorted out since my EPG program guide was still broken),\\nit still wouldn\'t play... (the exact same stream link worked in `VLC`, and even in Emby\'s IPTV Plugin it played TV channels fine,\\nyet Jellyfin just threw an error and refused to play... so I gave up on it too... and wiped the working TA setup along with it).\\n\\n### Xetve\\n\\nThis is an IPTV Tuner and Guide aggregator tool, though I never managed to figure it out in the end...\\n\\n#### Related Links\\n\\n* [IPTV-org](https://iptv-org.github.io/)\\n* [XMLTV Source](https://xmltv.host/)\\n* [EPG.PW](https://epg.pw/xmltv.html?lang=zh-hant)\\n\\n### Emby\\n\\nEven though Live TV costs money, there is a free `IPTV` plugin you can install. Basically, you can watch channels by adding valid m3u links from iptv-org one by one... However, bandwidth can be insufficient, and the picture quality is an unbearable 480p... so giving up is probably the sensible choice...\\n\\n# Conclusion Again...\\n\\nEven though it failed... at least I spent some time tinkering... I\'ll see what else I can dig into later..."},{"id":"2024/03/28-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2024/03/28-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2024-03-28-01.md","title":"Random Ramblings Version 2024-03","description":"Just rambling","date":"2024-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.84,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Random Ramblings Version 2024-03","date":"2024-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2024/03/28-01","description":"Just rambling"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Random Ramblings - Version 2024-09","permalink":"/en/2024/09/05-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ramblings Version 2024-02","permalink":"/en/2024/02/17-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nHaving moved and settled in for a while now, life has gradually grown routine. Aside from annoying sciatica, there\'s nothing particularly special about this quiet life....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Recent Updates\\n\\nIn short, I\'ve been putting my mind into learning how to cook again... (yes... cooking).\\n\\n## Cangshu / Shuipen Lamb Soup (Knockoff Version)\\n\\nDuring the years I lived in Suzhou, whenever winter came around, I\'d often go with coworkers for a bowl of Cangshu lamb soup. For someone like me who dislikes the gamey smell of mutton, it was one of the few lamb dishes I actually liked (along with lamb skewers).\\n\\nWhen I went back to visit my wife\'s family last year, I also brought back some stewing spice packs for Shuipen lamb soup, so I made this:\\n![Knockoff Cangshu / Shuipen Lamb Soup Mix](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/lamp.jpg)\\n\\nThe reason it\'s a knockoff is that Cangshu lamb soup doesn\'t use stewing spices, and Shuipen lamb soup doesn\'t have napa cabbage and the like...\\nI literally just threw them both together.\\n\\nThen, when friends came over for a housewarming celebration, I made this again:\\n\\n![Lamb Soup + Dry Pot](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/lamp+chicken01.jpg)\\n\\nThough I later realized you really shouldn\'t put dry pot and lamb soup together in a yin-yang hotpot... otherwise the dry side burns...\\n\\n## Paella (Knockoff Version)\\n\\nAfter eating paella with my wife and kid during a trip to Dayou Terraced Park, I suddenly felt inspired to try making it myself, resulting in Prototype #1. It\'s called a knockoff version because I didn\'t use expensive saffron..\\n\\n![Paella (Fake)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/paella01.jpg)\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nCooking really is a joyful thing... It\'s making me wonder if I should start writing my own recipes.... (Actually, forget it\u2014writing recipes requires tons of photos....)\\nSince my kid seems to love eating pasta, I\'ll probably spend some time figuring out how to make good pasta next...\\n\\nAlso, I won\'t be putting the recipes for the dishes in the photos above onto the wiki..."},{"id":"2024/02/17-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2024/02/17-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2024-02-17-01.md","title":"Ramblings Version 2024-02","description":"Random ramblings","date":"2024-02-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/en/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"FreeBSD","permalink":"/en/tags/free-bsd"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Ramblings Version 2024-02","date":"2024-02-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Windows","FreeBSD","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2024/02/17-01","description":"Random ramblings"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Random Ramblings Version 2024-03","permalink":"/en/2024/03/28-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"New Server! New IT Device!","permalink":"/en/2024/01/30-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Foreword\\n\\nAs mentioned in the previous post, I recently moved and reorganized <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>all the systems and services at home</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">the Combined Fleet</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>. Besides the hardware focus from the last post, I also want to <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>review the software side</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">vent and complain</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# OS Installation\\n\\nI used to put quite a few installation scripts on my wiki, but this time I realized a lot of them aren\'t needed anymore (or don\'t need to be written like that).\\n\\n## Archlinux\\n\\nEver since `Archlinux` introduced the `archinstall` automated install script.. I haven\'t needed to write my own Archlinux install scripts anymore (of course, if you need to deploy many hosts with specialized configs you still can...). Mainly, Docker is so convenient now that I don\'t need so many VMs running individual Archlinux hosts, so the ROI of writing automated install scripts has dropped dramatically...\\n\\n### Unifi Network Application\\n\\nAfter fighting with this thing, I still couldn\'t get it running properly in Docker (just like `dnsmasq`), so in the end I had to put it on an\\n\\nArchlinux host. Installing it was pretty simple though\u2014just as mentioned in the last post, you only need to install it from `AUR`.\\n\\n~~However, I later discovered it might be an SSL issue\u2014I gave it an internal domain pointing to it, yet couldn\'t access the admin UI via that domain and could only connect with `https://ip:8443`~~ Confirmed afterward that it wasn\'t a DN issue; Vaultwarden just had the wrong saved entry so I couldn\'t log in.... (I repent!)\\n\\n## Windows\\n\\nEver since Windows 10, installing Windows hasn\'t taken much time or effort anyway. Now, possibly because it\'s leaning closer to Linux (not sure if that\'s the reason), Windows\'s CLI tools have gotten pretty nice too. So nowadays I install apps directly with `winget`\u2014not because it\'s cooler, just because apps installed via winget can easily be updated with `winget`.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Search for app XXX\\nwinget search XXX\\n\\n# Install app XXX\\nwinget install XXX\\n\\n# Uninstall app XXX\\nwinget uninstall XXX\\n\\n# Update all apps\\nwinget update --all\\n```\\n\\n## PhotonOS\\n\\nThere\'s really nothing special to configure for this. For version 5.0 that I installed this time, as long as the initial IP setup is fine, at most you just\\n\\nadd the NFS folder to mount in `/etc/fstab`, remember to install `docker-compose` (now you can just install it via `yum install docker-compose`), and run `systemctl enable docker.service` plus `systemctl enable remote-fs.target` so the machine starts Docker and mounts the NFS folder automatically on boot.\\n\\n### Docker\\n\\nAfter testing, no matter how I tried, `nfs-volume` still kept failing on me. So for now I\'m sticking to the old-school method: creating Docker volumes locally and using a script to automatically back them up to the NFS folder, which is simpler.\\n\\n# Summary\\n\\nI think I need to do a major overhaul of the stuff in my wiki, because quite a lot of things aren\'t used anymore. Going forward, scripts will probably lean toward personal-use tools, and I\'ll likely spend some time studying Ansible again."},{"id":"2024/01/30-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2024/01/30-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2024-01-30-01.md","title":"New Server! New IT Device!","description":"New toys~~~","date":"2024-01-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":5.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"New Server! New IT Device!","date":"2024-01-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2024/01/30-01","description":"New toys~~~"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ramblings Version 2024-02","permalink":"/en/2024/02/17-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Fight! New Working NB","permalink":"/en/2023/07/27-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nA little while ago I finally finished renovating and moving into my new place. Staying true to the spirit of <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>hardware lifecycle upgrades</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">chasing shiny new toys</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, I replaced almost all the networking gear and servers at the new house, so naturally it\'s time to jot down some notes on the hardware swaps and setup changes.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n\\n# Hardware Overview\\n\\n* ASUS TS110-E14\\n  \\n  * This is now my main ESXi Server, currently running the following VMs:\\n    \\n    * FreeBSD 14-release x1: Gateway + Firewall\\n    \\n    * Archlinux x1: DHCPd + DNS + UniFi Network Application\\n    \\n    * PhotonOS 5 x3: Docker hosts for Containers\\n    \\n    * XigmaNAS 13 With PCI passthrough x2: Server Backup and Temp\\n\\n* HPE MicroServer Gen8\\n  \\n  * XigmaNAS 13: Main NAS for Media Center\\n\\n* UniFi Switch Enterprise 8 PoE\\n  \\n  * Main Switch\\n\\n* Access Point U6 In-Wall\\n\\n* Access Point U6 Pro (in transit)\\n\\n# Detailed Notes\\n\\n## UniFi Series\\n\\nHonestly, the main motivation here was just showing off...\\n\\nI figured I\'d upgrade my new home\'s IT infrastructure to prosumer/semi-enterprise grade, and being able to push settings to APs from a single central controller seemed much more convenient. Plus I got burned by the **TP-Link Deco X75** I bought for my parents\' place earlier\u2014a pricey Mesh AP that didn\'t even have a whitelist mode for MAC Address filtering...\\n\\nOriginally I thought the apartment wasn\'t big so a single **U6 In-Wall** would cover everything, but unfortunately the placement wasn\'t great and the signal behind the TV wall ended up weak.\\n\\nSo I bought a **U6 Pro** to fill in the dead zone.\\n\\n## HPE MicroServer Gen8\\n\\nThis is an older box\u2014I used to have two of them, but my parents\' ASUS Server died (after ~10 years, it definitely earned its keep), so I had to move one back there to serve as their NAS. The remaining one got a **10GbE NIC + 16TB HDD x4** upgrade, turning it into a **48TB** usable **raidz1** NAS.\\n\\n## ASUS TS110-E14\\n\\nNo surprises here\u2014this is the **ESXi Server**, though setting up the foundational network environment was still quite a headache.\\n\\n### FreeBSD 14 Gateway\\n\\nSticking with old habits, I still went with the tried-and-true FreeBSD + pf + ipfw for the gateway.\\n\\n#### Network Architecture\\n\\nInitially I wanted to put everything on a single Class C subnet to keep things simple (which meant no DMZ isolation, though DMZ was basically half-baked in my hands anyway). Turns out I hit a **NAT Loopback** issue where internal hosts couldn\'t access ~~my external-facing services, so I had to revert back to the original WAN+LAN+DMZ layout~~\u2014after tweaking the pf rules, I fixed it by referring to [this guide](https://www.twbsd.org/cht/book/new/ch12.htm):\\n\\n```shell\\n# Nat Loopback\\nno nat on $int_if proto tcp from $int_if to $int_net\\nrdr on $int_if proto tcp from $int_net to $ext_ip port { 25, 165, 993 } -> 192.168.0.2\\nnat on $int_if proto tcp from $int_net to 192.168.0.2 port { 25, 165, 993 } -> $int_if\\n```\\n\\nSo in the end, I still managed to consolidate everything back onto a single Class C subnet.\\n\\n### Archlinux Dnsmasq + UniFi\\n\\nThis turned out to be part of the big landmine of this build. My original plan was to just spin up both **dnsmasq** and **UniFi Network Application** in Docker containers and call it a day, and well...\\n\\n#### Dnsmasq\\n\\nIt *ran* in Docker, sure, but because of IP subnetting and me not digging carefully into network mapping, the service appeared to be up while none of the clients could actually get DHCP or DNS working properly. After fighting with it forever, moving dnsmasq onto a standalone VM made everything work immediately without changing a single thing...\\n\\n#### UniFi Network Application\\n\\nThis one was even more infuriating. Either the **MongoDB** version specified in the docs threw issues, or once MongoDB was sorted out, the controller refused to serve the web UI properly due to Java version mismatches... After wading through walls of documentation, I discovered that on **Archlinux** with `yay` installed, all you need is:\\n\\n```bash\\nyay -S unifi\\n```\\n\\nAnd then just make sure the JRE version is 17... One single command took care of everything...\\n\\n### XigmaNAS\\n\\nGiven the storage needs of the Docker backend and system backups, I mounted a ZFS Mirror NAS via NFS to the various servers for basic backups. Later on, I\'ll pair it with a self-hosted Git server for overall internal project management.\\n\\nAlso, on my parents\' old server I used to attach an HDD directly in ESXi as a TEMP drive for BT downloads and overflow storage, and during this migration I suffered horribly because certain VMs refused to boot without that HDD attached.\\n\\nSo this time I mounted the Temp HDD inside XigmaNAS and exported it over NFS instead.\\n\\n# Pain Points Along the Way\\n\\n## XigmaNAS Refusing to Mount the Temp Drive\\n\\nFor whatever reason, my existing XigmaNAS VM just wouldn\'t add the Temp drive, so in the end I had to spin up an extra VM with dedicated PCI Passthrough hardware running a separate XigmaNAS instance solely for exporting Temp storage.\\n\\n## UniFi Series\\n\\nAs mentioned earlier, the cursed UniFi-Network-Application wouldn\'t behave properly in Docker... plus Dnsmasq didn\'t play nice either, so I ended up spinning up an extra VM.\\n\\n## Poor WiFi AP Placement\\n\\nMy brain must have been eaten by something during the interior design phase, because the U6 In-Wall ended up in a terrible spot\u2014facing away from two bedrooms and two bathrooms, leaving weak signal in all of them... So I had to add a U6 Pro, kicking off the UniFi asexual reproduction cycle ahead of schedule...\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nLiving in a new home is wonderful, though the moving process itself is not... Getting new servers and redesigning the architecture is fun(?), whereas emergency-rescuing dying hardware and migrating mountains of data is definitely not...\\n\\nI\'ll update the wiki with the detailed config changes later on."},{"id":"2023/07/27-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2023/07/27-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2023-07-27-01.md","title":"Fight! New Working NB","description":"New toy, and the company paid for it","date":"2023-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.16,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Fight! New Working NB","date":"2023-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2023/07/27-01","description":"New toy, and the company paid for it"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"New Server! New IT Device!","permalink":"/en/2024/01/30-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Change to Archlinux","permalink":"/en/2023/07/18-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nDue to <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>unknown reasons</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">an 80% chance my kids did it</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, the screen on my company-issued laptop completely died,\\nleaving me living with an external monitor only for quite a while...\\n\\nWith the help of our <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>partner-in-crime</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">kind-hearted</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> IT guy, I got another laptop replacement..\\n\\nNaturally, I had to do some <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>Fine Tune</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">illegal modification</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>..\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Machine Introduction\\n\\nI guess I got lucky this time\u2014not only is this batch not <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>Lenovo</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">the evil CCP brand</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, it was originally meant for an ME, so they gave me a `Dell Precision 3561`.\\n\\nIt turned out to be a blessing in disguise with an overall spec bump:\\nCPU from 11th gen i5 to 11th gen i7 (okay, I know both are CPUs from several generations ago)\\nRAM from 16GB to 32GB (this actually makes a real difference... since I like running all sorts of random stuff)\\nGPU from Intel Iris@Xe to Nvidia T600 Laptop (alright, a bit faster)\\n\\n# Machine Setup\\n\\nBasically this is still a company laptop, so I can\'t modify it too drastically (though the kind IT guy did give me Admin privileges so I could do some tuning first).\\n\\n## Basic\\n\\nSince it\'s a company machine, it naturally runs `Windows 11 pro`.\\nWhen I got it, the company\'s work software was already pre-installed (what? You thought my job only needed a browser? How could that be, I still need Office too!), so I just used `Winget` to install the following:\\n\\n* Open-shell - Okay, this isn\'t the most important thing, I just hate the post-`Win10` Start menu so I installed it.\\n\\n* VirtualBox - This is actually one of the core pieces; with vbox I can pretty much do my own thing.\\n\\n* WSL - This is also quite important, though I ran into some issues this time.\\n\\n## VitrualBox\\n\\nThis is really simple\u2014just installing Arch Linux inside a VM...\\n\\n## WSL\\n\\n### Dilemma\\n\\nThis Windows installation is super weird\u2014I actually couldn\'t install the new version of WSL from anywhere... it kept throwing `error code  0x80070490`.\\nRunning `wsl --update` just told me element not found....\\nThe price of that is... WSL Distros can\'t properly use `systemd` (which is absurd, since one of Windows 11\'s selling points is built-in `systemd` support in the new WSL).\\n\\n### Workaround and Result\\n\\nI basically switched to [ArchWSL2 from Sileshn](https://github.com/sileshn/ArchWSL2) because that version is actively maintained...\\nAs for the systemd issue, the only reason I actually needed systemd inside WSL was for `systemd-resolved.service` for `Wireguard`, so switching `resolvconf` to `oopenresolv` solved it.\\n\\n### Little Discovery\\n\\nI really love the default customized zsh theme in `Manjaro KDE`. Previously I always copied the customization files directly from `/usr/share/zsh/`, until I recently discovered that `AUR` actually has `manjaro-zsh-config`. After installing it, you just need to make sure `~/.zshrc` has the right contents. The only minor downside is that its console color is `Manjaro green` instead of `Arch blue`.\\n\\n# Conclusion and Future Plans\\n\\nConclusion? Honestly, that\'s about it\u2014once everything is set up, just be good and go to work....\\nThe next step is figuring out how to squeeze a bit more `GPU` performance out for WSL or the VM..."},{"id":"2023/07/18-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2023/07/18-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2023-07-18-01.md","title":"Change to Archlinux","description":"Converting to Archlinux","date":"2023-07-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Change to Archlinux","date":"2023-07-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2023/07/18-01","description":"Converting to Archlinux"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Fight! New Working NB","permalink":"/en/2023/07/27-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Keeping Up with the Times","permalink":"/en/2023/07/14-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAmazed by `Archinstall`, I\'m considering switching from `Manjaro` to `Archlinux`. Let\'s start with my `WSL` and a VM in `VirtualBox`...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# ArchWSL\\n\\n## Github Project\\n\\n* Link - [GitHub - yuk7/ArchWSL](https://github.com/yuk7/ArchWSL)\\n\\n* Online Manual - [How to Setup | ArchWSL official documentation](https://wsldl-pg.github.io/ArchW-docs/How-to-Setup/)\\n\\n## Notes\\n\\n* You need to initialize `archlinux-keyring` first to avoid issues:\\n  \\n  ```bash\\n  [root@wsl]$ pacman-key --init\\n  [root@wsl]$ pacman-key --populate\\n  [root@wsl]$ pacman -Sy archlinux-keyring\\n  [root@wsl]$ pacman -Syyu\\n  ```\\n\\n* You need to create a regular user account (otherwise some GUI apps will have issues):\\n  \\n  ```bash\\n  # \u5B9A\u7FA9wheel\u7FA4\u7D44\u7684User\u90FD\u53EF\u4EE5sudo\\n  [root@wsl]$ echo \\"%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL\\" > /etc/sudoers.d/wheel\\n  # \u5EFA\u7ACB\u4E00\u500B{username}\u5E33\u865F,\u4E26\u4E14\u5C6C\u65BCwheel\u7FA4\u7D44\\n  [root@wsl]$ useradd -m -G wheel -s /bin/bash `{username}`\\n  ```\\n\\n* You need to set `{username}` as the default user\u2014this operation must be done outside WSL:\\n  \\n  ```bash\\n  [User@Windows]$ Arch.exe config --default-user `{username}`\\n  ```\\n\\n# Archlinux Guest in Virtualbox\\n\\n## Install\\n\\nBasically you just boot from the ISO, pick your options in `archinstall`, and install directly. From testing it myself, if you need a desktop you can just choose its `desktop profile`. Looking at my own choice of `KDE`:\\n\\n* It installs both `Xorg` & `Wayland` by default, and you can choose between them at the `SDDM` login screen.\\n  \\n  * Currently `Wayland` support still isn\'t great\u2014even `ArchWiki` recommends sticking with `Xorg` for now.\\n\\n## Essential Packages\\n\\nRecommended essential packages are as follows:\\n\\n| Pkg Name               | Description                                                     |\\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| git                    | Aside from regular Git usage, installing `AUR` packages before `yay` is set up (including installing `yay` itself) relies on it. |\\n| base-devel             | This is actually a package group containing compilers and related tools, needed if you compile packages yourself. |\\n| virtualbox-guest-utils | VirtualBox guest tools including special drivers, equivalent to VMware Tools. |\\n| yay                    | Anyone using an Arch-based distro knows what this is. Manjaro lets you install it directly with pacman, while on Arch you have to install it using the method below. |\\n\\n### How to Install yay (under Archlinux)\\n\\n```bash\\n# \u4E0D\u80FD\u7528root\\n[user@archlinux]$ git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/yay-git.git\\n[user@archlinux]$ cd yay-git\\n[user@archlinux]$ makepkg -si\\n```\\n\\n## Notes\\n\\n* systemd services need to be enabled manually:\\n  \\n  * systemd-resolved.service\\n    \\n    * This is the systemd version of resolvconf, mainly needed by `wireguard`.\\n    \\n    * `systemctl enable --now systemd-resolved.service`\\n  \\n  * vboxservice.service\\n    \\n    * This automatically loads components from virtualbox-guest-utils.\\n    \\n    * `systemctl enable --now vboxservice.service`\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nFrom what I\'ve seen so far, as long as you copy over some customization configs from `Manjaro` (or another distro you like, such as zsh-theme-powerlevel10k settings), `Archlinux` won\'t look plain at all.\\n\\nInstallation has also become super easy. In daily use it\'s Arch-based, pretty much no different (compared to Manjaro).\\n\\nI\'ll probably need to observe it for a while longer before making a clearer comparison of pros and cons."},{"id":"2023/07/14-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2023/07/14-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2023-07-14-01.md","title":"Keeping Up with the Times","description":"Catching up on things","date":"2023-07-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":5.04,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Keeping Up with the Times","date":"2023-07-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2023/07/14-01","description":"Catching up on things"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Change to Archlinux","permalink":"/en/2023/07/18-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"The Steam Deck Is Awesome!!","permalink":"/en/2023/01/11-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nIt\'s been half a year since I last updated this blog, mostly because I\'ve been <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>busy with work and various life chores</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">being lazy</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>. However, some major news happened recently, and after <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>a sudden epiphany</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">getting tired of reading web novels</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, I tinkered with a few new toys that have been updated during this time.\\n\\nSince these are all scattered thoughts, I\'m just bundling them all together into this post.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Red Hat Stops Publishing Source Code for Downstream Rebuilds\\n\\n## Personal Take\\n\\nAs a commercial distribution (and one that charges money), using licensing and withholding direct access to `source code` to protect its commercial value is completely normal. `SUSE`\'s commercial edition (SLES) doesn\'t hand out `Source code` for direct downstream rebuilds either.\\n\\nMoreover, both of them have their own upstream `open source` editions:\\n\\n| | Red Hat | SUSE |\\n| -------------- | ------------------------ | --------------------- |\\n| Open Source Upstream | Fedora / CentOS stream | OpenSuSE |\\n| Commercial Edition | Red Hat Enterprise Linux | SUSE Linux Enterprise |\\n\\nThe ones getting killed off right now are `RHEL` downstream clones. Thinking about it, it makes sense\u2014the company developing it needs to make money, and developers need to put food on the table.\\n\\nAs for all the \\"they broke promises they made before the company took off\\" drama, well\u2014people get divorced even after getting married, and vows sworn before the gods get ignored all the time...\\n\\nAs for SUSE and Oracle jumping out right now to ride the wave, I think they\'re mostly seizing the opportunity to kick Red Hat while the sentiment is hot, seeing if public opinion can sway some frustrated Red Hat users into switching to their own paid distros. Let\'s grab some popcorn and watch how this plays out.\\n\\n# NixOS\\n\\nRecently, NixOS suddenly blew up among Linux YouTubers for some reason, so I hopped on the bandwagon and gave it a try too.\\n\\n## Hands-on Impressions\\n\\nHonestly, the moment I touched it, the shock was pretty similar to the first time I installed `Gentoo`, and my takeaway was pretty much the same\u2014basically:\\n\\n> Whoa~ this thing\'s special features are so cool~ I can do this, and I can do that too~~\\n\\nThen, after cooling down and thinking about it: sure, you *can* use it, though it\'s quite a hassle, and you don\'t really *have* to use it either.\\n\\nThere\'s no compelling reason that makes it strictly indispensable...\\n\\n### Strengths\\n\\n`NixOS` is basically a dedicated OS owned by a package manager... (a bit like Prussia in a certain era\u2014other countries have an army, while Prussia was an army that had a country).\\n\\nThe core of `NixOS` is the `Nix` package manager, so it can configure the entire system using a single (or multiple importable) `.nix` configuration file (well, in theory\u2014user configs in `~/.config` still behave like usual). And every time you update, add, or remove a package, it rebuilds system symlinks so each `version` is isolated, and you can even spin up separate profile dev environments without touching the host system.\\n\\nSounds super cool and awesome, right?\\n\\n### Drawbacks\\n\\nSomething that sounds this flashy is bound to have corresponding drawbacks that haven\'t been solved (equivalent exchange, after all). After playing around with it briefly, I noticed:\\n\\n* To make permanent system changes, you *have* to edit `/etc/nixos/configure.nix` and run `nixos-rebuild switch` to update the whole system generation.\\n\\n* The system itself has major release versions and `channel`s. And to fully achieve the \\"one config file to rule them all\\" ideal, they introduced something called `flake`... making it even more convoluted. Honestly, going rolling-release from the start would have been simpler...\\n\\n* Huge disk space requirements. While you can use `nix-garbage-collection` to delete generations outside a certain range, doing so kinda defeats the whole point of the rollback features its fans brag about\u2014if you wipe all your snapshots, what are you supposed to roll back to??\\n\\n### Mini-Conclusion\\n\\nIt\'s pretty great for tinkering, or if you have a huge fleet of identical machines to deploy and manage\u2014assuming you don\'t constantly tweak the system, or you have a dedicated test machine to dial in the config before applying it everywhere.\\n\\nAs a daily-driver desktop OS, it\'s probably not ideal...\\n\\nUnless you really need multiple dev environments coexisting on one system without interfering with each other *and* you also deeply despise container tech like Docker...\\n\\n# ArchInstall\\n\\nEarlier, I saw some YouTubers talking about a new tool in `Archlinux` that makes installation friendlier for newcomers.\\n\\nI ignored it for a long time until I finally tested it myself recently\u2014and wow, it blew me away.\\n\\nI\'ve written my own install scripts before, and I\'m sure lots of Arch users have written their own too.\\n\\nThis thing is basically that, except far more complete.\\n\\nRight now it looks like you can just pick a few options and end up with a fully working desktop.\\n\\nThe only pity is I haven\'t figured out how to customize its default profile yet...\\n\\nLooks like there\'s finally enough reason to move off Manjaro... (though Manjaro still has some handy little utilities that Arch doesn\'t include...).\\n\\n# NeoVim+Nvchad\\n\\nI actually ended up messing with this again because I couldn\'t resist tinkering while testing `NixOS` (otherwise I hadn\'t touched it in ages).\\n\\nJust a few key takeaways:\\n\\n* On Arch-based Linux, please do **not** install `NvChad` from the `AUR`\u2014it causes errors. Just do a clean `git clone` like a good citizen.\\n\\n* The default keybindings in the newer version are actually pretty nice; you barely need to remap anything manually, and `<leader>+ch` lets you view the hotkey `cheat sheet`.\\n\\n* I still haven\'t sorted out the LSP and formatting setup yet\u2014I\'ll find time to look into it later..."},{"id":"2023/01/11-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2023/01/11-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2023-01-11-01.md","title":"The Steam Deck Is Awesome!!","description":"GabeN, you really outdid yourself","date":"2023-01-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Game","permalink":"/en/tags/game"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":5.19,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The Steam Deck Is Awesome!!","date":"2023-01-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Game","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2023/01/11-01","description":"GabeN, you really outdid yourself"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Keeping Up with the Times","permalink":"/en/2023/07/14-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Unfortunate Incident....","permalink":"/en/2023/01/11-02"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nI picked up a Steam Deck\u2014my first real toy in a long time since having kids...\\n\\nAs for why it took me so long to write about it, all I can say is that it\'s just that awesome....\\n\\nIt let me experience a thrill I hadn\'t enjoyed in a long time \u2014 the satisfaction of actually beating a game.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Brief Overview\\n\\nTaking advantage of KOMODO\'s Asian distribution, I also bought a 512GB Steam Deck (even though I already have a 2230 1TB SSD on hand, I wasn\'t planning on modding it myself....).\\n\\nI\'ll skip the unboxing stuff since there are already tons of unboxing articles and videos online, and just share my own thoughts...\\n\\n## Primary Use\\n\\nBefore getting it, I honestly didn\'t expect to play games on it that often.... I figured I\'d mess around with the hardware a bit and then it would end up collecting dust just like my PSP... Turns out I was wrong....\\n\\nCoinciding with the Steam Winter Sale, I picked up a few games at a discount that were basically collection-tier picks for me:\\n\\n`Octopath Traveler` and `Triangle Strategy`.. Starting with these two games, I realized my late nights were completely inseparable from the Steam Deck....\\n\\n(And as a result, I later bought `Tactic Orge Reborn` while it was still pricey.)\\n\\n## Quick Review\\n\\nValve actually put a lot of care into SteamOS, with many nifty built-in utilities. The few minor drawbacks are basically:\\n\\n* The OS is read-only; installing new applications relies on `flatpak`.\\n\\n* Valve\'s repository stability isn\'t great (though I haven\'t run into issues myself\u2014probably an early-days thing).\\n\\n* The jump from 3.0 --\x3e 3.3.4 had huge changes. Personally, I think that\'s partly an Arch-based habit where updates bring big shifts; on the other hand, it shows Valve is actively improving their product.\\n\\n* Insufficient battery life.\\n\\n* The screen is only 1280x800, shy of 1080p (though on a screen this small, the difference isn\'t very noticeable).\\n\\n* Too big and heavy (personally, it\'s fine in my own use).\\n\\nThere are quite a few pros\u2014or rather, standout traits\u2014as well:\\n\\n* Complete UI integration. Sure, a handheld *should* get this right, but looking at other Windows-based Chinese handhelds makes it feel miles ahead.\\n\\n* Ability to lock FPS to reduce power draw; whether you cap it at 40fps makes a huge difference to battery life.\\n\\n* Thoughtful tweaks like Night Mode.\\n\\n* Desktop Mode is available. Honestly, Desktop Mode feels a bit awkward because casual players just gaming will barely ever need it, yet when you need to set up extra things, it becomes essential (though it\'s definitely best used with an external keyboard and mouse).\\n\\n## Handy Extra Tools\\n\\n* [Steam Tinker Launch](https://github.com/sonic2kk/steamtinkerlaunch)\\n  \\n  You basically just install it through `ProtonUP-Qt`, and it\'s a god-tier utility. You configure it in Steam\'s launch options much like `Proton`. Once set up, it launches this menu before the game starts, allowing you to link a `custom program`, or check `tool -> cheat engine` to automatically launch `cheat engine` after the game starts with the process already attached\u2014you just load the CT file directly.\\n  \\n  The only slight flaw is that when launching Cheat Engine, the touch input area doesn\'t quite align with the actual screen display.\\n\\n* [ProtonUp-Qt](https://davidotek.github.io/protonup-qt/)\\n  \\n  An installer utility for Steam Proton and other plugins (like STL). You can install the flatpak version via Discover (or just download the AppImage). If you like, you can also install `Proton-GE` through it.\\n\\n* [Proton-GE](https://github.com/GloriousEggroll/proton-ge-custom)\\n  \\n  A custom build of Proton with plenty of extra patches bundled in.\\n  \\n  [Proton GE: How Does It Differ from Proton? (Gaming) | Linux Addicts](https://www.linuxadictos.com/zh-TW/proton-ge-en-que-se-diferencia-con-proton.html)\\n\\n* [Emudeck](https://www.emudeck.com/)\\n  \\n  Thanks to the Steam Deck\'s `excellent performance`, it\'s great for running emulators. Emudeck is an all-in-one installer (yes, its job is strictly unified installation; the emulators themselves are still the individual standalone emulators). Out of all these tools, this is the only one I haven\'t installed yet (because my SD card hasn\'t arrived, so I don\'t have the storage space).\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nIf your Steam library is full of mouse-and-keyboard-heavy PC games like `AOE` or `HOI`, then the Steam Deck isn\'t really for you. If you only play ultra-demanding AAA PC games, the Steam Deck isn\'t for you either. The Steam Deck is made for controller-friendly games with lighter loading demands, and it\'s ideal for people who only have fragmented pockets of time to game. As for whether you\'re familiar with or good at Linux, that has no serious bearing on using the device itself\u2014at most, the better you know Linux, the more likely you are to tinker with or break SteamOS settings.\\n\\nWould I recommend the Steam Deck to others??\\n\\nHonestly, for the same price (or just a tiny bit more), you can get a low-end gaming laptop... and even the lowest-end gaming laptop outperforms the Steam Deck by a mile...\\n\\nYou might say, \\"But it has the dedicated SteamOS, which is awesome!\\" Well, if you install Manjaro without Game Mode, you can get something 87% similar to SteamOS\u2014not to mention someone released HoloISO that can be installed on regular PC hardware (though I failed to install it in VirtualBox). So there\'s really no need to buy a Steam Deck just for its OS.\\n\\nAfter narrowing it down like that, the remaining target audience seems like a bunch of people with more money than sense.... (though I\'m broke, so I shouldn\'t belong in that category).\\n\\nTo me, this product is like Microsoft making the low-value Surface lineup just to teach laptop manufacturers how to design and build hardware suited for Windows 8/10 (honestly, running Windows on a Surface really was pretty pleasant\u2014too bad I barely use Windows anymore). Valve has made real software and hardware contributions to Linux Gaming, and hopefully plenty of manufacturers will step up and compete to build even better products down the line."},{"id":"2023/01/11-02","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2023/01/11-02","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2023-01-11-02.md","title":"Unfortunate Incident....","description":"Sob...","date":"2023-01-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.26,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Unfortunate Incident....","date":"2023-01-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Server","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2023/01/11-02","description":"Sob..."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The Steam Deck Is Awesome!!","permalink":"/en/2023/01/11-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Z-Library","permalink":"/en/2022/11/14-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nThis is actually a pointless rant post... though the incident itself isn\'t the worst misfortune ever.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Story Outline\\n\\n## How It Started\\n\\nSo here\'s what happened: one day while I was slacking off at work, I suddenly noticed the storage pool on one of my home NAS boxes had vanished. Looking closer, the PCI-passthrough SATA expansion card had suddenly died...\\n\\n## Emergency Response\\n\\nAlthough rebooting ESXi would temporarily bring the expansion card back to life before it died again after a while, I acted decisively and immediately ordered a new SATA expansion card (and threw in some other stuff on PChome that cost more than the card itself). Currently still waiting for delivery.\\n\\n## Emergency Response II - The Idle Gen8\\n\\nSince I also have an idle Gen8 sitting at home, last night I tried harvesting parts from the Gen8 to see if I could fix it. Long story short, while it\'s also a 4-port card and the card itself works, its 1-to-4 breakout cable has issues (probably), so out of four drives it could only detect three.... still a fail in the end..\\n\\n## Emergency Response III - The Active Gen8\\n\\nThen I turned my attention to the active Gen8, thinking the idle card still had at least two working ports so swapping them might work.... Then I opened up the machine and realized how naive and silly I was...\\n\\nThe SATA expansion card in that machine... is a 2-port card.... damn it!! (I really cut corners back then.)\\n\\n## Current Status\\n\\nWhat else can I do? Right now I can only shut down all services bound to the dead NAS... unplug power to the HDDs, and wait until the new card arrives at home.\\n\\n## The Infuriating Part\\n\\nMy main NAS went down, and even after frantic troubleshooting I still had to shut it down.. Yet most of my family\'s reaction was:\\n\\n### Doesn\'t matter... we don\'t really use it anyway, only you do....\\n\\nYeah, right, only I use it! I won\'t bother factoring in whether you guys use it or not when planning the next generation~~~\\n\\n# Future Plans...\\n\\nLater I chatted with my mentor, which shattered quite a few of my existing assumptions:\\n\\n* If you haven\'t bought vMotion, or don\'t want to switch to Proxmox VE, it\'s still better to consolidate VMs onto a single machine..\\n  \\n  Thinking about it carefully, as long as you beef up the hardware on one VM host, there\'s really no need to spread ESXi across the other two Gen8s.. Running ESXi with only 16GB RAM is pretty useless anyway...\\n\\n* Just use your Gen8 as a pure NAS..\\n  \\n  That\'s actually true, because a Gen8 only has one PCIe slot, and eventually it\'ll need a 10Gb NIC.... I originally wanted to do iSCSI and whatnot, but switching back to a pure NAS means the OS basically just needs a USB stick, leaving all the hardware resources for the NAS.\\n\\n* Ditch Raidz1 and just use ZFS mirror (formerly RAID 1) instead\\n  \\n  Right now HDD prices per GB for anything under 8TB are basically dirt cheap/not worth it, so the recommendation is to go straight for 16TB HDDs x2. Currently two 16TB drives cost around 19k NTD, so in a 4-bay machine you can gradually build two 16TB pools.\\n\\nGiven my current situation and budget, this seems like the better option\u2014after all, just rebuilding the 10Gb backbone, Wi-Fi, and buying a base server is already super expensive (almost 100k NTD). Trying to go all-in all at once right now would be stretching way too thin.\\n\\nPlus, if I cheap out and fill all four bays with 4TB drives right now.. there\'ll be no room for future expansion anyway.. going straight for larger HDDs is definitely better.."},{"id":"2022/11/14-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/11/14-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-11-14-01.md","title":"Z-Library","description":"A treasure... a seized treasure","date":"2022-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Books","permalink":"/en/tags/books"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Z-Library","date":"2022-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Books","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/11/14-01","description":"A treasure... a seized treasure"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Unfortunate Incident....","permalink":"/en/2023/01/11-02"},"nextItem":{"title":"Neovim","permalink":"/en/2022/11/09-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nActually, I hadn\'t even noticed this treasure trove.. until it got seized by the FBI.....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Z-Library\\n\\n### Overview\\n\\nBased on the description on [Z-Library - Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia](https://zh.m.wikipedia.org/zh-tw/Z-Library), it actually sounds pretty positive.. but in reality it\'s a site all the major publishers couldn\'t wait to get rid of...\\n\\nBasically it\'s a gigantic free e-book library.... and because of that, it got raided by the FBI....\\n\\n### Current Status\\n\\nBasically the web domain names were seized.. but something like this obviously won\'t truly disappear, so it went underground... Based on this login page that didn\'t get taken down:\\n\\n[An account registration page I\'m not sure needs to be kept low-key](https://zh.singlelogin.me/)\\n\\nAfter registering an account, it gives you three options:\\n\\n#### Tor\\n\\nThis \\"onion network\\" is to some extent the \\"dark web\\" that was hyped up like crazy a few years ago.... Though in practice it isn\'t actually that scary... It\'s mainly just because connecting to it requires a dedicated browser that it feels extra sneaky....\\n\\nHowever, the desktop version of `Brave` that I use regularly actually has built-in Tor browsing... you just need to open a Private Window with Tor.... (Though based on my test today, it\'s pretty slow to respond... it takes a while to connect, probably because there are still far fewer nodes compared to the regular internet.)\\n\\n#### I2P\\n\\nHonestly this thing is pretty similar to Tor, just with even more emphasis on anonymity.... However... it really does require downloading a dedicated browser to use.... Personally, I\'m not really a fan of tools that require installing extra software like that....\\n\\n#### Telegram Bot\\n\\nHonestly this really caught me by surprise... it\'s awesome....\\n\\nJust add its bot (`@firstlibrarybot`) in your Telegram and link your account (instructions are right on its registration page, just follow along).\\n\\nThen right inside that bot chat you can search for the books you want... and any file under 50MB can be downloaded directly in Telegram.. seriously generous.\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nHonestly, this thing is basically skirting the edge of legality.. no idea how much longer it can survive underground like this... so if you want any books, it\'s probably smarter to grab them while you can....."},{"id":"2022/11/09-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/11/09-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-11-09-01.md","title":"Neovim","description":"Favorite editor","date":"2022-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Neovim","date":"2022-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/11/09-01","description":"Favorite editor"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Z-Library","permalink":"/en/2022/11/14-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"DistroBox","permalink":"/en/2022/11/03-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nRecently I spent some time revisiting and digging into some fundamental tools.. I didn\'t expect `vim` to have such an awesome successor..\\n\\nThis `NeoVim` fork supports all kinds of plugins and features, and paired with the pre-built `NvChad`, it\'s basically insanely good.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# NeoVim + NvChad\\n\\n## Neovim\\n\\n[Official Neovim Website](https://neovim.io/)\\n\\nThis thing is actually pretty much the same as Vim, except it supports `lua` configuration files and a huge variety of plugins.\\n\\nStill, just for all the new tricks it supports, it\'s well worth replacing the `vim` I\'ve been using for ages with it.\\n\\n## NvChad\\n\\n[NvChad \xb7 Neovim with lua is cool](https://nvchad.com/)\\n\\nHonestly, this thing is ridiculously awesome....\\n\\nBy writing basic configurations in Lua for Vim plugins supported by Neovim, it transforms it into something that feels almost like a completely different editor (yep, you can basically make it look just like VS Code).\\n\\nWith your own customization, you can even use it to replace a markdown editor... (I even saw a corresponding plugin for picgo... it even has image hosting upload support... why would you even need any other editor...)\\n\\n## Customization\\n\\nTo be honest, customizing this requires knowing a few things:\\n\\n* Lua syntax\\n\\n* How to install and configure Vim plugins\\n\\nBasically, under the framework already set up by NvChad, you use its override mechanism to swap out certain definitions... (Of course, you can also fork its git repo and rewrite your own version from scratch...)\\n\\nRight now, the only thing I\'ve customized myself is the key mapping part (since the default ones were a bit sparse). Later on, I\'ll put it into my Ansible setup to apply it across all my clients."},{"id":"2022/11/03-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/11/03-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-11-03-01.md","title":"DistroBox","description":"This thing is sick","date":"2022-11-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.97,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"DistroBox","date":"2022-11-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/11/03-01","description":"This thing is sick"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Neovim","permalink":"/en/2022/11/09-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Halloween - The Amazing KDE","permalink":"/en/2022/10/31-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nI also learned about this awesome tool from YouTube. Here\'s the link to the original video:\\n\\n[![Debian Stable: Fresh Kernel and the AUR! - YouTube](http://img.youtube.com/vi/p9AdhNgR69k/0.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p9AdhNgR69k \\"Running Arch Linux & AUR inside Debian with Distrobox\\")\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Basic Concept\\n\\n## DistroBox\\n\\nProject URL - [GitHub - 89luca89/distrobox](https://github.com/89luca89/distrobox)\\n\\nIntro page - [Distrobox: Try Multiple Linux Distributions via the Terminal](https://itsfoss.com/distrobox/)\\n\\nPut simply, it uses Docker or Podman to spin up another Linux distribution and export apps from inside it to run on the host outside.\\n\\n# Hands-on Experience\\n\\nI personally tested it on `Fedora 36 KDE Plasma Edition` (unfortunately I deleted the entire VM without taking screenshots).\\n\\nIt does work, though there are a few quirks:\\n\\n## DE & WM\\n\\nBasically, whatever you\'re running on the host outside is what gets used inside. So... when I was on `Wayland` outside, `ulauncher` inside acted super weird...\\n\\n## Kernel\\n\\nSince it\'s built on `docker`/`podman`... how could the kernel possibly be different??\\n\\n## Related Commands\\n\\nAll that said, it\'s very likely I just haven\'t figured out the best way to use it yet. Even just having exported programs pick up external config files is already great\u2014in fact, I\'m not even sure why that machine had my Brave profile without Brave installed, yet when Brave launched from inside the container it actually loaded the host\'s config... awesome! (Though `ulauncher` died beyond repair.)\\n\\nHere are the basic commands:\\n\\n| Out/In distrobox | command | usage | Example | Manual Link |\\n| ---------------- |:-----------------------------------------------------:| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| outside | distrobox create -n name -i Docker image | Create a distrobox container | distrobox create -n Archlinux -i Archlinux | [Distrobox-create](https://github.com/89luca89/distrobox/blob/main/docs/usage/distrobox-create.md) |\\n| outside | distrobox enter `<name>` | into named container | distrobox enter Archlinux | [Distrobox-enter](https://github.com/89luca89/distrobox/blob/main/docs/usage/distrobox-enter.md) |\\n| outside | distrobox list | list all containers | distrobox list | [Distrobox-llist](https://github.com/89luca89/distrobox/blob/main/docs/usage/distrobox-list.md) |\\n| outside | distrobox rm `<name>` | delete container | distrobox rm Archlinux | [Distrobox-rm](https://github.com/89luca89/distrobox/blob/main/docs/usage/distrobox-rm.md) |\\n| Outside | distrobox upgrade `<name>` | upgrade container | distrobox upgrade Archlinux | [Distrobox-upgrade](https://github.com/89luca89/distrobox/blob/main/docs/usage/distrobox-upgrade.md) |\\n| Inside | distrobox-export -a `<app Name>`  -ep `<path to outside>` | export apps installed inside container to outside to use | distrobox-export -a Brave -ep ~/.local/bin | [Distrobox-export](https://github.com/89luca89/distrobox/blob/main/docs/usage/distrobox-export.md) |\\n\\n## Extra Notes\\n\\nIn all my examples, I used `distrobox` to spin up an `Archlinux` container and abuse the `AUR`. The main reason is that there\'s basically no need to run `Fedora` or `Debian/Ubuntu` on top of an Arch-based distro (apart from updates occasionally breaking things, Arch\'s software availability pretty much sweeps everything else...).\\n\\nThere is one awkward catch though\u2014rather than saying I\'m already shaped like `Archlinux`, it\'s more accurate to say I\'m shaped like `Manjaro`... Unfortunately `manjaro` fails to deliver\u2014its official Docker image is terrible... nowhere near as good as Archlinux\'s...\\n\\n# Possible Ideal Setup Going Forward\\n\\nI\'ve been trying `Fedora` lately and it really does look like an awesome base OS\u2014paired with the ability to install from the AUR, it would be practically unbeatable (if things were actually as simple as a fool like me imagines, anyway). Still, looking at its desktop style and customization options... I\'ll probably stay in the shape of `Manjaro` for quite a long time yet...."},{"id":"2022/10/31-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/10/31-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-10-31-01.md","title":"Halloween - The Amazing KDE","description":"Jack, this is truly amazing","date":"2022-10-31T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.11,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Halloween - The Amazing KDE","date":"2022-10-31T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/10/31-01","description":"Jack, this is truly amazing"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DistroBox","permalink":"/en/2022/11/03-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Haven\'t Updated in a While","permalink":"/en/2022/10/12-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nEven though foreign reviewers had tested this before, there\'s still a gap until you experience it yourself.   \\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# KDE\\n\\n## Stereotypes About KDE\\n\\nBack around the KDE 3.x era, I used it once because it looked pretty, and from then on I had a long-standing stereotype:\\n\\n> `KDE` is a very resource-heavy desktop...\\n\\nBecause back then I remember comparing it to `Windows Vista/7`... Indeed, KDE with all visual effects turned on was a very heavy DE.\\n\\n## Manjaro XFCE\\n\\nThe reason I\'m bringing up Manjaro XFCE is that when I started seriously using a Linux Desktop, I started with Manjaro XFCE.. At first I didn\'t dare think about using the KDE Edition because I was afraid my VM couldn\'t handle it. Only later, when I had a physical machine capable of running it and wanted a prettier look... did I give the KDE edition a try.\\n\\n## The Astonishing KDE\\n\\nHonestly, I was mentally prepared for the machine to run slower, only to find there didn\'t seem to be any difference... Though in terms of flashiness it doesn\'t look quite as wildly imaginative as GNOME (yes, KDE is a sensible, down-to-earth homemaker... ).\\n\\n## Performance Differences\\n\\nThis goes back to my VNC machine. I switched through a few DEs (initially because I thought XFCE wasn\'t pretty enough) and found that every single one I switched to felt heavy...\\n\\nUntil I switched back to the KDE edition. After stripping out some things a VNC host doesn\'t need, I found it actually runs super smoothly (idle RAM about 600~800MB, around XFCE usage)... On top of that, `xrdp`-related configs mysteriously refused to run properly on XFCE (probably just because I hadn\'t written the config file right), plus Manjaro doesn\'t make installing `lxqt` very convenient... (Now that Manjaro Architect isn\'t supported by default anymore, it\'s pretty hard to install... non-prepackaged desktop environments.)\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nI love KDE... even though GNOME looks prettier and dresses up nicer.\\n\\nI love KDE... even though backing up desktop settings means having to `cp` a pile of files that look messy, with no `dconf` available.\\n\\nGNOME is a flashy, seductive siren.. Sure, it\'s glamorous and catches the eye, but KDE is the one you can actually settle down and live a good life with.\\n\\n(The above remarks represent personal impressions only, with no intention to mislead or disparage GNOME enthusiasts...)"},{"id":"2022/10/12-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/10/12-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-10-12-01.md","title":"Haven\'t Updated in a While","description":"Just rambling","date":"2022-10-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":4.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Haven\'t Updated in a While","date":"2022-10-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/10/12-01","description":"Just rambling"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Halloween - The Amazing KDE","permalink":"/en/2022/10/31-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Tragedy....","permalink":"/en/2022/09/07-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nIt\'s been a long time since I updated my Blog. Although the main reason is that I\'m lazy, that doesn\'t mean I haven\'t been doing anything...   \\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Rambling Corner\\n\\nI haven\'t updated my Blog recently, but quite a few things have happened.\\n\\n* Had a great trip around Southeast Asia - Malaysia & Thailand\\n\\n* Writing a Universal script\\n\\nThese two things are probably the most worth writing about from late September up until today.\\n\\n# Southeast Asia\\n\\nNothing special really\u2014I just went to Malaysia and Thailand on a business trip.\\n\\n## Malaysia\\n\\nHonestly, even though I try not to have prejudices, I did realize I had some bias toward Southeast Asia. I originally thought Malaysia\'s infrastructure and such would be way behind Taiwan\'s, only to go there and realize the clown was me...\\n\\nMalaysia has pretty solid infrastructure and gives off an overall laid-back yet prosperous vibe, and their e-payment adoption is streets ahead of Taiwan.... Seriously makes you feel like if Taiwan doesn\'t step up its game, it\'s going to fall way behind...\\n\\n## Thailand\\n\\nMy impression of Thailand was still stuck back in 2014. On this trip I noticed their digitalization is actually pretty decent... Also maybe because I stayed in Pattaya this time, it felt less rural than my previous time in Phetchaburi.\\n\\nThough since you can easily buy stuff from PChome Thai in Taiwan nowadays, I didn\'t really feel the need to bring back a ton of local souvenirs...\\n\\n# Universal Script\\n\\nStrictly speaking, this is the thing really worth writing about. It\'s basically an expanded version of the `Arch Linux Automatic Install Script` I wrote before, aiming to cram in all the Unix-like OSes I use myself.\\n\\nCurrently (planned) scope covered:\\n\\n* Manjaro Linux with Desktop for Console and VNC/RDP server\\n  \\n  * KDE (90% complete)\\n  \\n  * Xfce (0% complete)\\n  \\n  * Gnome (0% complete) - considering whether to drop this\\n  \\n  * Cinnamon (0% complete)\\n\\n* Fedora Linux with Desktop for Console and VNC/RDP server (0%)\\n\\n* Linux Mint with Desktop for Console and VNC/RDP server (0%) - considering whether to keep\\n\\n* Arch Linux (0%)\\n\\n* Photon OS (0%)\\n\\n* FreeBSD (0%)\\n\\n## Concept and Approach\\n\\nBasically, it\'s about writing an auto-detection script first that routes to the corresponding child script for configuration or installation.\\n\\n```mermaid\\ngraph TB\\n    id1[Bash script from web server via curl] --\x3e id2{define children script via system Var}\\n    id2 -- Manjro Linux in /etc/os-release --\x3e id3{Select type as Console or VNC/RDP server}\\n    id2 -- Arch Linux in /etc/os-release --\x3e id4{Select type as Server or Console or ...etc}\\n    id3 -- select Console --\x3e id5{auto select by DE ex:KDE or Cinnamon}\\n    id5 -- detect KDE--\x3e id6[run children script for KDE console usage set up]\\n```\\n\\nThat diagram roughly captures the logic above.\\n\\nRight now the progress is basically that I\'ve finished writing that initial selector script, and the core Manjaro part (mainly KDE) is done. The other DE sections will need fine-tuning after I actually install those editions.\\n\\n## Other Takeaways\\n\\n### Script - if condition\\n\\nThis is probably the feature I used most while writing the script recently.. definitely spent quite some time studying how to write the conditions.\\n\\n## Script - source other script\\n\\nI introduced this feature pretty late in the process, and it\'s awesome... Basically, just like in other programming languages, you can split out modules you\'ve already written (or highly repetitive code) into separate files and load them using `source`. Combined with `function` features I learned later, you can load everything first and only call the function when needed, and even pass additional arguments when calling it.\\n\\n## Script - function\\n\\nThis started out as a fancier way to write scripts\u2014originally intended for cosmetic stuff like banners or menus\u2014but later I found you can load all kinds of random things through functions... so I started going wild with it....\\n\\n## VNC/RDP\\n\\nThe biggest discovery is that these two things can actually coexist and run together.... just install what needs to be installed and you\'re good to go....\\n\\nIn a Manjaro environment, you need `tigervnc` for the VNC server and `xrdp` for the RDP server.\\n\\nOne caveat though: when connecting to `xrdp`, you must not pre-fill the username by default\u2014it\'ll cause issues. You need to go in blank and type it afterward.\\n\\n## DE review\\n\\nPreviously I used Manjaro XFCE for VNC and Manjaro KDE for console. However, today I found GNOME really doesn\'t suit my taste, so I took a cue from Linux Mint and tried Cinnamon and found it\'s actually pretty okay\u2014especially since you can back up the entire DE\'s settings with `dconf dump /org/cinnamon > file.name`... I\'m so happy about that....\\n\\nMaybe later I\'ll try switching my main DE from KDE over to Cinnamon, though since Manjaro Cinnamon isn\'t an Official edition, its Chinese localization and performance tuning aren\'t super polished... there\'s a good chance it\'ll take a lot of time to fine-tune....\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nI\'ve actually accumulated quite a bit of material to write about during this period... but honestly out of sheer laziness I simplified it again and again... Once it\'s finished later, I\'ll break it apart and write it into the Wiki."},{"id":"2022/09/07-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/09/07-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-09-07-01.md","title":"Tragedy....","description":"Way too tragic","date":"2022-09-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Tragedy....","date":"2022-09-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/09/07-01","description":"Way too tragic"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Haven\'t Updated in a While","permalink":"/en/2022/10/12-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ansible Private Usage","permalink":"/en/2022/08/12-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nToday I suddenly discovered... my `/var/lib/docker/volumes` directory is empty...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Story Summary\\n\\nRight while I was recently wrestling with `Drone CI`\u2014yet another tool I was forced to give up on due to lack of skill....\\n\\nI suddenly realized my `/var/lib/docker/volumes` directory was empty.....\\n\\nThinking back, could it have been caused a month or two ago when I got itchy fingers and used `portainer` to clean up `unused` directories in the volume library... If so, it\'s pretty impressive that my internal services kept running normally for one or two months....\\n\\n# Scope of Impact\\n\\nActually it\'s nothing special\u2014just that all internal services got wiped out.... Looks like today\'s afternoon will be great practice for rebuilding everything from scratch.....\\n\\n# Remediation\\n\\nAll I can say is thank goodness I kept all my `docker-compose.yml` files backed up beforehand, so the services just need to be rebuilt.... and that\'s it... (having to redo the painful configuration all over again, though).\\n\\n# Silver Lining\\n\\nThe only thing that counts as a silver lining is that because I had to reconfigure everything, I went and read the docs for these services and discovered they added new features in newer versions that I can put to use right now (finding joy in misery, I guess....)."},{"id":"2022/08/12-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/08/12-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-08-12-01.md","title":"Ansible Private Usage","description":"For personal use","date":"2022-08-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.18,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Ansible Private Usage","date":"2022-08-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/08/12-01","description":"For personal use"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Tragedy....","permalink":"/en/2022/09/07-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Self-hosted Git server","permalink":"/en/2022/08/09-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAfter playing around with Ansible for a few days, I have a few thoughts regarding personal use.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Concepts\\n\\n## About Ansible\\n\\nHonestly, Ansible really is a powerful tool, and it has a huge community quietly contributing behind it, so there are tons of `playbook` examples on `GitHub` you can reference and borrow from.\\n\\nBeing able to test out my setup smoothly is really thanks to these two references:\\n\\n* Ansible tutorial video series by Learn Linux TV\\n  [![Getting started with Ansible 01 - Introduction - YouTube](http://img.youtube.com/vi/3RiVKs8GHYQ/sddefault.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3RiVKs8GHYQ&list=PLT98CRl2KxKEUHie1m24-wkyHpEsa4Y70)\\n  This series explains things in great detail; anyone interested in Ansible should watch it first.\\n\\n* [GitHub - PauloPortugal/manjaro-playbook: Manjaro/Arch Linux Ansible provision playbook](https://github.com/PauloPortugal/manjaro-playbook)\\n  \\n  Since my main desktop is `Manjaro`, this repo was super helpful for me.\\n\\n## Limitations of Ansible\\n\\nEven though Ansible is powerful and makes everything look awesome, it still has these limitations (for me):\\n\\n* There is no interactive manual input during execution\u2014everything has to be written out in advance. If you host it on a public Git server like `GitHub`, security concerns come up.\\n\\n* Behavioral differences are defined via Host Vars, while non-repetitive actions are controlled by separate playbook sections. Combined with the point above, it doesn\'t feel great for initial server setup right after installation.\\n\\n* It operates over an SSH account, so similarly, on a freshly installed machine without an account and corresponding SSH key set up yet, you can\'t use it right away.\\n\\n## Kiwi-Style Workflow\\n\\n* Handle general initial setup through a `shell script`, keeping interactive prompt options so the script stays versatile.\\n\\n* Paired with scripts, use Ansible mainly for highly repetitive, homogeneous tasks\u2014for example, keeping all servers updated in sync, and version-controlling config files across all servers through Ansible (since Ansible is typically used alongside a Git server).\\n\\n* Use Ansible to automatically provision environments that require complex setup\u2014such as multiple desktops, or simplifying the work when reinstalling a desktop (while a script can do this too, scripts have a harder time managing version control for individual config files).\\n\\n# Implementation\\n\\nTaking a closer look at what I\'m currently working on, aside from machines that aren\'t on the internal network where `ansible` comes in handy, pretty much everything else can already be handled with an internal `Git server` + `shell script` (to some extent, even the `rsync` + `shell script` approach I originally wanted would have been enough....)."},{"id":"2022/08/09-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/08/09-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-08-09-01.md","title":"Self-hosted Git server","description":"Do it yourself and be self-sufficient","date":"2022-08-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.15,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Self-hosted Git server","date":"2022-08-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/08/09-01","description":"Do it yourself and be self-sufficient"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ansible Private Usage","permalink":"/en/2022/08/12-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Kindle E-Book Push Format Change","permalink":"/en/2022/08/02-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nBecause I want to use `ansible` and `ansible-pull` to automate my <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>Desktops & Servers</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">various toys</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, I needed a Git server to go along with it. Since I felt it\'s safer to keep things at home, I spun up a `gitea` instance.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Installation Process\\n\\nThere\'s actually not much to say\u2014it\'s just a simple `docker-compose up -d`. The only pitfall was that `sqlite` couldn\'t work properly, so I switched to `mariadb` and got it working in one shot... All the detailed notes are placed in the `wiki`.\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nBecause it\'s on an internal network and on a shared Docker host, I ended up only using the very insecure `http` for Git operations (I didn\'t even configure `SSH`), so you could say it\'s super bare-bones...\\n\\nNext, I\'ll shift my focus to <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>Ansible</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">netboot.xyz</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (wait, something feels off here...).\\n\\nNo, just kidding\u2014I\'ll still focus on Ansible first, after all I don\'t really have much practical use for Netboot.xyz other than showing off..."},{"id":"2022/08/02-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/08/02-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-08-02-01.md","title":"Kindle E-Book Push Format Change","description":"Three days without reading makes one\'s face unseemly","date":"2022-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.71,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Kindle E-Book Push Format Change","date":"2022-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/08/02-01","description":"Three days without reading makes one\'s face unseemly"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Self-hosted Git server","permalink":"/en/2022/08/09-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Organizing Oracle Cloud Free Tier","permalink":"/en/2022/08/02-02"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nI\'ve long had the habit of using `calibre` + the `kindle app` to read e-books <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>freely provided by kind souls</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">freeloaded</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>. A while ago, Amazon announced that the long-standing `mobi` format was being phased out in favor of `epub`...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Main Changes\\n\\nI\'ve always used `calibre-web` to <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>manage</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">show off</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> the e-books I have on hand. After checking, thankfully the newer version supports this feature.\\n\\nSo I happily swapped out the image... (Besides, the converter bundled with the image I used before isn\'t needed under the new rules anyway, and I never even used it back in the old version...\\n\\n## Calibre-web\\n\\n```json\\nversion: \'3\'\\nservices:\\n  calibre-web:\\n    image: lscr.io/linuxserver/calibre-web:latest\\n    container_name: calibre-web\\n    environment:\\n      - PUID=1000\\n      - USE_CONFIG_DIR=true\\n      - OAUTHLIB_RELAX_TOKEN_SCOPE=1\\n      - DOCKER_MODS=linuxserver/calibre-web:calibre\\n    volumes:\\n      - /Books/lib/location:/books\\n      - /var/lib/docker/volumes/calibre/config:/config\\n    labels:\\n      traefik.enable: true\\n      traefik.http.routers.calibre-web.rule: Host(`$Your_Domain`)\\n      traefik.http.routers.calibre-web.tls: true\\n      traefik.http.routers.calibre-web.tls.certresolver: myresolver\\n    restart: unless-stopped\\n```\\n\\nYep, I switched to the more frequently updated image from `linuxserver.io`.\\n\\n## Result\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/calibre-web-epub.PNG)\\n\\nOnly one format shows up for me because I already cleared out all the `mobi` files; otherwise there would be a dropdown menu to pick from.\\n\\n# Bonus Effect\\n\\nHonestly, not needing two formats is great. Previously I had to convert downloaded books using desktop `calibre` and upload both formats to the server, taking up nearly double the storage.. Now it saves both effort and disk space.\\n\\nKilling two birds with one stone\u2014very nice.\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nActually, I don\'t really know what the difference is between `mobi` and `epub`... Still, unifying on a single format is definitely more convenient...."},{"id":"2022/08/02-02","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/08/02-02","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-08-02-02.md","title":"Organizing Oracle Cloud Free Tier","description":"Even free stuff needs maintenance","date":"2022-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.78,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Organizing Oracle Cloud Free Tier","date":"2022-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/08/02-02","description":"Even free stuff needs maintenance"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Kindle E-Book Push Format Change","permalink":"/en/2022/08/02-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bored~ Tweaking Blog Theme Settings","permalink":"/en/2022/07/27-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was archived and reposted from the old site.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\n<ruby><font  color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>Throwback Series</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  color=\\"red\\">Slacking at Work</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>: Chronicles of a Freeloader ~~ Messing Around with OCI - Part.I (There may or may not be a Part.II)\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Story Outline\\n\\n## Previously\\n\\nA few days ago while messing around with the machines (Wait, that phrasing sounds a bit sketchy?!), I noticed the freeloaded VPS currently serving most of my services was severely short on RAM.. so I decided to set my sights on that ARM-based VPS that had been sitting idle.\\n\\n## The Main Event\\n\\n### Rebuilding\\n\\nHonestly, I don\'t even know how that old system got so messed up before.. even Traefik\'s corresponding features were acting weird. So I put the system out of its misery and spun up a brand new one with the exact same specs (actually I originally wanted to split it into two x86 instances.. but looking at that gorgeous 4Gbps network and 24GB RAM, I shamelessly clicked it again).\\n\\n### Side Episode\\n\\nAlong the way I discovered it could create four network interfaces, so I excitedly created four, then despairingly discovered you can only have two `public IP`s, so I deleted two. Then I discovered having two IPs caused Traefik to go haywire.... so I deleted one more...\\n\\n## Service Migration\\n\\nActually, the biggest takeaway was discovering that `Traefik` really is a Swiss Army knife\u2014the URLs it reverse-proxies can be completely unrelated to its own domain.... Pretty awesome, so I moved the services over without users noticing a thing and without changing the domains....\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nAlthough it\'s super convenient to migrate services painlessly, the price was that the `docker-compose.yml` files I had originally organized by machine DN turned into a complete mess. Though in the end I still sorted them out by host machine (all of this lives in a private repo where nobody else can see it anyway, so what am I even talking about)."},{"id":"2022/07/27-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/07/27-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-07-27-01.md","title":"Bored~ Tweaking Blog Theme Settings","description":"Trying a different flavor","date":"2022-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Bored~ Tweaking Blog Theme Settings","date":"2022-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/07/27-01","description":"Trying a different flavor"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Organizing Oracle Cloud Free Tier","permalink":"/en/2022/08/02-02"},"nextItem":{"title":"Isso - again","permalink":"/en/2022/07/26-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nThe <ruby><font  color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>Throwback Series</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  color=\\"red\\">Slacking at Work</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> series is back again... Since I had nothing better to do, I decided to dig into the current NexT theme to see if there\'s anything fun to play with...\\n\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Modifications\\n\\n## Codeblock - Code Fence Effect Adjustments\\n\\n### Config Changes\\n\\n```json\\ncodeblock:\\n  # Code Highlight theme\\n  # All available themes: https://theme-next.js.org/highlight/\\n  theme:\\n    light: default\\n    dark: tomorrow-night\\n  prism:\\n    light: prism\\n    dark: prism-dark\\n  # Add copy button on codeblock\\n  copy_button:\\n    enable: true\\n    # Available values: default | flat | mac\\n    style: mac\\n```\\n\\n### Preview\\n\\n#### Using Default/Flat Style\\n\\n![Default & Flat Effect](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/default_style.PNG)\\n\\n#### Using Mac Style\\n\\n![Mac Effect](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mac_style.PNG)\\n\\nLooks like Mac style is a bit flashier.....\\n\\n## Tag Effect Adjustments\\n\\n### Config Changes\\n\\n```bash\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Tags Settings\\n# See: https://theme-next.js.org/docs/tag-plugins/\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n# Mermaid tag\\nmermaid:\\n  enable: ture\\n  # Available themes: default | dark | forest | neutral\\n  theme:\\n    light: default\\n    dark: dark\\n```\\n\\n### Preview\\n\\n#### Enabling Mermaid Diagrams\\n\\n##### Markdown Code\\n\\n```bash\\n#```mermaid                                 #\u9019\u500B\u8981\u7528code fence\u8A9E\u6CD5\u8868\u793A\\nsequenceDiagram\\n    Alice->John: Hello John, how are you?\\n    loop every minute\\n        John--\x3eAlice: Great!\\n    end\\n#```\\n```\\n\\n##### Actual Result\\n\\n```mermaid\\nsequenceDiagram\\n    Alice->John: Hello John, how are you?\\n    loop every minute\\n        John--\x3eAlice: Great!\\n    end\\n```\\n\\n#### Different Styles - default\\n\\n##### Light theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-light-default.PNG)\\n\\n##### Dark theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-dark-default.PNG)\\n\\n#### Different Styles - dark\\n\\n##### Light theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-light-dark.PNG)\\n\\n##### Dark theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-dark-dark.PNG)\\n\\n#### Different Styles - forest\\n\\n##### Light theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-light-forest.png)\\n\\n##### Dark theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-dark-forest.PNG)\\n\\n#### Different Styles - neutral\\n\\n##### Light theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-light-neutral.PNG)\\n\\n##### Dark theme\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/mermaid-dark-neutral.PNG)\\n\\nI later discovered it might be an issue with my Dark theme.js\u2014when the Mermaid light & dark themes are set differently, it only renders the light theme style.. so in the end I set both to the dark theme.\\n\\n# Conclusion...\\n\\nJust having way too much free time... honestly I barely even use Mermaid for drawing diagrams anyway..."},{"id":"2022/07/26-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/07/26-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-07-26-01.md","title":"Isso - again","description":"A comment system I refuse to give up on","date":"2022-07-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.15,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Isso - again","date":"2022-07-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/07/26-01","description":"A comment system I refuse to give up on"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Bored~ Tweaking Blog Theme Settings","permalink":"/en/2022/07/27-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ansible - again","permalink":"/en/2022/07/25-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nThe <ruby><font color=\\"lightblue\\"><del>reviewing the past to learn the new</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">rehashing old stuff</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> series is back again... This time it\'s the ISSO comment system...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nI\'ve set this thing up before... but I forgot why I took it down later. Was it because of splitting Docker hosts? Or because of introducing `Traefik`... Anyway, I stopped using it after that....\\n\\n## Simple Process\\n\\nHonestly, if this succeeds I\'ll have to go update the documentation in the Wiki....\\n\\n<font color=\\"red\\"><del>What\'s really weird is that even though I got `isso` running successfully.. no comment section shows up on my Blog... Seriously spooky.... Wonder if it\'s because of `https`....</del></font>\\n\\nSeriously spooky.. I tested so many times with `hexo s` and failed.. then when I actually ran `hexo g -d`, the comment system was suddenly there....\\n\\n# Conclusion...\\n\\nAfter all that fuss, even though nobody\'s going to leave comments on this self-indulgent blog of mine.. I still kind of wanted to set up a comment system just to see if really nobody does... People sure love making trouble for themselves when they get older..."},{"id":"2022/07/25-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/07/25-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-07-25-01.md","title":"Ansible - again","description":"Never giving up on Ansible","date":"2022-07-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.81,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Ansible - again","date":"2022-07-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/07/25-01","description":"Never giving up on Ansible"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Isso - again","permalink":"/en/2022/07/26-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"2022-07-21-Curtail","permalink":"/en/2022/07/21-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nLately I\'ve been <ruby><font color=\\"blue\\"><del>tirelessly studious</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">bored out of my mind</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, so I picked Ansible back up again to study it after having touched it briefly and given up before.\\n\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nAs written in <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[My Wiki](https://kiwi0093.github.io/wiki/wiki/Network/Ansible/Install%20&%20Configuration/1-Intro/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [Kiwi Wiki](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs), Ansible entries are currently being organized)*, this is a handy server management tool from Red Hat. Using Python + SSH, it can run playbooks or commands from the management node on the client side to control and manage clients. Its biggest advantage is having many modules to verify client state (for example, whether a certain package is installed, etc.) in order to achieve configuration management across clients.\\n\\n## Limitations\\n\\n* Basically, to connect smoothly you need to pair it with an SSH private key that doesn\'t require entering a passphrase each time.\\n\\n* Execution speed isn\'t as fast as running a script directly.\\n\\n* Unless there is a large amount of repetitive configuration, it won\'t be faster or easier to use than a general-purpose shell script.\\n\\n## General Structure\\n\\nBasically, you use `inventory` to manage the target hosts and `action-definition.yml` to define automated actions.\\n\\nThis `action-definition.yml` can be a top-level playbook, the main tasks inside a role, or another `action-definition.yml` pulled into tasks via `inculde_tasks`. That way, when writing, you can build standalone objects piece by piece and then tie them all together in a top-level playbook with variables and conditionals\u2014eventually achieving a generic server management framework (at which point you can go back to `inventory` to define different groups doing different things).\\n\\n# Conclusion...\\n\\nHalfway through writing this, I realized that for configuring servers, running a script is still much easier and more pleasant, and easier to modify too. However, for state management, this is way more convenient than scripts...."},{"id":"2022/07/21-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/07/21-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-07-21-01.md","title":"2022-07-21-Curtail","description":"Image Compression","date":"2022-07-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":0.68,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"2022-07-21-Curtail","date":"2022-07-21T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/07/21-01","description":"Image Compression"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ansible - again","permalink":"/en/2022/07/25-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Home DNS Going Haywire","permalink":"/en/2022/07/20-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nI saw this on Feedly....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Overview\\n\\nThis is an image compression program. For someone like me who throws images onto GitHub as an image host, it should be quite a necessary tool..\\n\\n[Curtail is an Awesome Image Compressor Tool for Linux - OMG! Ubuntu! (omgubuntu.co.uk)](https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/2022/07/curtail-is-an-image-compressor-app-for-ubuntu)\\n\\n## Installation\\n\\nSince I use `Manjaro`, it just takes one line\\n\\n```bash\\nyay -S curtail\\n```\\n\\nAnd it\'s all set....\\n\\n## Usage\\n\\nLaunch it to bring up the GUI, then drag the images in.... done.....\\n\\n# Conclusion...\\n\\nA simple and handy little tool. Honestly, configuring auto-upload in MarkText is way more troublesome."},{"id":"2022/07/20-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/07/20-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-07-20-01.md","title":"Home DNS Going Haywire","description":"DNS Down...","date":"2022-07-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.01,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Home DNS Going Haywire","date":"2022-07-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/07/20-01","description":"DNS Down..."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"2022-07-21-Curtail","permalink":"/en/2022/07/21-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bye-Bye WSL~~","permalink":"/en/2022/07/16-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nBecause of annoying ads... I set up AdGuard at home, but for the past few days, for some reason, my home internal network has been failing because DNS went down....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Simple Debugging\\n\\nThis section is basically nonsense..\\n\\nSeeing a DNS error whenever there\'s no internet connection.... it\'s definitely something fishy with DNS.\\n\\n# Workaround\\n\\nCurrently, I pointed the DNS specified in DHCPd to another dnsmasq instance (since I need to map a bunch of internal DN services). And, lo and behold, it worked like magic\u2014the home network is super smooth now (except for seeing ads).\\n\\n# Root Cause Analysis\\n\\n* AdGuard is running normally\\n\\n* The disconnections are intermittent\\n\\n* The same machine is also running `tdarr`\\n\\nMy guess (the irresponsible version with no evidence) is that the machine is overloaded, causing AdGuard to hang on by a thread and trigger the issue...\\n\\n# Conclusion...\\n\\nBuy a new server, a beefier one.. (just kidding)\\n\\nIn the future, I\'ll probably need more Docker hosts to split up resources and ensure core backbone services have enough resources to run."},{"id":"2022/07/16-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/07/16-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-07-16-01.md","title":"Bye-Bye WSL~~","description":"Farewell","date":"2022-07-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Bye-Bye WSL~~","date":"2022-07-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/07/16-01","description":"Farewell"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Home DNS Going Haywire","permalink":"/en/2022/07/20-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Obsidian As Blog/Wiki editor","permalink":"/en/2022/06/22-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nSince it\'s a company computer, I had been holding back from messing around too much, thinking I\'d just make do with WSL2+WSLg and not push my luck (though honestly that was already pushing it pretty far). But last weekend, when my itchy fingers decided to tidy up the ManjaroWSL instance I was using, I realized I couldn\'t reproduce that fully functional WSL setup I had just wiped out... and so I said bye-bye to WSL.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Current Solution\\n\\nCalling it a \\"solution\\" sounds a bit heavy\u2014it\'s really just a Manjaro KDE guest in VirtualBox @ Windows 10 host.\\n\\n# The Painful Journey\\n\\n### Hypervisor Choice\\n\\nActually, before settling on the final setup, I hesitated a lot between VirtualBox and VMware Workstation Player...\\n\\nTo be honest, if it weren\'t a company machine where I had to be careful about licensing, I would have gone straight for VMware Workstation Pro..\\n\\nWhy didn\'t I pick QEMU? Well.... the answer is dead simple: I assumed performance on a Windows host would be terrible, so I skipped it without even testing....\\n\\nIn the end, VMware Player\'s features were just too bare-bones\u2014it doesn\'t even have auto-resize.... so I reluctantly gave up on it.\\n\\n### Convert from WSL to Virtualbox\\n\\nHonestly I only have myself to blame for being stupid here...\\n\\nWhen removing WSL I was still half-hearted about it, so Hyper-V related components weren\'t cleanly uninstalled, and I ran into almost every weird symptom you\'d normally see over a whole month:\\n\\nLike the guest OS randomly freezing, Linux failing to boot with multiple cores, or being super slow even when it worked....\\n\\nIn the end, turning off Windows\'s Virtual Machine Platform fixed everything... zero bullshit issues after that.\\n\\n### System Tuning\\n\\nWe\'re really lucky to live in this era\u2014none of that manual post-install base tuning is needed anymore, everything comes preconfigured out of the box...\\n\\nJust remove what you don\'t want, install what you need, and you\'re ready to roll...\\n\\n### The Obligatory Rant\\n\\nEven though everything looks great, I still have to complain about that Guest/Host shared folder feature. I guess it\'s not that they didn\'t want to make it good, there must be real limitations, because in short, if I put my Git repo inside that shared folder, all kinds of bizarre issues happen: `git clone` fails mysteriously, weird files under `.git` get locked by the host and can\'t be removed... or `npm install` fails because symlinks fail...\\n\\nCompletely shattered my fancy dream of doing Git & Hexo on Linux while editing in Windows; now I just have to be good and do all of that inside Linux...\\n\\n### Next Steps\\n\\nNeed to test whether a multi-monitor setup can run one full-screen OS per monitor (if that works, it\'d actually be pretty awesome).\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nWindows is best left for gaming (and work).... WSL, Microsoft\'s attempt at integrating Linux, really feels like a crippled piece of cannon fodder\u2014can\'t run anything depending on systemd (I tried genie systemd and it was pretty awful too), plus `resolv.conf` has its own set of issues.\\n\\nIn an era where Linux increasingly depends on systemd, I can only describe WSL as tasteless yet hard to throw away.\\n\\nAnd as for that flashy-looking WSLg\u2014it\'s not terrible, but it\'s basically just VcXsrv wrapped in a Microsoft skin without any special Microsoft black magic..."},{"id":"2022/06/22-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/06/22-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-06-22-01.md","title":"Obsidian As Blog/Wiki editor","description":"Using Obsidian as the primary editor","date":"2022-06-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Obsidian As Blog/Wiki editor","date":"2022-06-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/06/22-01","description":"Using Obsidian as the primary editor"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Bye-Bye WSL~~","permalink":"/en/2022/07/16-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Reserve Proxy for Private Network","permalink":"/en/2022/03/15-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nSince Typora started charging earlier, I switched to TextMark as my new Markdown Editor. Although there wasn\'t anything wrong with it, seeing someone use Obsidian for note-taking made me want to switch over as well.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Basic Usage\\nIt\'s actually very straightforward because Obsidian is a cross-platform note-taking app that can be installed directly regardless of which OS you use. You just open an existing directory and point it to your current Git repo location.\\n\\n# What Stood Out\\nHonestly.. I haven\'t seen it yet... Maybe it\'s because I don\'t really need its strongest feature\u2014backlinks\u2014yet?\\nAlso, some people love Obsidian\'s graph view, enjoying the sight of a bunch of nodes connected together and feeling like their knowledge is all linked up too.\\nHowever, when I used Obsidian to directly open the notes I had written before... they were all isolated nodes with nothing connected....\\nMaybe it needs extra setup later, though considering I don\'t read my notes directly in Obsidian\u2014instead converting them via Hexo into a static website and pushing them to GitHub\u2014that probably won\'t be useful anyway..."},{"id":"2022/03/15-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/03/15-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-03-15-01.md","title":"Reserve Proxy for Private Network","description":"Redirecting here and there","date":"2022-03-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Reserve Proxy for Private Network","date":"2022-03-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/03/15-01","description":"Redirecting here and there"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Obsidian As Blog/Wiki editor","permalink":"/en/2022/06/22-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Penguin & Container Evangelism - 1","permalink":"/en/2022/02/23-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nReorganizing the internal services at home.. splitting internal and external servers into Private & DMZ. However, always using `http://ip:port` for internal connections is really ugly (okay, I know I\'m obsessive), so I wanted to set up a reverse proxy to route them directly.. Unfortunately, it turned out to be another story of half-baked skills..\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Goal\\n\\nIt\'s actually pretty simple. I wasn\'t planning to do anything crazy complicated like that `dual chroot bind` setup from years ago. Instead, I used a simple `dnsmasq+adguard home` combo to handle internal DNS (including internal domain mapping and ad blocking).. Why didn\'t I use `Pi Hole`?\\nBecause I\'m lazy and I saw AdGuard Home first, though to keep things minimal I might also just move ad-blocking directly into dnsmasq.\\n\\nPS: Thanks for [this great link provided by Master](https://pgl.yoyo.org/adservers/)\\n\\n# Basic Architecture\\n\\nYou can\'t really call it an architecture... It\'s just that compared to using Traefik externally, internal services don\'t need automatic SSL (and it\'s hard to do SSL for internal domains anyway), so internally I added an `Nginx Proxy Manager` and used NPM for forwarding.\\n\\n## The Disaster\\n\\nFor some reason, what should have been a super simple config just refused to work.. for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nhttp://emby.private -> http://emby.private:8888\\n```\\n\\nYet it failed to map over.\\n\\n## Solution\\n\\nIn the end, pointing directly between Docker containers worked:\\n\\n```bash\\nhttp://emby.private -> http://emby:8888\\n```\\n\\nThat settled it (provided they are all on the same Docker network).\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nOnly one thing to say... my skills are still way too green~~~"},{"id":"2022/02/23-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/02/23-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-02-23-01.md","title":"Penguin & Container Evangelism - 1","description":"Penguins plus containers... the foundation of modern infrastructure","date":"2022-02-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.36,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Penguin & Container Evangelism - 1","date":"2022-02-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/02/23-01","description":"Penguins plus containers... the foundation of modern infrastructure"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Reserve Proxy for Private Network","permalink":"/en/2022/03/15-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Only Office Workspace","permalink":"/en/2022/02/15-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nLately I\'ve been <ruby><font  color=\\"blue\\"><del>sharing the wonders of</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  color=\\"red\\">brainwashing</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> our company\'s IT team to get them leaning as much as possible toward Linux/Containers\u2014and it actually worked...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# A Certain Magical Project\\n\\n## Origin\\n\\nThis whole thing started when the boss assigned me to support the IT department on a certain project (company confidential, so no details..).\\n\\nThe key point is that my assigned role was to act as the project\'s PM, except a meddlesome guy like me... still stuck his hands into the project\'s server architecture anyway....\\n\\n## The Main Offense\\n\\nBecause this project needed `SQL-Server + JDK environment + Samba` on the company side,\\n\\nright as our IT team was about to wheel out their heirloom Windows Server to handle everything, I ran my mouth and dropped:\\n\\n`Couldn\'t all of this be done directly with Penguins + Containers, and be easier to back up too?`\\n\\nAnd because my show-off personality had already bragged about my home setup to the IT folks before... I got hit with:\\n\\n`We think so too, except we don\'t know how\u2014could you help get the environment up and running?`\\n\\n## Result\\n\\n### Amazon Linux 2\\n\\nSince this machine will eventually run directly on AWS... the Linux distro was locked to Amazon Linux.\\n\\nI have to say, testing this thing locally on VMware Workstation is a real buzzkill....\\n\\nInstalling it is fussy and annoying (well, maybe not actually that hard\u2014mostly my own lack of skill where a single network config nearly killed me).\\n\\nOnce installed, using it actually feels very similar to `Oracle Linux` (obviously\u2014they\'re practically forked from the exact same upstream...).\\n\\n### MS SQL-Server on Docker\\n\\nCredit to M$ for being generous enough to provide an official Docker image for SQL-Server 2019, so all I had to do was install it...\\n\\n(Yep, what I call \\"development\\" was just cranking out a working `docker-compose.yml` and calling it a day.)\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nSo far it\'s actually been pretty fun (then why all the complaining earlier~~ what a tsundere).\\n\\nTo make things even more thoughtful later on, I\'ll probably write a script that automatically handles all the Amazon Linux environment configuration (<ruby><font color=\\"blue\\"><del>thoughtful automated service</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font color=\\"red\\">turning everyone else helpless</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>), so if the server ever crashes or needs cloning in the future, it can just be spun right back up....\\n\\nAnd it also gives me a chance to actually test whether the skills I know are good enough for a commercial environment or need more polishing..."},{"id":"2022/02/15-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/02/15-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-02-15-01.md","title":"Only Office Workspace","description":"open office","date":"2022-02-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Only Office Workspace","date":"2022-02-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/02/15-01","description":"open office"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Penguin & Container Evangelism - 1","permalink":"/en/2022/02/23-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid ~~ Part-II","permalink":"/en/2022/02/07-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nContinuing from the previous G-Suite issue.. today I turned my attention to OnlyOffice.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# The Beginning\\n\\nActually it wasn\'t entirely because of G-Suite; it was mainly that I was wondering whether I should further integrate and upgrade the services I host at home in the future, so I gave OnlyOffice Workspace a try. Turns out it was quite a hassle.\\n\\n# OnlyOffice Workspace\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nIt\'s basically a free version of Office 365. Workspace includes:\\n\\n* Mail\\n  \\n  * Web Client\\n  \\n  * SMTP server\\n  \\n  * Custom Domain\\n\\n* Document\\n  \\n  * Word\\n  \\n  * Excel\\n  \\n  * PowerPoint\\n\\n* Project\\n  \\n  * Task\\n  \\n  * Gantt chart\\n\\n* CRM\\n\\n* People\\n\\n* Calendar\\n\\n## Docker-compose\\n\\nUnsurprisingly, this is also installed using `docker-compose`. As usual, I\'ll put the detailed steps on the Wiki.\\n\\nHowever, there\'s a big gap in this installation setup.\\n\\nUnlike other Docker setups, its SQL initialization scripts and MySQL configs aren\'t baked into the image\u2014you have to add them manually beforehand. Because of that, there were tons of issues right from the start.\\n\\n## Server Resources\\n\\nEven though it stated the requirements right at the beginning, I foolishly tried running it first on my low-resource Oracle Cloud instance. The result was that it completely exhausted system resources and still couldn\'t start.... Later I could only recover by rebooting and frantically killing the containers during the brief window while the system was still responsive... before things went back to normal...\\n\\nAfterward, running it on my machine at home, it definitely does consume quite a bit of resources (though my binge-watching stack already takes up a good chunk of RAM\u2014around 60% RAM usage), yet it\'s still within a workable range.\\n\\n## Remaining Issues\\n\\n* Documents fail to open\\n  \\n  From what I read online, this is caused by Traefik v2 middlewares not being configured properly.\\n\\n* Don\'t know how to configure Traefik Middleware\\n  \\n  Alright.... combined with the above, that basically means I can\'t get it working right now~~\\n\\n* No Traditional Chinese interface\\n  \\n  No fix for this either.\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nJudging by the results so far, it\'s actually pretty exciting. That said, as long as Office and Google Docs still have free tiers available.. aside from having a custom-domain email address, Project management, and being able to download documents directly, there doesn\'t seem to be much incentive to jump through hoops to force-install it....."},{"id":"2022/02/07-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/02/07-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-02-07-01.md","title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid ~~ Part-II","description":"An update on the tragedy","date":"2022-02-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.4,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid ~~ Part-II","date":"2022-02-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/02/07-01","description":"An update on the tragedy"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Only Office Workspace","permalink":"/en/2022/02/15-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Typora Is Going Paid","permalink":"/en/2022/01/27-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nLooking at Google\'s announcement saying there were still nearly five months left, I got careless\u2014and so, the tragedy kept escalating....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n\\n# The True Form of the Tragedy\\n\\n## Can\'t receive emails in my inbox..\\n\\nWhat happened was such a coincidence\u2014I wouldn\'t even have noticed that my inbox had stopped receiving emails...\\nuntil one day I foolishly wanted to play HOI4 on Linux (since game streaming kept running into issues, I just brute-forced running it directly on Linux).\\nThen I forgot my Steam password, tried to reset it, and kept never receiving the email...\\nAnd that\'s when I realized, crap, the mailbox can\'t receive mail anymore...\\n\\n## Steam Solution\\n\\nUsed my phone number... reset the password and then changed the email address...\\n\\n## LinkedIn Solution\\n\\nUsed my phone number... changed the password and then swapped out both the email and phone number (otherwise having to keep turning on my China SIM card all the time is really exhausting..)\\n\\n## Oracle Cloud\\n\\nI already knew from last time that it was hopeless... but today got even worse\u2014one of the accounts got locked.... and of all things, it locked the one I was actually using...\\nAfter whining to customer support, in the afternoon I noticed that the Indian support rep actually swapped my locked account over to my Osaka account that had never been able to create a VM...\\nGuess that counts as a stroke of luck.....\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nLinking a phone number really does come in handy... otherwise today would have been completely unsolvable....."},{"id":"2022/01/27-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/01/27-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-01-27-01.md","title":"Typora Is Going Paid","description":"It actually costs money now~~","date":"2022-01-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Wiki","permalink":"/en/tags/wiki"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.21,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Typora Is Going Paid","date":"2022-01-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Blog","Wiki","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/01/27-01","description":"It actually costs money now~~"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid ~~ Part-II","permalink":"/en/2022/02/07-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid~~","permalink":"/en/2022/01/24-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAfter a recent system update, I suddenly noticed a \\"BUY License\\" prompt popping up whenever I used Typora... After looking into it, I found out Typora is going paid...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Free Alternatives\\n\\n### MarkText\\n\\n* Marktext - [Hompage](https://marktext.app/) , [Github]([GitHub - marktext/marktext: \u{1F4DD}A simple and elegant markdown editor, available for Linux, macOS and Windows.](https://github.com/marktext/marktext))\\n\\nThis is an MIT-licensed Markdown editor. In fact, this article is already being written with it.\\n\\n## Differences from Typora\\n\\n* Different hotkeys\\n  \\n  I was used to Typora\'s `ctl+/` to toggle between source code mode and preview mode. Here the default is changed to `alt+ctrl+s`, which takes some getting used to.\\n\\n* Interface changes\\n  \\n  That\'s understandable, though the biggest issue is its borderless design and the lack of a hotkey to zoom in\u2014the editing area looks pretty small, so I\'ll need to get used to it.\\n\\n* Built-in GitHub image uploader\\n  \\n  This is genuinely generous. Previously Typora relied on PicGo (`pico-go`) to upload images; having this as a built-in feature is really nice, and it only needs a GitHub repo token.\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nThere\'s definitely an awkward adjustment period when switching software, but compared to paying 14.9 USD, my stingy nature will probably drive me to get used to this editor very quickly."},{"id":"2022/01/24-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/01/24-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-01-24-01.md","title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid~~","description":"Tragedy...","date":"2022-01-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.98,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid~~","date":"2022-01-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/01/24-01","description":"Tragedy..."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Typora Is Going Paid","permalink":"/en/2022/01/27-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Home Service Reorganization","permalink":"/en/2022/01/22-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was archived and reposted from the old site.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nA couple of days ago, my long-uncontacted master brought bad news:\\n\\"G Suite is going to start charging money, run while you can~\\"\\nFor someone like me who has tons of accounts and bindings tied to the email address my master gave me, this was a bolt from the blue....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Changing Email Addresses\\n\\n## Final Deadline\\n\\n### The free edition of G Suite will officially shut down after 2022/6/30\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nEven though I have lots of bindings, the ones with the biggest stakes\u2014banks\u2014were actually the easiest to update (after all, those are tied to a natural person, so as long as the country doesn\'t change my national ID number, I\'m not screwed).\\n\\nRented VPS accounts are all pretty troublesome, though most of the servers won\'t be renewed anyway so it\'s not actually that bad (actually I just forgot to change those...), except....\\n\\n# Cloudflare\\n\\nI basically finished changing the account so I can bind a different email address, but in the process I messed up a huge bunch of corresponding SSL keys.\\n\\nThe auto-update SSL key feature really is a double-edged sword\u2014the moment DNS settings weren\'t configured properly, the domain\'s corresponding SSL key broke.... I have to wait anywhere from a week to three months for it to be released before I can use it again... =_=!!\\n\\nOn top of that, I happened to discover that domains with the proxy feature enabled can\'t have too many layers or things break... Adding this to my to-do list for now.\\n\\n# Oracle Cloud\\n\\nThis one is a tragedy that cannot be changed... Looks like I can only keep using it as long as I never forget the password.....\\n\\n# Zoho Mail\\n\\nAs an alternative, the only one I\'ve found so far is this\u2014an email service that supports custom domains, with up to 5 free mailboxes....\\n\\nWho knows how long \\"free\\" will actually stay free though....\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nAnyway, while there\'s still a free option available, I\'ll use it first. If it really stops working, I\'ll just have to host my own Mail Server. Good thing setting one up with Docker nowadays is super convenient\u2014as long as you have a server, that is...."},{"id":"2022/01/22-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/01/22-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-01-22-01.md","title":"Home Service Reorganization","description":"Reorganizing my home services","date":"2022-01-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.43,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Home Service Reorganization","date":"2022-01-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/01/22-01","description":"Reorganizing my home services"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The Tragedy Stemming from G Suite Going Paid~~","permalink":"/en/2022/01/24-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Oracle Cloud","permalink":"/en/2022/01/10-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAfter experimenting with Traefik and other tools on another VPS recently, I felt that my existing home architecture wasn\'t that great, so I decided to reorganize the services currently running at home.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Original Architecture and Services\\n\\n## Original Services\\n\\n### Infrastructure\\n\\n* Nginx Proxy Manager - Front-end reverse proxy server\\n* Mariadb - Primary DB\\n* Portainer - Docker Web GUI\\n* Watchtower - Tool for automatically updating Containers/Images\\n\\n### Services\\n#### Basic Network Services\\n* Wireguard - VPN to Home\\n* Guacamole - Web terminal to Home\\n#### Multimedia Network Services\\n* Navidrome - Music Streaming\\n* Calibre-web - E-Book Library \\n##### Binge-Watching Bundle\\n* qbittorent - Major BT downloader\\n* Emby - Media Front-end\\n* Sonarr - Automatic tool for Drama/Anime\\n* Radarr - Automatic tool for Movie\\n* Lidarr - Automatic tool for Music\\n* Chinesesubfinder - Automatic tool for Chinese Sub file\\n* Jackett - RSS feed interface\\n* Flaresovlerr - Tool for supporting Jackett\\n#### Others\\n* Librenms - Monitoring \\n* phpBB - BBS\\n* Heimdall - Dashboard\\n\\n# Planned Updated Version\\n\\n### Infrastructure\\n\\n* Nginx Proxy Manager - Replace with Traefik\\n* Mariadb - Going forward, instead of centralizing it, set up separate DBs per service based on requirements to simplify backups\\n* Portainer - Keep\\n* Watchtower - Keep\\n\\n### Services\\n\\n#### Basic Network Services\\n\\n* Wireguard - Keep\\n* Guacamole - Keep\\n\\n#### Multimedia Network Services - Keep Everything\\n\\n* Navidrome - Keep\\n* Calibre-web - Keep \\n\\n##### Binge-Watching Bundle - Keep Everything\\n\\n* qbittorent - Major BT downloader\\n* Emby - Media Front-end\\n* Sonarr - Automatic tool for Drama/Anime\\n* Radarr - Automatic tool for Movie\\n* Lidarr - Automatic tool for Music\\n* Chinesesubfinder - Automatic tool for Chinese Sub file\\n* Jackett - RSS feed interface\\n* Flaresovlerr - Tool for supporting Jackett\\n\\n#### Others\\n\\n* Librenms - Pretty pointless; still considering what to replace it with or whether to drop it\\n* phpBB - Unused, dropping it\\n* Heimdall - Unused, dropping it"},{"id":"2022/01/10-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2022/01/10-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2022-01-10-01.md","title":"Oracle Cloud","description":"A generous cloud provider","date":"2022-01-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Oracle Cloud","date":"2022-01-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2022/01/10-01","description":"A generous cloud provider"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Home Service Reorganization","permalink":"/en/2022/01/22-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Traefik","permalink":"/en/2021/11/18-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nWhile browsing around casually, I noticed Oracle has an Always Free Cloud tier, so on an impulse I signed up.\\n\\n[Oracle Cloud](https://www.oracle.com/cloud/)\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Basic Overview\\n\\nThe free tier comes with a huge pile of DB-related services (classic Oracle) that I have no use for. For me, only the following perks are really useful:\\n\\n* You can create two VMs - the free OS options are only Ubuntu/CentOS/Oracle Linux.. (no Arch-based distros and no Debian...)\\n* An outdated CPU + 1GB RAM\\n* 486Mb/s network with unlimited bandwidth\\n* Supposedly 100GB of storage combined across the two\\n\\n# Current Usage\\n\\nHonestly, something like this is great for setting up a proxy/tunnel (as long as the direct connection speed from behind the firewall is good enough...), though this time I prioritized running Calibre-Web on it because I noticed there are actually quite a few light novel fans at my company too.\\n\\nSo it felt more practical to spin up an e-book library for everyone to use (of course, I\'ll probably also put V2Ray on the same machine later on).\\n\\n# Service Stack\\n\\n* Basic OS - Ubuntu 21.0 mini\\n* Docker Conainers\\n  * Traefik  - Proxy/Redirect\\n  * Portainer - Docker GUI\\n  * watchtower - Auto Updater\\n  * Calibre-web - Ebook library \\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nAside from the otaku-friendly utility mentioned above, what\'s actually even more important is using this to practice working with Traefik. From the looks of it, the next major overhaul of my home setup will probably replace my current Nginx Proxy Manager with Traefik\u2014after all, it\'s awesome that it doesn\'t even require port mapping."},{"id":"2021/11/18-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/11/18-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-11-18-01.md","title":"Traefik","description":"A handy reverse proxy","date":"2021-11-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Container","permalink":"/en/tags/container"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.11,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Traefik","date":"2021-11-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Server","Container","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/11/18-01","description":"A handy reverse proxy"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Oracle Cloud","permalink":"/en/2022/01/10-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Pathfinder:WoTR","permalink":"/en/2021/11/05-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nBecause I wanted to self-host a Mail Server without touching my home server, I tested Traefik + V2ray...\\n\\nThe results were pretty good.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Basic Architecture\\n\\nThis has probably been explained in many places already: Traefik is a great tool that ~~automatically~~ relies on Label definitions in `Docker-compose.yml` to automatically generate the corresponding Virtual Host for each container.\\n\\n# Comparison with Nginx Proxy Manager\\n\\n1. NPM is ultimately just a proxy\u2014you still have to expose/map the container ports yourself.\\n2. NPM\'s SSL certificates are stored in `/etc/letscrypt` just like Certbot, whereas Traefik stores them directly inside `ACME.json` in a different format.\\n3. NPM is more intuitive to operate, while Traefik requires understanding `Docker-compose.yml` label definitions.\\n\\n# Results\\n\\nHonestly, it\'s quite nice. You don\'t need to define ports or anything like that\u2014basically you just define your Hostname in `docker-compose.yml` and it automatically generates everything.\\n\\nIt\'s very suitable for people who want a fully automated migration.\\n\\nAlthough there are also features like load balancing, I don\'t need them yet, so....\\n\\n# Drawbacks\\n\\nWhen using Traefik, you basically don\'t use your main domain directly; instead, you isolate every container by assigning subdomains."},{"id":"2021/11/05-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/11/05-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-11-05-01.md","title":"Pathfinder:WoTR","description":"CRPG: A D&D-like game I enjoy","date":"2021-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Gaming","permalink":"/en/tags/gaming"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.21,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Pathfinder:WoTR","date":"2021-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Gaming","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/11/05-01","description":"CRPG: A D&D-like game I enjoy"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Traefik","permalink":"/en/2021/11/18-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Windows Game Server and Stream","permalink":"/en/2021/10/13-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\n[![Pathfinder:WoTR](http://img.youtube.com/vi/nhsvrXpyulU/sddefault.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nhsvrXpyulU)\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# The Game Itself\\n\\nActually, I haven\'t even reached Chapter 2 yet\u2014after all, I only get to play in short bursts at night after the kids fall asleep, connecting to my GL-73 via Moonlight. Still, you can tell this installment feels much more like a complete game compared to the previous *Pathfinder: Kingmaker*. As for the story so far, even though it\'s a classic heroic tale, it\'s relatively dark... feels pretty great.\\n\\n# Moonlight\\n\\nIt\'s actually very useful, though it still has the following issues:\\n\\n* Insufficient frame smoothness\\n* Needs `MSTSC.EXE` to simulate a desktop to work properly\\n* Colors sometimes look weird and require reconnecting\\n\\nFirst, regarding the frame smoothness issue: my test environment uses a 1Gb wired home network, configured at around 20M bitrate with 1080p 30fps, yet it still feels stuttery at times...\\n\\nNot sure where the bottleneck is.\\n\\nNext is desktop simulation: this is mainly because Steam Big Picture isn\'t very smooth, so I have to launch it this way. Honestly, I could also just launch the game directly, but since I need to do desktop tasks like browsing Nexus Mods, using the desktop directly is still more convenient (maybe in the future if I really stream from a TV box, I\'ll handle it differently).\\n\\nFinally, the color issue: there\'s no real fix for this either\u2014I don\'t know which setting needs tweaking\u2014but reconnecting fixes it, so it doesn\'t really matter.\\n\\nAfter listing the issues, I still have to praise it: even with Intel integrated graphics, it plays smoothly... (So far in testing, only VMs on ESXi can\'t run smoothly, because they lack basic VP9 or HEVC support...)\\n\\n# Heavy Modding\\n\\nOkay, I admit the real reason my progress is so slow is that I keep restarting and heavily modding the game....\\n\\nThe mod selection isn\'t as rich as *Kingmaker* yet (though most of the essential ones are already out). Since there are already plenty of classes, I\'ve only seen one Class Mod so far, though I imagine more and more will appear later, including mods for other rideable mounts and such.\\n\\nHowever, there is something I already modded back in my *Kingmaker* days\u2014making a follower with a mercenary\'s appearance (basically treating a humanoid as an animal companion).\\n\\nI figured out how to mod that this time too.... See my Wiki for details.\\n\\nI guess that\'s about it.... I\'ll finish playing through it whenever I have time later...."},{"id":"2021/10/13-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/10/13-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-10-13-01.md","title":"Windows Game Server and Stream","description":"The most useful Windows server... a Gaming Server","date":"2021-10-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/en/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Network","permalink":"/en/tags/network"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Windows Game Server and Stream","date":"2021-10-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Windows","Network","Server","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/10/13-01","description":"The most useful Windows server... a Gaming Server"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Pathfinder:WoTR","permalink":"/en/2021/11/05-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Automated Media Tracking (Windows-Based)","permalink":"/en/2021/10/13-02"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nActually, I thought of this concept a long time ago, just never had an actual need so I didn\'t set it up...(just lazy)\\n\\nRecently though, because I wanted to play [Pathfinder:WoTR](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1184370/Pathfinder_Wrath_of_the_Righteous/), I went ahead and set it up.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Basic Concept\\n\\nIt\'s actually very simple: turn a Windows PC capable of gaming into something you can remotely stream and play on, so you can freely use any other console/device to play games without being tied down to your Gaming PC/laptop.\\n\\nThere are plenty of guides and tutorials on how to do this\u2014even [Learn Linux TV](https://www.youtube.com/c/LearnLinuxtv) has a tutorial...\\n\\nVideo below:\\n\\n[![Setting up a Windows to Linux Game Streaming Server](http://img.youtube.com/vi/z2U09XubbAE/sddefault.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z2U09XubbAE)\\n\\nIf you want to set it up, you can check out the video yourself.\\n\\n# Related Tools\\n\\n* Wake on Lan\\n\\n  This is basically a feature every motherboard has nowadays\u2014just Google how to use it in detail. On the client side, Android has APKs and Linux can use `gwake`; both are super simple.\\n\\n* Nvidia Experiance\\n\\n  This is mainly because I use an Nvidia card, so it\'s installed anyway. If you don\'t use an Nvidia card, I\'d recommend Steam Link or Kino Console, though neither is as fast as pairing with Moonlight.\\n\\n* Moonlight\\n\\n  This is a really great client\u2014fast, free, awesome, and available on every platform.\\n\\n# A Few Thoughts\\n\\n## Rambling\\n\\nI thought before that with growing needs of all kinds, you\'d end up needing lots of powerful devices: a fast tablet, a laptop beefy enough to game on, another lightweight portable laptop, a laptop that integrates well with work, and a whole bunch of others.\\n\\nSince the services at home had already been gradually virtualized and consolidated via VMs, and after introducing Docker and WireGuard the boundary between home and outside grew increasingly blurry\u2014from the Windows P2P box on a VM starting 10 years ago to Guacamole + Manjaro not long ago\u2014I\'ve really been heading down the path of cloudifying my systems so the client device goes back to truly being a thin console that doesn\'t need high speed or high performance (though a decent NIC is still needed). Yet the most original and important function of a PC/laptop for me\u2014Gaming\u2014had still never been integrated...\\n\\nThis test confirmed that as long as network conditions allow, even Gaming can be remoted along with everything else. Still, due to power consumption and other factors, I wouldn\'t recommend cramming it into a VM right from the start (even with PCI passthrough); leaving it powered on burning electricity when you\'re not gaming is just too wasteful.\\n\\n## Key Takeaways\\n\\n* The importance of Wake on Lan\\n\\n  To put it bluntly, it\'s about saving power... Honestly, if you don\'t care about electricity bills or the fate of polar bears, you could leave your Gaming Server running 24/7...\\n\\n* Auto Login\\n\\n  This is actually really important because of Windows\' quirks: if you don\'t actually log in after booting, Nvidia Shield won\'t start, so you won\'t be able to connect. And if you connect via RDP, it\'ll conflict and RDP doesn\'t actually log into the local desktop session... So without auto-login you\'d need a console you can connect to\u2014something like VNC or DWService (and you\'ll also need it when typing a PIN to pair a device, unless you just walk over to the machine locally, set it up, and leave).\\n\\n* MSTSC.EXE\\n\\n  You need to add this into Shield to launch directly as a substitute for Steam\'s Big Picture mode, otherwise you\'ll have to get a Fake HDMI dongle or actually plug in a monitor (mentioned in LLT).\\n\\n  This is another Windows quirk... but adding `MSTSC.EXE` solves that problem (though it also turns it into a remote desktop where controllers aren\'t convenient for direct control).\\n\\n## Post-Use Impressions\\n\\nMoonlight really is quite smooth\u2014when the network is solid, it doesn\'t feel much different from playing locally, though when the network is bad it stutters badly...\\n\\nAlso, as long as your network allows it, it\'s best not to set the streaming resolution lower than what the game itself is running at... because then it has to compress it down even further before sending it out... which actually introduces more latency."},{"id":"2021/10/13-02","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/10/13-02","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-10-13-02.md","title":"Automated Media Tracking (Windows-Based)","description":"A pretty useless shitpost\u2014who runs the *arr stack on Windows anyway...","date":"2021-10-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/en/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Network","permalink":"/en/tags/network"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":4.33,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Automated Media Tracking (Windows-Based)","date":"2021-10-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Windows","Network","Server","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/10/13-02","description":"A pretty useless shitpost\u2014who runs the *arr stack on Windows anyway..."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Windows Game Server and Stream","permalink":"/en/2021/10/13-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"PF Got Knocked Out","permalink":"/en/2021/09/03-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nActually, the fundamentals of automated media tracking are all the same, just that it seemed some people needed a Windows-based setup so I wrote this earlier.\\n\\nThough compared to Windows-based setups, they probably prefer the HDD sneaker-net approach...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[sleele\'s Blog](https://sleele.com/2020/03/16/%e9%ab%98%e9%98%b6%e6%95%99%e7%a8%8b-%e8%bf%bd%e5%8a%87%e5%85%a8%e6%b5%81%e7%a8%8b%e8%87%aa%e5%8a%a8%e5%8c%96/comment-page-2/#comment-1651)\\n\\n## Basic Concepts\\n\\n### Components\\n\\nRegardless of which platform you use, it\'s mainly achieved by having the following tools divide up the work:\\n\\n* [jackett](https://github.com/Jackett/Jackett) - Responsible for searching on Indexers and sending the results back.\\n* [sonarr](https://sonarr.tv/) - This is the core component. After subscribing to the TV shows you want via TVDB, it sends commands to Jackett to look for torrents, then passes the found info to the downloader to download, and finally renames and organizes the downloaded files neatly (to match Emby\'s formatting requirements).\\n* [radarr](https://radarr.video/) - Basically has the same functionality as sonarr, except this one specializes in movies. So just as it\'s recommended to run separate sonarr instances for TV shows and anime, for movies you run another radarr instance.\\n* [qBittorrent](https://www.qbittorrent.org/) - Basically a BitTorrent downloader; used mainly because sonarr can control its downloads directly through its WebUI interface.\\n* [Emby](https://emby.media/) - This one is actually optional; its real purpose is just to give your media library a pretty UI so any device can watch by account. Simply put, having it just makes things look a bit more high-end.\\n* [Chinesesubfinder](https://github.com/allanpk716/ChineseSubFinder) - A tool that automatically finds Chinese subtitles for you.\\n\\n### Basic Requirements\\n\\nSince it automatically downloads to fill up your media library, there are mainly two things to satisfy:\\n\\n* Plenty of storage space - Whether it\'s storage for the media library or temporary space for BT downloads, both eat up a lot of disk space. Especially for BT temp storage, if possible it\'s best to use a disposable HDD you don\'t mind wearing out to extend the lifespan of your media library drives.\\n* A host that can stay powered on long-term - This is also why most tutorials say to use NAS + Docker; after all, a NAS is definitely running 24/7, making it the best fit for this.\\n\\n### Brief Workflow Explanation\\n\\nOnce configured, you just add the TV shows or movies you want to subscribe to in the Sonarr/Radarr interface and leave it alone. Whatever is available on the Indexers will be automatically queued in the downloader, then you wait for it to finish downloading, it\'ll automatically rename the files, wait a bit more and it\'ll find and place the subtitles on its own, and then you\'ll see it show up on your Emby.\\n\\n### Differences from the Rest of the Blog\\n\\nThe biggest difference is that everything else on the Blog is installed via Docker. If you want to run this on Windows, you\'ll have to download the Windows versions of these tools (I\'ve basically only used the Docker method myself, though I\'ve confirmed all of these programs provide Windows builds, so there shouldn\'t be any issue).\\n\\n* [jackett](https://github.com/Jackett/Jackett/releases)\\n* [sonarr](https://sonarr.tv/#download)\\n* [radarr](https://radarr.video/#download)\\n* [qBittorrent](https://www.qbittorrent.org/download.php)\\n* [emby](https://emby.media/download.html) - Make sure it\'s the emby server edition\\n* [Chinesesubfinder](https://github.com/allanpk716/ChineseSubFinder/releases)\\n\\n### Setup Method\\n\\nI think Sleele\'s Blog explains it in great detail, so I won\'t repeat it. At most, in the next section I\'ll mention a few caveats I ran into during setup.\\n\\n### Caveats\\n\\n#### Profile\\n\\nIn sonarr/radarr\'s profile settings, you define how high of a video resolution to grab. All I can say here is don\'t get greedy. At first I set it to maximum 4K quality, and a single movie turned out to be 70GB. Unless you have ridiculously huge storage, stay within your means\u2014generally setting it around 1080p webrip puts a movie at roughly 2~5GB.\\n\\n#### indexer\\n\\nGenerally I use RARBG for US TV shows and movies, and whatever else depending on mood. For anime I use DMHY, though friends in mainland China should double-check whether these indexers are blocked by the firewall\u2014if so, remember to route the machine that Jackett connects from through a proxy/VPN.\\n\\n#### Manual Downloads\\n\\nI basically download anime manually, and Chinese dramas too (since these tools have weaker support for both). For manually downloaded files, just put them under the show/movie\'s main folder defined in sonarr/radarr and then use their manual import feature to import and organize them, and you\'re all set.\\n\\n#### Other\\n\\nThe various components mentioned above can actually be split to run on separate independent machines, just remember that `/download` and `/media` must be directly controllable by sonarr/radarr. Beyond that, I suggest you give it a try first and come back to discuss if you run into any issues (even using TeamViewer to help troubleshoot is totally fine)."},{"id":"2021/09/03-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/09/03-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-09-03-01.md","title":"PF Got Knocked Out","description":"PF got hammered until it crashed","date":"2021-09-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"FreeBSD","permalink":"/en/tags/free-bsd"},{"inline":true,"label":"Network","permalink":"/en/tags/network"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.74,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"PF Got Knocked Out","date":"2021-09-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["FreeBSD","Network","Server","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/09/03-01","description":"PF got hammered until it crashed"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Automated Media Tracking (Windows-Based)","permalink":"/en/2021/10/13-02"},"nextItem":{"title":"Integrate Arch Linux into Chrome OS","permalink":"/en/2021/08/24-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was archived and reposted from the old site.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nLately I don\'t know if I\'ve been enjoying auto-downloading shows a bit too much, because my family keeps complaining that the home internet died.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Symptoms\\n\\nNot much else to explain, really\u2014the home gateway died, and checking it showed:\\n\\n### PF state reach limit\\n\\nLooking it up online, the general reason is that the PF state entries overflowed, so PF stopped working.\\n\\n# Countermeasures\\n\\n## Basic Countermeasure\\n\\nI had asked my master about this before, and his approach was using crontab to schedule a daily reboot at a fixed time, taking advantage of that mechanism to also let FreeBSD auto-update.\\n\\nBeing a good disciple, of course I copied that approach\u2014<ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>that\'s the ticket~~</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">shikashi~~</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>\u2014but yesterday afternoon it broke again not long after rebooting.\\n\\n## sshguard\\n\\nAlright, this was actually already in the PF config file I copied from my master ages ago. The reason my master needed periodic reboots was also because this table would overflow (which shows just how many <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>people randomly brute-forcing SSH</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">assholes</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> there are).\\n\\nThen I took a closer look and realized I hadn\'t even installed sshguard..... So forget the blocklist table growing big enough to overflow\u2014I didn\'t even have a ban table set up at all, which is why it blew up even faster....\\n\\n## BT & Auto-Downloading Shows\\n\\nHonestly, I don\'t even know whether using this thing has any impact, though in my heart I lean toward \\"no impact\\"\u2014after all, I used to go all-out downloading stuff with other P2P tools before and never saw it blow up..\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nSimply put, I can only keep monitoring... and see whether the current countermeasures actually work."},{"id":"2021/08/24-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/08/24-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-08-24-01.md","title":"Integrate Arch Linux into Chrome OS","description":"This is a technique for running Arch Linux inside Chrome OS","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Integrate Arch Linux into Chrome OS","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/08/24-01","description":"This is a technique for running Arch Linux inside Chrome OS"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"PF Got Knocked Out","permalink":"/en/2021/09/03-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Recent Mood","permalink":"/en/2021/08/20-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was archived and reposted from the old site.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\n[![Integrate Arch Linux into Chrome OS](http://img.youtube.com/vi/I6sAvwHgYwQ/sddefault.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I6sAvwHgYwQ \\"Integrate Arch Linux into Chrome OS\\")\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Brief Description\\n\\nHonestly, this is something that really caught my eye. Based on my previous tests with Chrome OS-based systems, they always felt very crippled\u2014not only because they require Google services to function properly, but also because what they can do on their own is much more limited... However, this approach of using LXC to run Linux opens up a lot more possibilities (or rather, tinkerability).\\n\\nThat said, it defaults to Debian, which I <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>am not that fond of</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">don\'t really know how to use</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, and that kind of kills the mood for me. But now there\'s a way to swap what\'s inside to Arch Linux, which is genuinely exciting.\\n\\n# Other References\\n\\n## Arch Linux\\n\\nHonestly, the famously comprehensive Arch Wiki also has a related guide as follows:\\n\\n[Arch Linux for Chrome OS device](https://wiki.archlinux.org/title/Chrome_OS_devices)\\n\\n## Chrome OS\\n\\n* [Wikipedia Introduction](https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrome_OS)\\n* [Installation Guide - Chinese](http://eshensh.net:2369/how-to-install-chromeos-in-pc/)\\n* [Installation Guide - English](https://www.alphr.com/download-install-chrome-os/)\\n\\n# Hands-on Implementation\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nYeah... it\'s blank... because I don\'t own a Chromebook, and I don\'t really feel like spending money to buy one... Maybe later when I have time, I can grab a USB drive and try it on <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>my dedicated testing terminal</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">my company PC</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>."},{"id":"2021/08/20-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/08/20-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-08-20-01.md","title":"Recent Mood","description":"A pointless post solely about a middle-aged guy\'s unproductive mental state","date":"2021-08-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.98,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Recent Mood","date":"2021-08-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/08/20-01","description":"A pointless post solely about a middle-aged guy\'s unproductive mental state"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Integrate Arch Linux into Chrome OS","permalink":"/en/2021/08/24-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Way to Go, GitHub","permalink":"/en/2021/08/16-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Foreword\\n\\nLately there\'s been a lot of commotion in my life, along with some shifts in my mindset, so I\'ve been wanting to write something that, to readers, is pure rambling nonsense...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Summary\\n\\nBecause things are messy and all over the place, I\'m going to tell this story using <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>reverse chronology</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">jumping around randomly</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>.\\n\\nSimply put, I\'m not in a great mood\u2014not only have there been some frustrations on the server side, a friend did something that made me want to roll my eyes and curse him out.. and after some time, I finally realized some psychological reactions triggered by changes in my workplace/life....\\n\\nStill, I think I should be able to gradually adjust through <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>self-growth and adaptation</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">binge-reading trashy novels/videos and other leisure</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>.\\n\\n# Overview of Events\\n\\n## Desktop Crash\\n\\nThis one is the simplest and easiest to fix, because it was purely caused by a lack of technical skill.\\n\\nThe Manjaro system I\'ve been running <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>sneakily under the radar</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">blatantly, with zero fear</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> at work finally crashed starting from I/O errors because it\'s on a USB stick. Yesterday I was even dumb enough to stop `yay -Syu` midway because it was too slow,\\n\\nwhich resulted in a missing kernel and an unbootable mess... (I did manage to rescue it afterward, though).\\n\\nMeanwhile, the Manjaro xrdp setup I put together has performance so awful I have zero interest in using it.... (alright, alright, I\'ll figure something out soon~~)\\n\\nSo my mood is just completely in the dumps....\\n\\n## Nobody Uses PHPBB\\n\\nI excitedly set up a phpBB thinking it\'d be great for my circle to download stuff... Then I checked everyone\'s last login timestamp...\\n\\nDamn it\u2014they literally registered just to humor me and never used it again.... Here I was diligently posting the stuff I grabbed....\\n\\nF**k!! So jaded with the world~~\\n\\n## A Friend\'s Nonsense\\n\\nSince I\'d be silenced if I spilled the details... all I can say is:\\n\\n<font  size=\\"+10\\"  color=\\"darkpurple\\">Damn it~~ You haven\'t been a virgin for ages, what the hell are you doing~~</font>\\n\\n## Psychological Gap\\n\\nAlright, this is actually the main thing dominating my emotions lately.\\n\\n### Before\\n\\nA manager at a big company, with 20 underlings beneath me, handling upper management, subordinates, and external affairs effortlessly (even if some people thought I was a tactless jerk, I didn\'t give a damn).\\n\\nI had enough clout just by speaking up... and various odd jobs were handled by junior staff (work-related odd jobs, damn it!).\\n\\nEven though I enjoyed the convenience and POWER that came with the title~~ I loved saying, \\"I don\'t care about titles~~\\" (yeah, looking back now, total tsundere).\\n\\n### After\\n\\nA one-person department\u2014across the whole company, probably only the cleaning auntie has less say than me.... Have to do every single thing myself...\\n\\n### After setting the scene...\\n\\nSince it was my own choice, I really have nothing to complain about, and not having to manage people really did relieve a lot of stress in certain areas, yet there\'s still a sense of letdown.\\n\\nSo I started wondering: where does this feeling come from??\\n\\nI think maybe it\'s just that after all these years, once I took off the environmental Buff that never truly belonged to me, it forced me to realize once again that I\'m just an ordinary middle-aged otaku....\\n\\nMy abilities aren\'t any more outstanding than my peers; everything in the past was just good luck, not real skill...\\n\\nProbably all of that is why I feel empty and keep wanting to cling to past glories~~~\\n\\nDuring a Roman triumph, someone had to stand behind the general constantly reminding him: \\"Remember you are mortal, not a god; this glory and feeling of omnipotence is only temporary...\\"\\n\\nThe ancients were truly wise and pragmatic.\\n\\nI think I should reread *The Story of the Romans*\u2014revisit the things that made me who I am before I ever put on that fake Buff...\\n\\nMaybe\\n\\nthat way I can once again reaffirm\\n\\n<ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>my self-worth</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">that I\'m just a dime-a-dozen ordinary otaku</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>"},{"id":"2021/08/16-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/08/16-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-08-16-01.md","title":"Way to Go, GitHub","description":"Issues related to GitHub tightening security","date":"2021-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":0.77,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Way to Go, GitHub","date":"2021-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Blog","Server","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/08/16-01","description":"Issues related to GitHub tightening security"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Recent Mood","permalink":"/en/2021/08/20-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Tragedies & A Sense of Accomplishment","permalink":"/en/2021/08/16-02"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nFor security reasons, starting August 13 of this year, GitHub no longer accepts logging in via HTTPS with a username and password...\\n\\nFor details, please see [this article](https://github.blog/2020-12-15-token-authentication-requirements-for-git-operations/)\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Changing the Login Method\\n\\nMaybe because I found setting up SSH keys and all that too tedious, I had always been using a username/password to update my Blog/Wiki\\n\\nAnd today it finally broke\\n\\n## Token\\n\\nUsing a token isn\'t really a big deal\u2014after all, pico also uses a token to upload...\\n\\nBut in order to use a token, I now have to switch both my Blog and Wiki to Private to prevent the data written in _config.yml from leaking out...\\n\\nWhat a hassle...."},{"id":"2021/08/16-02","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/08/16-02","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-08-16-02.md","title":"Tragedies & A Sense of Accomplishment","description":"A story about refusing to be pushed around by the company system and turning to remote desktop instead","date":"2021-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Tragedies & A Sense of Accomplishment","date":"2021-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Server","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/08/16-02","description":"A story about refusing to be pushed around by the company system and turning to remote desktop instead"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Way to Go, GitHub","permalink":"/en/2021/08/16-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Replacing the Blog Comment System","permalink":"/en/2021/08/06-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Foreword\\n\\nOver the past few days there has actually been some <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>material</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">crappy annoying incidents</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> I could write about for my Blog/Wiki, though maybe because work has been getting back on track lately, I just felt too lazy to write....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Annoyance A - Webtop & Remote Desktop\\n\\n## Backstory\\n\\nEmbarrassing to admit... while I was <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>at work</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">slacking on company time</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, I suddenly got asked: \\"How come there are no connection logs for you on the AD Server? Haven\'t you been coming into the office??\\"\\n\\nThat\'s when it hit me: when I\'m in the office, I really do have to behave and use the company\'s system to connect to the company intranet so I don\'t draw attention...\\n\\nSuddenly, the webtop/Remote Desktop setup I previously thought was pointless became super relevant.\\n\\n## Webtop\\n\\nBecause of that, I spun up Linuxserver.io\'s webtop, only to discover.. even though it sounds great with two distros and multiple DEs to choose from,\\n\\nin actual use they all felt <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>like complete crap</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">not very smooth to use</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, so I gave up on it pretty fast. Still, I noticed its approach was simply running Apache Guacamole to expose internal VNC/RDP sessions,\\n\\nso I naively thought.. I\'ll set one up myself too....\\n\\n## Apache Guacamole\\n\\nActually, it\'s not hard to set up\u2014just write a `docker-compose.yml` and run it. Using it to connect to Windows via RDP afterward was pretty smooth, but my Manjaro turned into a tragedy..\\n\\n### Manjaro - VNC\\n\\nLooking at tutorials, it seemed simple enough: just spin up TigerVNC, tweak a few things, and it should work... Then all I got was a completely black screen...\\n\\nLater I tried `x0vncserver`, which wasn\'t much better either.. so I gave up pretty quickly...\\n\\n### Manjaro - Xrdp\\n\\nThis one at least looked half-decent once running.. but it was super slow\u2014even sluggish compared to a Windows VM with fewer resources allocated. Even though I\'m keeping it alive for now.. I already want to nuke it...\\n\\nUsing it like this, I might as well <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>just connect via WireGuard and open an RDP client</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">turns out it\'s just as awful...</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>\\n\\n\\n\\n# Annoyance B - Automated Media Stack\\n\\n## Backstory\\n\\nI saw an awesome automated media-bingeing stack on some blog, so I wanted to build one myself... and then... and then everything blew up.\\n\\n## Docker Network\\n\\nFor some reason when I spun up Emby, its default network was `192.168.0.0/16`, which collided directly with my internal network architecture... For a moment I freaked out thinking I had completely broken my `pf` config.... So I had to tear down a bunch of containers just to pinpoint where the issue was.... Afterward I went and learned how Docker container network configs are actually written.\\n\\n## RAM & Disk....\\n\\nThis one really was....\\n\\nSimply put, I was way too naive before... How could anything video-related possibly run smoothly on tiny RAM and a barely-there disk space?!\\n\\nThe bottom line was my PhotonOS had too little RAM and not enough default disk, and then I botched the online disk expansion.\\n\\nSo Saturday became a full reinstall....\\n\\n## Reinstalling PhotonOS\\n\\nStrictly speaking, this wasn\'t really an annoyance\u2014it worked well as a test of how familiar I\'ve become with PhotonOS and Docker over this period....\\n\\nIt only took me about an hour to get all the containers back up; the only irritating part was:\\n\\n## SSL\\n\\nEven though I backed up the `/etc/letsencrypt` directory, the symlinks inside its `live` directory couldn\'t just be copied straight over with `cp`, so I ended up having to recreate the links one by one manually.. so painful...\\n\\n\\n\\n# Summary\\n\\n* I\'ll look into Manjaro\'s Xrdp/VNC later when I have time; as long as it works for now, that\'s good enough...\\n* The automated media stack is really powerful now that it\'s running, though the anime part barely works at the moment... which is annoying...\\n* Been slacking lately.. looks like I\'ll have plenty of stuff to write in the Wiki later...."},{"id":"2021/08/06-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/08/06-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-08-06-01.md","title":"Replacing the Blog Comment System","description":"Self-hosting my own comment system","date":"2021-08-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/blog"}],"readingTime":1.41,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Replacing the Blog Comment System","date":"2021-08-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Server","Old-Blog","Blog"],"slug":"2021/08/06-01","description":"Self-hosting my own comment system"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Tragedies & A Sense of Accomplishment","permalink":"/en/2021/08/16-02"},"nextItem":{"title":"Tragedy~~","permalink":"/en/2021/08/05-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from an old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nPreviously I tried setting up Disqus and an unsuccessful DisqusJS, but honestly it didn\'t look great, so I switched to Isso. Though even with that, nobody\'s going to leave comments on a diary-style blog like this anyway...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Isso\\n\\nThis is a self-hosted comment system, yet it\'s very lightweight, and the Hexo-theme-NexT I\'m using has built-in support for it. While searching around for usable comment systems, none of the others felt quite right, so I decided to go with this one.\\n\\n* <font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\">Supports anonymous comments</font> I figured nobody commented probably because logging in to comment was too much hassle.. ~~actually it\'s just that nobody reads it...~~\\n* <font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\">Supports Markdown</font> So I can add notes on my own posts.. ~~how lonely do you have to be to comment notes on your own blog...~~\\n* <font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\">Accessible without a VPN</font> Unless the Great Firewall goes crazy and blocks even Cloudflare\'s CDN.\\n\\n# Installation\\n\\nI ran the server side with Docker and tweaked a bit of NexT\'s config, and it was ready to go...\\n\\nI\'ll put the detailed steps over on the Wiki.\\n\\n# Thoughts\\n\\nSeeing how simple this thing is.. I suddenly wondered, was I an idiot before??\\n\\nWhy did I go and register for a bunch of useless services and fiddle around forever only to end up with a half-baked result?\\n\\nYes.. I\'m complaining about you guys.\\n\\nDisqus, Valine.... you guys are awful to use..."},{"id":"2021/08/05-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/08/05-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-08-05-01.md","title":"Tragedy~~","description":"This is a tragedy caused by misconfiguring a Docker volume...","date":"2021-08-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Tragedy~~","date":"2021-08-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Server","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/08/05-01","description":"This is a tragedy caused by misconfiguring a Docker volume..."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Replacing the Blog Comment System","permalink":"/en/2021/08/06-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"phpBB","permalink":"/en/2021/08/04-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was archived and reposted from the old site.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nYesterday I excitedly received the server RAM brought back to Taiwan via <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\"><del>human courier</del></font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">kind Uncle Wang\'s generous help</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, and immediately upgraded my <ruby><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\">flagship carrier</font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+1\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">ESXi</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> to 32GB.\\n\\n<ruby><font  size=\\"+3\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\">Shikashi</font><rp>(</rp><rt><font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"darkred\\">However</font></rt><rp>)</rp></ruby>, tragedy struck just like that...\\n\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# What Happened\\n\\nActually, what happened is pretty simple: after my PhotonOS rebooted, I noticed the forum wouldn\'t start up properly. Looking closer, the Docker volume mapping had failed. On top of just rebooting,\\n\\nbecause the original config didn\'t have a restart policy set and it didn\'t come back up automatically after the reboot, my itchy fingers ran `docker-compose up -d`.\\n\\nAnd then Docker very smoothly went ahead and recreated the container for me....\\n\\nAnd then all the data was gone and blown up.....\\n\\n# Root Cause\\n\\nThe post-mortem investigation result left me completely speechless, pasted below:\\n\\n## Excerpt from my original docker-compose.yml\\n\\n```yaml\\n...    \\n    volumes:\\n      - /var/lib/docker/volumes/phpbb/mariadb:/bitnami/mariadb\'\\n...\\n```\\n\\n## Excerpt from my fixed, working docker-compose.yml\\n\\n```yaml\\n...    \\n    volumes:\\n      - /var/lib/docker/volumes/phpbb/mariadb:/bitnami/mariadb\\n...\\n```\\n\\nSee it? All because of one single `\'` character....\\n\\n# Impact\\n\\nNothing much, really.\\n\\nJust that all of yesterday\'s hard work went completely down the drain.....\\n\\n\\n\\nDammit..."},{"id":"2021/08/04-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/08/04-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-08-04-01.md","title":"phpBB","description":"An old-school PHP BBS","date":"2021-08-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.46,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"phpBB","date":"2021-08-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Server","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/08/04-01","description":"An old-school PHP BBS"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Tragedy~~","permalink":"/en/2021/08/05-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Wireguard","permalink":"/en/2021/07/30-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAfter moving back to Taiwan, sharing files with friends in China like I used to became more troublesome and difficult... so I thought about using a forum + cloud drive approach for sharing.\\n\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n\\n# Basic Concept\\n\\nActually, a cloud drive alone is enough for sharing files, but constantly passing download links back and forth over mobile IM apps\u2014especially when not everyone is in the same chat group (or even using different IM platforms)\u2014\\n\\nis really a hassle. So I wanted to set up a place to post links. If everything were shared solely by me, I could probably just write something like a blog to list them out.\\n\\nUnfortunately, the main people doing the sharing aren\'t really that geeky.... So I figured I\'d set up a simple platform, and after shopping around I picked phpBB to run a forum for it.\\n\\n(Pretty much the same idea as places like Light Novel Kingdom or 3DM.)\\n\\n# phpBB\\n\\n<font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"blue\\">Why choose phpBB?</font>\\n\\nGood question. Actually I didn\'t really pick it carefully\u2014mostly it\'s because back in college I set up a very old version of phpBB, though back then my conclusion was that it felt less convenient than a traditional BBS (because it was slow).\\n\\nLater on, though, these PHP-based forums popped up everywhere and the software got comparatively better.\\n\\nThen why didn\'t I pick Discuz, which dominated the Greater China region?\\n\\nEven though there are tons of comparisons online with fans on both sides, the real reason for choosing it is way simpler than all that..\\n\\n<font  size=\\"+3\\"  color=\\"darkgreen\\">Because phpBB has a Docker image you can spin up effortlessly~~~</font>\\n\\nYes, all of it comes from my lazy desire to solve everything with Docker\u2014that simple, that unpretentious of a reason.\\n\\nAs usual, the detailed setup steps will go into the Wiki.\\n\\n# Free Cloud Storage\\n\\nSpeaking of this, it\'s honestly a bit awkward. Nowadays there are plenty of free cloud drives available, yet sharing across Taiwan and China narrows the choices down a lot.\\n\\nThe ones commonly used in Taiwan either require a VPN to reach from China or are painfully slow.\\n\\nThe ones commonly used in China generally offer huge space and fast speeds inside China, but the account registration process makes anyone in Taiwan want to back out immediately.\\n\\nLater, while downloading novels, I stumbled upon one where registration only needs an email, gives 500GB of free space, requires no login to download, and has decent speeds in both China and Taiwan:\\n\\n<font  size=\\"+2\\"  color=\\"blue\\">[Chengtong Netdisk (ctfile)](https://www.ctfile.com/)</font>\\n\\nNo idea how long this thing will last either, anyway that\'s how free hosting goes\u2014everyone just uses it while it works, and if it dies we\'ll find another one."},{"id":"2021/07/30-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/07/30-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-07-30-01.md","title":"Wireguard","description":"Setting up my first WireGuard","date":"2021-07-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/en/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"}],"readingTime":1.89,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Wireguard","date":"2021-07-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["VPN","Server","Old-Blog","Linux"],"slug":"2021/07/30-01","description":"Setting up my first WireGuard"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"phpBB","permalink":"/en/2021/08/04-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Random Notes - A VPS With No Clear Purpose","permalink":"/en/2021/07/14-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAlthough I no longer need <ruby>scientific internet access<rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>circumventing the GFW, aka a \\"ladder\\"</rt></ruby> after leaving China, a bunch of services at home are still behind NAT, so setting up a VPN to connect back home is pretty normal.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# WireGuard\\n\\nYes, I didn\'t pick V2ray\u2014which I knew inside and out in China\u2014and instead went with WireGuard, which has direct support in the Linux Kernel. Here\'s why:\\n\\n* Outside of China, there\'s no need for obfuscated connections\\n* WireGuard is now directly supported by the Linux Kernel\\n* WireGuard is simpler to configure (well... once you understand it, yeah, it really is)\\n\\nHowever, because this protocol explicitly tells everyone \\"I\'m a VPN,\\" naturally, no matter how smoothly it runs in Taiwan, you can\'t use it as a ladder to climb the GFW.\\n\\nIf you want to bypass censorship, you\'re still better off sticking with something obfuscated like V2ray.\\n\\n# Setup Method\\n\\nAs usual, I put the detailed technical steps on my <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[Wiki](https://kiwi0093.github.io/Wiki-site/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [WireGuard Wiki](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs/HomeLab/Docker/Services/w-0055))*\u2014go take a look yourself.\\n\\n# Ramblings\\n\\n## Wireguard-Manager\\n\\nThe first thing I tried running was Wireguard Manager with a Web UI. Using Docker, it spun up painlessly, but... it didn\'t work... (Yeah, I spent an entire afternoon on a nice-looking interface whose core functionality simply didn\'t work...)\\n\\n## WireGuard\\n\\nLater I gave up on that and just used WireGuard directly. I have to admit, I initially thought it would be a hassle, but once I actually used it I found the image provided by `linuxserver.io` is genuinely great\u2014you just specify how many peers you want in `docker-compose.yml` from the start and it creates them all cleanly without fuss. Adding more peers to `docker-compose.yml` later also appends them automatically.\\n\\n## Linux Client\\n\\nHonestly, Android setup is super easy\u2014you literally just scan a QR code. Linux setup, on the other hand, is relatively troublesome; every single parameter has to match for it to work."},{"id":"2021/07/14-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/07/14-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-07-14-01.md","title":"Random Notes - A VPS With No Clear Purpose","description":"Dealing with an idle VPS","date":"2021-07-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"V2ray","permalink":"/en/tags/v-2-ray"},{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/en/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.4,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Random Notes - A VPS With No Clear Purpose","date":"2021-07-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["V2ray","VPN","Server","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/07/14-01","description":"Dealing with an idle VPS"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Wireguard","permalink":"/en/2021/07/30-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"BuyVM","permalink":"/en/2021/07/08-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nRecently I\'ve been <ruby><del>going to the office every day to work diligently</del><rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>hiding out at the office to escape the kids pestering me</rt></ruby>, so I took the time to reorganize some services I had previously set up, along with that VPS from last week. Honestly, its speed is just so-so\u2014testing only gets around 7~8 Mbps.\\n\\nI\'m also thinking about how to expand my Docker playground, which hasn\'t bloated to its absolute limit yet....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Experiments\\n\\n## Portainer-agent\\n\\n### Ramblings\\n\\nI installed Docker on the VPS without knowing what to do with it, so the very first container I ran was `portainer-agent`. Pairing it with my existing host dashboard, I found it actually works! Even without running `k8s` or `docker-swarm`, you can add nodes to manage (though the price is having to install `portainer-agent`).\\n\\n### Thoughts\\n\\nSince I put the setup steps on the Wiki, I\'ll just ramble a bit here. My impression is that using `portaner-ce` + `portainer-agent` together with its Stacks feature is really nice\u2014you don\'t need to drop into the console to run `docker-compose` commands. For anyone who doesn\'t insist on doing everything via CLI, I think it\'s genuinely convenient (especially since Stacks can be migrated or duplicated to other machines).\\n\\n## Nginx Proxy Manager\\n\\n### Ramblings & Thoughts\\n\\nThe main reason I installed this thing was that I originally wanted to expose various web services through multiple layers, and writing Nginx configs by hand felt annoying, so I installed it. After using it for a while, though, I noticed that Nginx `location` rules are tedious to configure and break easily, so I gradually started thinking I might as well just attach a Virtual Host for every single service. After all, this thing can request SSL certificates too, and not doing it that way would feel like <ruby><del>letting down the me who paid for a domain name...</del><rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>being needlessly fussy</rt></ruby>.\\nI originally thought about setting one up on the VPS as well, then looked at my V2ray config file and suddenly felt too lazy... forget it....\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nStill, messing around with this made me realize a problem.\\n\\nRight now, this habit of mapping a bunch of random services directly to the host IP works fine behind a private network, but on a directly exposed VPS it feels pretty sketchy. Even though nothing serious is running on it, I still don\'t like leaving the front door wide open like that. Later on, I should really get into the habit of configuring Docker networks properly and keeping things tucked inside the internal Docker network as much as possible."},{"id":"2021/07/08-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/07/08-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-07-08-01.md","title":"BuyVM","description":"Notes on a VPS","date":"2021-07-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"V2ray","permalink":"/en/tags/v-2-ray"},{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/en/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.77,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"BuyVM","date":"2021-07-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["V2ray","VPN","Server","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/07/08-01","description":"Notes on a VPS"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Random Notes - A VPS With No Clear Purpose","permalink":"/en/2021/07/14-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Recent Updates - Calibre-web & Navidrome","permalink":"/en/2021/07/05-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nAnynode, which I rented back when I was still in Suzhou, seems to have been sold, so I got an email notifying me that the service was being migrated to BuyVM. After two or three days of tinkering, I finally got the new VPS service up and running today...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# BuyVM/Frantech\\n\\n## VPS Plans\\n\\n[Plan Page](https://my.frantech.ca/cart.php?gid=39)\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/buyvm.PNG\\" alt=\\"AMD Ryzen KVM\\" style={{ width: \\"60px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />\\n\\nFor an unmetered-bandwidth VPS, <font  color=\\"green\\">$2.00USD</font> a month\u2014or only <font  color=\\"green\\">$19.67USD</font> if paid annually\u2014is a really generous price, and they even offer a Luxembourg server outside DMCA jurisdiction. On top of that, they also offer an attachable <font  color=\\"orange\\">Block Storage</font> service: for a little extra each month, you get a pretty sizable amount of capacity to use.\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/block_storage.PNG\\" alt=\\"Block Storage\\" style={{ width: \\"60px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />\\n\\nIf you want to do... whatever with it, it\'s definitely pretty handy.\\n\\nAs for speed, honestly I have no idea, since I\'m no longer physically on a network in <ruby><del>the Celestial Empire</del><rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>the neighbor across the strait</rt></ruby> to test it myself, so I can only ask friends still over there to help test.\\n\\nStill, if someone wants to host their own site but lacks the hardware and bandwidth, it\'s worth considering\u2014for under $5 USD a month you get plenty of storage to work with.\\n\\n## Server Setup\\n\\nThis VPS finally shows some real effort: instead of the SolusVM interface, it actually looks like the real deal, and the machine itself allows installing Windows or custom ISOs. That said, I tried Arch Linux and it didn\'t seem to boot very smoothly, so I just went with the default Debian option.\\n\\nThe control panel is called Stallion, and it looks much classier than the management panels used by previous budget VPS providers. Hopefully this turns out to be a solid provider across every metric.\\n\\n\\n\\n## Anynode Migration\\n\\nNow for the main point: because Anynode was sold, the way they handled the migration to BuyVM was pretty interesting. Instead of directly giving me two ready-to-use VPS instances (yes, I had two Anynode VPSes under my name), they prorated the remaining time on both instances at BuyVM\'s current rates and refunded it into my credit account. My two old Anynode boxes were valid until November this year, and originally cost only $21 USD each for two years\u2014the remaining balance converted to a total of <font  color=\\"green\\">$28.05USD</font>, which actually works out to a pretty sweet deal.\\n\\n\\n\\n## Status of Other VPSes\\n\\nSince coming back, without any real use for them, that whole pile of VPSes I held has indeed been slowly withering away\u2014expiring or going unrenewed. Still, this week I did go through and tidy up the machines that are still alive. I originally wanted to run things with Docker, though looking closely, those VPSes seem to be OpenVZ or KVM, so I\'m not sure if Docker will run into issues."},{"id":"2021/07/05-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/07/05-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-07-05-01.md","title":"Recent Updates - Calibre-web & Navidrome","description":"Self-hosting Calibre-web and Navidrome with Docker","date":"2021-07-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Docker","permalink":"/en/tags/docker"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"}],"readingTime":2.06,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Recent Updates - Calibre-web & Navidrome","date":"2021-07-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Old-Blog","Docker","Linux","Server"],"slug":"2021/07/05-01","description":"Self-hosting Calibre-web and Navidrome with Docker"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"BuyVM","permalink":"/en/2021/07/08-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Upgrade ESXi Server","permalink":"/en/2021/06/26-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Introduction\\n\\nFollowing up on my previous server usage, I really started down the path of using Docker, and after checking out some `Self-hosted` services online, I picked two services to run:\\n\\n* ## <ruby>E-book Management System<rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>Calibre-Web</rt></ruby>\\n\\n* ## <ruby>Music Streaming<rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>Navidrome</rt></ruby>\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Markdown\\n\\nJumping off on a tangent to Markdown, which seemingly has nothing to do with the topic\u2014actually it does. Mainly, while organizing e-books recently, I saw a lot of cool text formatting, so I went looking up Markdown & HTML syntax. You can see those flashy colors and ruby annotations right above\u2014yep, that\'s my latest amusement.\\n\\n# Calibre-Web & Navidrome\\n\\nThe setup instructions are all in <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del><ruby><a href=\\"https://kiwi0093.github.io/\\">my notes</a><rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>Kiwi\'s Wiki</rt></ruby></del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [Kiwi Wiki](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs))*, so I won\'t repeat them here\u2014I\'ll mainly talk about the struggles along the way....\\n\\nFirst off, I wasn\'t very familiar with Photon OS, so just getting the hang of operating it took me <ruby>quite a bit of time<rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>reinstalling it many times</rt></ruby>. After that, I spent even more time getting used to Docker, eventually deciding that `docker-compose` is indeed much more convenient. Then came several days of scouring everywhere for EPUBs to fill up my library. Come to think of it, what took the most time wasn\'t setting up the service at all, but curating the content...\\n\\n# E-books & Kindle\\n\\nI have to say, collecting EPUB files of Japanese light novels really relies on the enthusiasm and sharing of fellow fans from <ruby><del>the great motherland aka. the Celestial Empire</del><rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>our neighbor across the strait</rt></ruby>. Searching around, almost all the books I found were in Simplified Chinese and hosted on Simplified Chinese websites. It really makes you feel that ACG culture across the strait is currently more thriving than on <ruby><del>this small, old, poor island</del><rt style={{color: \\"orange\\"}}>our great Taiwan</rt></ruby> (though putting it bluntly, piracy is still rampant\u2014still, since I\'m grabbing these things myself, I shouldn\'t sit here spouting self-righteous nonsense...)."},{"id":"2021/06/26-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/06/26-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-06-26-01.md","title":"Upgrade ESXi Server","description":"Upgrading ESXi","date":"2021-06-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.65,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Upgrade ESXi Server","date":"2021-06-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Server","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/06/26-01","description":"Upgrading ESXi"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Recent Updates - Calibre-web & Navidrome","permalink":"/en/2021/07/05-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Few Recent Updates: tmux, Nextcloud","permalink":"/en/2021/06/24-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Fleet Upgrade Plan\\n\\nEver since returning to Taiwan, the ongoing maintenance and upgrades for my home servers haven\'t stopped, and it\'s finally time to upgrade ESXi on the main server...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Upgrading ESXi\\n\\n## References\\n\\nThis upgrade mainly referenced [Master\'s experience](https://roidintw.kaienroid.com/2020/09/vmware-esxi-upgrade-from-60-to-65-with.html).\\n\\nThough he lived dangerously and ran it without entering maintenance mode\u2014I at least entered maintenance mode before executing it.\\n\\n## Getting the Files\\n\\nFirst, log into your My VMware account and look for the VMware vSphere Hypervisor download. Although 6.X currently has 6.7 out, all I saw were ISOs, so I just downloaded the 6.5 **VMware vSphere Hypervisor (ESXi) Offline Bundle**.\\n\\nIt\'s a zip file; once downloaded, just drop it into ESXi\'s datastore.\\n\\n## Upgrade Sequence\\n\\n### Maintenance Mode\\n\\nThis part is easy: connect to ESXi using the existing client and select Maintenance Mode.\\n\\n### Verify File Location\\n\\nUse PuTTY to SSH into your ESXi host:\\n\\n```bash\\nls /vmfs/volume/\\n```\\n\\nYou\'ll see your datastore, and the ZIP file you just uploaded will be inside.\\n\\n### Verify the Profile Image\\n\\n```bash\\nesxcli software sources profile list -d /vmfs/volumes/$(your_volume)/$(your_file.zip)\\n```\\n\\nThis lets you see its profile image.\\n\\n### Upgrade\\n\\n```bash\\nesxcli software profile update -d /vmfs/volumes/$(your_volume)/$(your_file.zip) -p $(profile-image)\\n```\\n\\nIt will start installing a bunch of VIBs and then prompt for a reboot (this part is annoying because its shell wouldn\'t accept `reboot` or `shutdown` commands, so I went to the client UI and clicked Reboot Host).\\n\\n### After the Upgrade Reboot\\n\\nLog in through the web interface instead, remember to exit Maintenance Mode, and then power back on whichever VMs need to be running.\\n\\n# Follow-up Plans\\n\\nThe main reason for this wave of upgrades is that I want to start migrating several services at home from standalone Linux VMs to running Docker directly on Photon OS, which saves both disk space and machine performance.\\n\\nSecond, it also cuts down on the Linux installation steps and tuning required just to run a service.\\n\\nSo there are several areas I\'ll need to spend time learning next:\\n\\n* Using Photon OS\\n  * Configuring multiple IPs on Photon OS\\n  * Changing the shell and installing/tuning related tools on Photon OS (after all this, I still end up jumping from Arch to Photon OS)\\n* Docker usage\\n  * Basic syntax\\n  * Practical ways to leverage Docker\\n* Which services I actually want to consolidate and run at home\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nActually, that last question is the biggest one. I set up Nextcloud recently, but it feels pretty underwhelming\u2014not sure I\'ll get much use out of it. I thought about setting up Airsonic for music streaming, yet I don\'t feel much motivation for it (mainly because while Nextcloud Music works, it feels sluggish right away). Or maybe setting up Emby (also feels unnecessary since most of the time I just go straight to streaming platforms). Setting up Calibre-web sounds a bit more promising, though that also involves organizing a huge pile of e-books... just thinking about it makes me tired..."},{"id":"2021/06/24-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/06/24-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-06-24-01.md","title":"A Few Recent Updates: tmux, Nextcloud","description":"Leisure activities of a time-thief during WFH","date":"2021-06-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"}],"readingTime":2.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"A Few Recent Updates: tmux, Nextcloud","date":"2021-06-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Old-Blog","Linux","Server"],"slug":"2021/06/24-01","description":"Leisure activities of a time-thief during WFH"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Upgrade ESXi Server","permalink":"/en/2021/06/26-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casual Chat","permalink":"/en/2021/05/26-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# WFH = \\\\ = WTF\\n\\nSince I\'ve been working from home lately, I can spend a bit of time pretending to work while actually messing around with some fun toys.\\n\\nSo I went ahead and tidied up my home server even further (previously it was just an emergency recovery; now it\'s entertainment disguised as expansion).\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Tmux\\n\\nHonestly, I discovered this thing while watching YouTube (see how slacker I am\u2014I had to browse YouTube just to learn `tmux` exists).\\n\\nNow it has become part of my standard setup (replacing `screen`).\\n\\nThis thing supports powerline, though it\'s pretty pointless since powerline only really works in an X environment (at least I haven\'t found a way for the console to render the relevant fonts properly).\\n\\nStill, even saying that, its features are slightly stronger than `screen`, so I\'ve started transitioning over to `tmux`.\\n\\n## Nextcloud\\n\\nThis one is a bit of reheating old leftovers... I actually played with it back when I was in Suzhou, though it wasn\'t very usable at the time (honestly, it hasn\'t gotten much better now either...).\\n\\nHowever, this time I did find Nextcloud Apps rather interesting.\\n\\n#### Nextcloud Music\\n\\nThe basic feature of this app is that you can play music in a Nextcloud folder directly from a web browser, and after building it into a media library, you can stream it out as well.\\n\\nPaired with Subsonic-series apps (like [Ultrasonic](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=org.moire.ultrasonic&hl=zh_TW&gl=US)), it can stream playback and even supports album covers and such\u2014making it a media server you can use even without Emby.\\n\\n(Though if this turns out to be the only useful feature left, I\'ll probably still go find another streaming server to use.)\\n\\n#### Nextcloud Deck\\n\\nThis is a Kanban/Deck app. Truth be told, I only use it because there isn\'t really a Google alternative (actually, I just wanted a free Milanote to use...).\\n\\n## Docker\\n\\nThis isn\'t unfamiliar either, though honestly I never spent time properly using Docker because I\'ve always just spun up VMs directly on VMware ESXi, rarely running multiple Docker containers on a single machine.\\n\\nBut Docker is getting more and more convenient these days\u2014for many things you can just grab an image and spin it up, saving the hassle of installation and configuration.\\n\\n#### [Webtop](https://docs.linuxserver.io/images/docker-webtop)\\n\\nHonestly, this is something that looks cool and sounds cool, yet when it comes to actually using it I really can\'t think of a use case....\\n\\nPut simply, it lets you run a desktop environment remotely through a browser. Currently it supports Alpine/Ubuntu with Xfce/KDE/MATE/IceWM/i3/Openbox (no Manjaro, which I like... =-=!!).\\n\\nSounds pretty nice, right? Wait\u2014if you already have a browser to use, that means you\'re already in a GUI environment, and you\'re opening *another* DE inside it... Aside from setting it up so a locked-down company computer can escape through a browser to breathe the air of freedom, I really can\'t think of what else it\'s good for.\\n\\n\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nBasically, working from home gets pretty boring\u2014you can\'t play games, and you can\'t focus on work either, so you get bored enough to go look for other distractions to try out."},{"id":"2021/05/26-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2021/05/26-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2021-05-26-01.md","title":"Casual Chat","description":"Reflections on some recent life changes","date":"2021-05-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Casual Chat","date":"2021-05-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2021/05/26-01","description":"Reflections on some recent life changes"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A Few Recent Updates: tmux, Nextcloud","permalink":"/en/2021/06/24-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Fyde OS &....","permalink":"/en/2020/12/18-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nDue to family reasons, I ended my expat life wandering in Suzhou and returned to Taiwan to settle down and live a quiet life.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Recent Updates\\n\\nLately I\'ve basically been a couch potato. After returning to Taiwan and facing the baptism of raising kids, I basically have zero time to spend tons of hours playing with new toys like before (actually, it\'s also because at work I have to work seriously instead of writing blog posts on the clock like I used to....).\\n\\n### Server\\n\\nSince I\'m back in Taiwan, my focus naturally shifted from ~~wall-hopping~~ \\"scientific internet access\\" back in Suzhou to focusing purely on the services themselves without having to worry about the network environment. Consequently, the server OS naturally switched back from Arch Linux\u2014which is friendly to bypass tools\u2014to FreeBSD, which I\'ve used for ages but was a nightmare to deal with over on the mainland.\\n\\n##### Server OS\\n\\nThere\'s still a bit of a transition period, though when reinstalling I noticed it really is way easier to set up than Arch.\\n\\nJust boot from the disc, pick the items you want to install, and basically without needing to use your brain at all, you can finish the installation in no time.\\n\\nThen just drop the old server\'s config files back in:\\n\\n* /etc/rc.conf\\n* /usr/local/etc/screenrc\\n* /etc/csh.cshrc\\n* /etc/pf.conf\\n* /etc/ipfw.conf\\n\\nPut them back and reboot.... done.... seriously way too easy.\\n\\n##### ESXi\\n\\nDue to certain issues (yep, those twice-a-week power outages), one of my ESXi servers died...\\n\\nFortunately, because that machine had ESXi installed on a USB drive, I was able to reinstall it and get it back up and running very quickly.\\n\\nThis time I chose to switch from ESXi 5.1 to the ESXi 6.5U2 custom ISO for HPE (since my Gen8 can only run up to the 6.5 pre-Gen9 ISO).\\n\\nThe best part after switching is that I finally don\'t need to install a separate client anymore. Since the old vSphere Client only supported up to 6.0 and I couldn\'t find the installer anyway, I switched over to the HTML5 web client, which actually works pretty nicely.\\n\\nHowever, if you don\'t get passthrough configured right on the first try, you\'ll run into trouble... and have to reinstall (since I don\'t have the desktop client to modify it).\\n\\nCost me a whole morning....\\n\\n##### PCHome 24 ~~H~~ D\\n\\nDuring the process I discovered one of the HDDs in my NAS had died. In the past, that was just a matter of whipping out a credit card, ordering four HDDs, and waiting for them to arrive.\\n\\nUnfortunately, this time I ran into the once-in-a-decade \\"PCHome 24D\\" delay event, turning waiting for HDDs into the hardest, most time-consuming task of all (though in the end I switched to buying them on Momo....).\\n\\nThat\'s it for this boring status update."},{"id":"2020/12/18-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/18-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-18-01.md","title":"Fyde OS &....","description":"Additional ChromeOS testing","date":"2020-12-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Android","permalink":"/en/tags/android"},{"inline":true,"label":"Chromebook","permalink":"/en/tags/chromebook"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.75,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Fyde OS &....","date":"2020-12-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Android","Chromebook","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/18-01","description":"Additional ChromeOS testing"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casual Chat","permalink":"/en/2021/05/26-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bliss OS","permalink":"/en/2020/12/16-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nLast time, Bliss OS, Phoenix OS, and even vanilla Android-x86 all had issues where APKs couldn\'t be installed directly (mainly because the lack of Google signatures prevented installation). Although I looked at plenty of guides on using `adb` to install APKs, in actual testing Bliss OS on the Surface Pro 4 frequently suffered from touch panel failures and other issues... so I had to look for another way out...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Fyde OS\\n\\nBasically, this isn\'t Android\u2014it\'s a *ChromeOS* derivative created by a Chinese vendor. However, because ChromeOS can also install APKs to run Android apps, and installing it is simpler than installing regular ChromeOS\u2014plus it claims to have an image specifically tailored for the Surface Pro 4\u2014I downloaded it to give it a try.\\n\\n### Official Info\\n\\n[Official Website](https://fydeos.com/)\\n\\n### Installation Method\\n\\nJust download the image matching your device on the [Download Page](https://fydeos.com/download), flash it onto a USB drive using [Etcher](https://www.balena.io/etcher/), boot from the USB, and finish running the installer...\\n\\n### User Experience\\n\\nSo far I\'ve confirmed that ChromeOS apps can be installed, Google Play can be installed, and Android apps can be installed via Google Play / Wandoujia / standalone APKs. Basic drivers work fine too, and the owner of the machine is currently doing further testing (to see if it crashes easily or runs into any weird issues...). What\'s already known is that intense use of the touch panel causes the touch panel driver to crash and stop responding... So if you want to play Android games with this, just go get a Bluetooth controller...\\n\\n\\n\\n## Irresponsible Speculation\\n\\nBecause I saw `anbox_fyde` on FydeOS\'s GitHub project, I speculate that FydeOS uses Anbox to run its so-called Android subsystem. If that\'s the case, after a while I\'ll spin up a VM with Manjaro + Anbox to see if Linux + Anbox works just as well...\\n\\nIf so, maybe Ubuntu Touch + Anbox could be a great choice!?"},{"id":"2020/12/16-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/16-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-16-01.md","title":"Bliss OS","description":"Another Android x86 built in China","date":"2020-12-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Android","permalink":"/en/tags/android"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.19,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Bliss OS","date":"2020-12-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Android","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/16-01","description":"Another Android x86 built in China"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Fyde OS &....","permalink":"/en/2020/12/18-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phoenix OS","permalink":"/en/2020/12/14-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nLately I don\'t know what got into me, but I\'ve become obsessed with installing Android on a PC again...\\n\\nFollowing Phoenix OS, I found another Android x86 distribution that fits my expectations even better \u2014 Bliss OS.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## [Bliss OS](https://blissos.org/)\\n\\nThis is an open-source version of Android x86, which inherently appeals to my tastes much more than Phoenix OS from a mainland company (okay, I admit I\'m biased against mainland software...).\\n\\nEven better, this team uses a Surface as their development machine, so Surface compatibility is quite good.\\n\\n#### Base System\\n\\nLike others, it comes in both x86 and x64 builds, and generally the x86 versions are based on older Android releases. What\'s thoughtful about this system, though, is that when downloading, it asks about the specs of the machine you plan to install it on and helps you pick the right ISO version\u2014largely preventing the tragedy I ran into with Phoenix OS last time... (Though to be fair, before installing Phoenix OS I didn\'t know the T100 had so many limitations either...)\\n\\nThe current stable release is based on Android 9.\\n\\nIt installs super bare-bones and clean, which really wins me over.. (Yes! I do have this weird quirk.)\\n\\nThough in mainland China that might not necessarily be a good thing.. since the GFW is sitting right there....\\n\\n#### Linux Drivers for Surface\\n\\nBasically it directly uses the Linux Surface drivers developed by [jakeday\'s linux-surface GitHub project](https://github.com/jakeday/linux-surface/tree/master/firmware). The painful part, however, is that you can\'t just pack this firmware directly into the ISO and call it a day.\\n\\nInstead, you have to inject the files while the touchscreen isn\'t working yet and then reboot (speaking of which, thank goodness the Surface Pro 4 I tested on yesterday came with a full set of accessories like the dock and keyboard...).\\n\\n## Test Results\\n\\nFirst, true to a Surface Pro 4, the i5 CPU + 8GB of RAM runs very smoothly, and the installation went smoothly following the official website\'s guide (okay, nowadays I prefer to steer clear of machines that only support UEFI boot...).\\n\\nAlthough the boot screen is pretty ugly\u2014it\'d be nice to customize it later...\\n\\nHowever, optimization might still not be quite right: yesterday I found that if you boot with the dock attached, Wi-Fi doesn\'t work...\\n\\nI\'ll leave the remaining minor quirks for the actual owner of the machine to poke at as they play with it...."},{"id":"2020/12/14-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/14-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-14-01.md","title":"Phoenix OS","description":"A Chinese-developed Android x86 distribution","date":"2020-12-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Android","permalink":"/en/tags/android"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phoenix OS","date":"2020-12-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Android","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/14-01","description":"A Chinese-developed Android x86 distribution"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Bliss OS","permalink":"/en/2020/12/16-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Berwick Saga Again","permalink":"/en/2020/12/07-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nA colleague had an ASUS T100 they wanted to convert to Android for their kids to play with..\\n\\nAfter looking around, it seemed the Chinese-developed Phoenix OS (basically a customized Android x86) would best fit their needs...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## ASUS T100\\n\\nThis is an early ASUS 2-in-1 netbook with very bare-bones specs, from that era when Windows 7 was transitioning to Windows 8.1 and every manufacturer was cutting down CPUs & RAM to lower prices\\n\\n [Detailed Specs](https://www.asus.com/us/2-in-1-PCs/ASUS_Transformer_Book_T100_Chi/specifications/)\\n\\n- Intel\xae Bay Trail-T Quad Core Z3775 1.46 GHz ~ 2.39 GHz Processor\\n\\n- Memory\\n\\n  OnBoard Memory 2 GB\\n\\n- Display\\n\\n  10.1\\" 16:10 Full HD (1920x1200) LED Backlight Touchscreen LCD Panel\\n\\n- Graphic\\n\\n  Integrated Intel\xae HD Graphics\\n\\n- Storage\\n\\n  32GB eMMC\\n  64GB eMMC\\n\\n- Card Reader\\n\\n  card reader (Micro SD )\\n\\n- Camera\\n\\n  Front 2 MP and Rear 5 MP\\n\\n- Networking\\n\\n  Dual-band 802.11 a/b/g/n\\n  Built-in Bluetooth\u2122 V4.0+HS\\n\\n- Interface\\n\\n  1 x COMBO audio jack\\n  1 x Micro USB\\n  1 x micro HDMI\\n  1 x micro SDXC card reader\\n  1 x Volume up/down\\n\\n- Audio\\n\\n  Built-in Speakers And Array Microphone\\n\\n- Battery\\n\\n  31 Whrs\\n\\n- Dimensions\\n\\n  Tablet:\\n  10.1 x 6.9 x 0.3 inch (WxDxH)\\n\\n- Weight\\n\\n  Tablet:\\n  1.3 lbs (with Polymer Battery)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## [Phoenix OS](http://www.phoenixos.com/)\\n\\nThis is a Chinese-customized Android system based on [Android X86](https://www.android-x86.org/)\\n\\nIts highlights include a UI tailored for PC environments, wired Ethernet support, and a default installation method that lets you use an `.exe` file directly under Windows to set up a dual-boot\\n\\n\\n\\n## Conclusion\\n\\n#### Day 1 \\n\\n* Even when forcing USB boot on the ASUS T100, it just stops at\\n\\n  ```bash\\n  grub>\\n  ```\\n\\n  At this point you have to manually type this to boot\\n\\n  ```bash\\n  configfile /efi/boot/grub.cfg\\n  ```\\n\\n* If you choose install, the keyboard stops working as soon as it enters the HDD selection screen.. so you can\'t proceed to the next step\\n* If you try using the `.exe` installer inside Windows instead, no matter how you run it, it always fails to install...\\n\\n#### Day 2\\n\\nAfter tinkering for a whole day, I finally discovered that the T100 is actually an odd relic of a bygone era that boots with 32-bit UEFI...\\n\\nBecause of this, I had to go redownload and make a 32-bit Windows 10 installation USB to reinstall the OS I had messed up\\n\\nThen I downloaded the 32-bit version of Phoenix OS (based on Android 5)\\n\\nAfter reinstalling Windows 10 and running the `.exe` installer, pressing `esc` at boot brings up the menu where selecting Phoenix OS boots right into the newly installed Phoenix OS\\n\\nWhat? You ask why not just use the x86 ISO to install Phoenix OS by itself?\\n\\nI wanted to, too, but the darn keyboard still wouldn\'t work~~~~"},{"id":"2020/12/07-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/07-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-07-01.md","title":"Berwick Saga Again","description":"Berwick Saga, a Fire Emblem-like game that won my heart\u2014I still haven\'t played anything else that suits my taste better.","date":"2020-12-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Game","permalink":"/en/tags/game"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.72,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Berwick Saga Again","date":"2020-12-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Game","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/07-01","description":"Berwick Saga, a Fire Emblem-like game that won my heart\u2014I still haven\'t played anything else that suits my taste better."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phoenix OS","permalink":"/en/2020/12/14-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Reorganizing Wiki Data","permalink":"/en/2020/12/04-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was archived and reposted from the old site.\\n:::\\n\\n![Berwick Saga](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Berwick_Saga_boxart.png)\\n\\nA 2005 PS2 game - Berwick Saga. Recently dug it back out to replay it on an emulator.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Berwick Saga\\n\\nThis is a turn-based strategy game similar to Fire Emblem (since the creator originally worked on FE before leaving to make this series, which led to a whole mess of disputes with Nintendo\u2014won\'t go into that).\\n\\n[The Japanese Wikipedia page is here](https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%86%E3%82%A3%E3%82%A2%E3%83%AA%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B0%E3%82%B5%E3%83%BC%E3%82%AC%E3%82%B7%E3%83%AA%E3%83%BC%E3%82%BA_%E3%83%99%E3%83%AB%E3%82%A6%E3%82%A3%E3%83%83%E3%82%AF%E3%82%B5%E3%83%BC%E3%82%AC)\\n\\nFor me, what makes it so fun isn\'t just the cavalry system where **_horses are consumables_**, but also the fact that this is a game where **_even the protagonist rides a horse_** and everyone goes full cavalry ~~high-speed movement~~ together (alright, it\'s weird little quirks like this that hit my sweet spot), plus it has PS2-era graphics (though playing it now, it really does look pretty bare-bones~~).\\n\\nAfter finishing this playthrough, I\'m planning to go back and replay the PS1 predecessor - **TearRing Saga: Utna Heroes Saga**. Apparently that one has a dual-protagonist system.\\n\\n## PCSX2\\n\\n[Official Link is here](https://pcsx2.net/)\\n\\nHonestly, this is a very powerful emulator... intuitive and smooth to use, and it even has a built-in cheat code system. Really great for a casual player like me to revisit PS2 games.\\n\\n\\n\\n## Conclusion\\n\\nFor the next little while, whenever I get back to my room, I\'ll probably be glued to the PC playing this... Let\'s see what other game catches my eye after a while...."},{"id":"2020/12/04-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/04-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-04-01.md","title":"Reorganizing Wiki Data","description":"Major overhaul of the Arch Linux section in the Wiki","date":"2020-12-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.31,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Reorganizing Wiki Data","date":"2020-12-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/04-01","description":"Major overhaul of the Arch Linux section in the Wiki"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Berwick Saga Again","permalink":"/en/2020/12/07-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Verbose Complaints About Arch Linux Automated Installation","permalink":"/en/2020/12/03-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nToday I kept obsessing over my Arch Linux installation script, and as I tinkered with it, I ended up rewriting almost everything in that section of the Wiki....\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n### Story Outline\\n\\nTo make that very personal script at least a tiny bit more general-purpose, I rewrote almost that entire part today..\\n\\n### Key Points (Without the Fluff)\\n\\nSimply put, I applied the `case` loop I learned yesterday and merged what used to look like several fragmented installation scripts into one where the user only has to type a single command and pick from a menu to finish installing any of my preset setups:\\n\\n* Plain Arch Linux - Honestly, this setup will probably be the script I use most often for testing things.. because nothing extra is installed; it\'s just something that boots up and has networking.\\n* Arch Linux with Nextcloud pre-installed - This one will probably see the least use, and its contents differ the most from the other scripts... (and it\'ll likely be the very last one I finish\u2014~~assuming I don\'t get lazy and just cut this option altogether~~)\\n* V2ray Server - This is basically dedicated to running an Arch Linux-based V2ray server at home in Taiwan, because I haven\'t seen a single VPS provider that offers Arch ~~and even if there were, I wouldn\'t pay extra for a pricier VPS just for Arch..~~\\n* V2ray Gateway - This is basically the prototype of the Gateway currently running in my room in Suzhou. The basic framework is all ready; all that\'s missing are the V2ray and `iptables` configs to make it identical.\\n* V2ray Gateway ver.K - This is the most contradictory one. This option completely reproduces my currently active Arch Linux Gateway with V2ray/iptables configs. You could say the whole core reason I wrote this auto-install script was for it.. but..... since the way the Wiki and scripts are hosted right now basically lays everything bare for anyone to see, there\'s no way I\'m going to publish my live production configs for anyone to copy... so this one will be a bit awkward.. I\'ll need to use encryption/decryption to restore the config files.\\n\\n### Thoughts\\n\\nI think one sentence sums it up:\\n\\n> I really do have way too much free time on my hands, turning a secret script into a complicated public version...\\n>\\n> Plus, as I kept writing, every single page got way too long, so I just chopped them all up into lots of small files...\\n\\nMaybe I really do come across as having it too easy....\\n\\nAlright, it\'s six o\'clock\u2014time for this time-theft slacker to clock out~~ bye everyone~~"},{"id":"2020/12/03-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/03-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-03-01.md","title":"Verbose Complaints About Arch Linux Automated Installation","description":"Personal struggles and ranting before the existence of archinstall","date":"2020-12-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.54,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Verbose Complaints About Arch Linux Automated Installation","date":"2020-12-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/03-01","description":"Personal struggles and ranting before the existence of archinstall"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Reorganizing Wiki Data","permalink":"/en/2020/12/04-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Intermittent Fasting","permalink":"/en/2020/12/03-02"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article is republished from archive of the old blog.\\n:::\\n\\nAlthough the Wiki contains a bunch of scripts for automated Arch Linux installation, I only started *seriously* verifying and supplementing them yesterday, and afterwards I wanted to punch myself...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Preface\\n\\nFirst of all, this blog post is not a technical supplement, but a pure ranting **_deep palace resentful wife article_**. Friends hoping to find valuable information here are kindly advised to turn left and head to <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[Wiki](https://kiwi0093.github.io/Wiki-site/wiki/Linux/Arch%20Linux%20&%20Manjaro/Archlinux%20Gateway%E8%87%Async%E5%AE%89%E8%A3%9D/1-Arch_linux_Gateway_installation/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [Wiki](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs/HomeLab/Linux/Archive/ArchInstallScript/w-0024))* which might actually be helpful to you.\\n\\n## All Complaints Below\\n\\n* `https://raw.githubusercontent.com` is blocked\\n\\n  I really want to curse... Putting things on GitHub was supposed to ensure that ~~the Great Firewall~~ the Celestial Empire wouldn\'t target GitHub, making it very convenient to use, but alas... the Celestial Empire still delivered a DNS pollution strike.. truly frustrating.\\n\\n* `pacstrap /mnt base` did not install the kernel\\n\\n  Looking at the new [Archlinux Installation Guide](https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/installation_guide), it says running `mkinitcpio` is not needed, which is true, ~~but it didn\'t mention that running `pacstrap` wouldn\'t conveniently install the kernel package along with it, so `mkinitcpio` naturally wouldn\'t run, and `grub-mkconfig` wouldn\'t build the boot menu either...~~ and booting turns into a tragedy....\\n\\n* Correction: Well, I was just being blind. The latest Installation Guide explicitly requires:\\n\\n  ```bash\\n  pacstrap /mnt base linux linux-firmware\\n  ```\\n\\n  This was my own foolishness.. but I\'m still going to complain anyway...\\n\\n* `netctl` became something that needs to be installed separately\\n\\n  Actually, I forgot whether it needed separate installation before. The original default network interface configuration instructions used `netctl`, but now it has changed to the `ip` command, though it can still be installed as a package and used.\\n\\n* `pacman-mirrors` is surprisingly not an Arch Linux package\\n\\n  Since I like using Manjaro for Linux Desktop, and Manjaro is almost identical to Arch Linux (well, aside from the logo, there are some differences), I didn\'t expect that following `yay`, there would be packages not found in Arch Linux... So during installation, I could only use `sed` commands to filter out desired locations in the mirrorlist... Let me check later if AUR supports installing it...\\n\\n* Postscript: Checked Arch\'s AUR, `pacman-mirrors` is not in there... (crying)\\n\\n## After the Complaints\\n\\nAlthough I love Arch, thinking about it carefully, I actually love Manjaro....\\n\\nI would probably only use Arch Linux on servers or gateways... though it seems possible to use Manjaro and directly install a GUI-less version for server use...\\n\\nHowever, I am a bit picky, and my requirements for servers and desktops differ. Servers are best kept as clean and minimalist as possible, while desktops prioritize convenience as long as they remain within the Arch family..."},{"id":"2020/12/03-02","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/03-02","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-03-02.md","title":"Intermittent Fasting","description":"A method picked up online when I desperately needed to lose weight: genuinely effective, though easy to break","date":"2020-12-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":2.06,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Intermittent Fasting","date":"2020-12-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/03-02","description":"A method picked up online when I desperately needed to lose weight: genuinely effective, though easy to break"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Verbose Complaints About Arch Linux Automated Installation","permalink":"/en/2020/12/03-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"The Ladder, Part II...","permalink":"/en/2020/12/01-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n[![Dr. Berg\'s Fasting Explanation](http://img.youtube.com/vi/SlzBMJvtGHo/0.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SlzBMJvtGHo \\"Bodily Changes During Fasting\\")\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Preface\\n\\nAs a lifelong chubby guy, after being hospitalized for high blood pressure earlier this year, and thinking about my wife and kids, I figured I really ought to take losing weight seriously to prolong my life. After searching online for weight-loss methods, I found that over the past year or two, a ton of people have been promoting **_intermittent fasting_** for weight loss.\\n\\n## Staged Fasting\\n\\n> **Intermittent fasting involves strategically eating properly during the *eating* window and consuming zero calories during the *fasting* window, letting insulin rest while glucagon kicks in to break down fat.**\\n\\n## Personal Experience\\n\\nIn my own case, after getting discharged from the hospital, I practiced a very-low-calorie diet, a low-carb diet, and 16/8 intermittent fasting. I once brought my weight down from 112.7 kg to 98 kg, and at the time it looked like it would keep dropping steadily..\\n\\nHowever, I came back to Taiwan for a vacation, and between hotel quarantine / foodpanda / and the not-so-desperate efforts of the mom who spent nearly thirty years raising me into this body shape, my weight started rebounding rapidly. Then... I returned to Taiwan once again for the birth of my daughter, and my weight went right back up to 109 kg and stubbornly refused to budge.\\n\\nI\'ve basically been doing 16/8 intermittent fasting consistently, but maybe my physical condition really isn\'t great, and I eat too much during the eating window, so it\'s been hard to see sustained, effective fat loss. That\'s why I recently switched to the much more aggressive 23/1 intermittent fasting. On day one, it started dropping\u2014from 109.6 kg \u2192 107.8 kg. Of course, day one isn\'t reliable, so I plan to monitor it for at least a week to see how it goes.\\n\\n## Conclusion\\n\\nSo far, intermittent fasting seems to be the most effective method I\'ve tried. The main challenge going forward will be figuring out how to integrate it seamlessly into daily life\u2014after all, the last time I broke my streak was due to a drastic change in lifestyle (was quarantine the main culprit for gaining weight?? Or was gorging myself during quarantine the main culprit??)"},{"id":"2020/12/01-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/12/01-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-12-01-01.md","title":"The Ladder, Part II...","description":"Switching V2Ray to VLESS and its extensions","date":"2020-12-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"V2ray","permalink":"/en/tags/v-2-ray"},{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/en/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":3.12,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The Ladder, Part II...","date":"2020-12-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["V2ray","VPN","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/12/01-01","description":"Switching V2Ray to VLESS and its extensions"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Intermittent Fasting","permalink":"/en/2020/12/03-02"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ladders and Their Accessories","permalink":"/en/2020/11/27-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was migrated from the old blog archive.\\n:::\\n\\n## Current Situation\\n\\nAfter getting talked into buying several new RackNerd VPSs over the last few days, I really got to practice installing V2ray 4.33 (as of today, I\'ve set up four new ones plus two updates). However, besides the machines under my own name, looking at my DNS list in CF... there are still other people\'s machines under my DN that I really ought to help upgrade...\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n\\nBecause the annoying part is:\\n\\n## *Not every 4.3x client is compatible with a 4.2x server*\\n\\nSo you have to upgrade the clients along with the server all at once, otherwise things easily end in tragedy...\\n\\n## Upgrading\\n\\nHonestly, given my usual lazy habits, it\'s just a VPS with nothing important on it anyway... backing up the contents of **V2Ray**\'s **config.json** and just hitting **reinstall** to start fresh would probably save more effort... Still, since there are VPSs I can\'t just **reinstall** at will, let\'s do the complicated upgrade method instead....\\n\\nWhy don\'t I copy the technical stuff over to the Wiki? Why would I put a one-off task that you can forget as soon as it\'s done into a Wiki??\\n\\n### Upgrade Details\\n\\nPut simply, the workflow is:\\n\\n* Stop and disable the service in systemd\\n* Use `rm` to wipe out the previously installed binaries and related files\\n* Use `mv` to move the config file from the old default */etc/v2ray/* to */usr/local/etc/v2ray/*. As for why they changed the path? I guess maybe ~~they were charmed by FreeBSD~~ they wanted to separate v2ray configs from the system\'s main config directory\\n* Install using the new script (if you don\'t want to change the default path, you\'d have to manually edit the script, which makes putting it in crontab a pain~~)\\n* Enable `v2ray.service`\\n* Start `V2ray.service` and call it a day\\n\\nIf you want to switch to **VLESS**, you just add the v2ray account configuration, handle the certbot keys (or generate keys another way, though I don\'t know how yet), and finally update `config.json`.\\n\\nThe detailed steps are pretty clearly documented in the [official documentation](https://github.com/v2fly/fhs-install-v2ray/wiki/Migrate-from-the-old-script-to-this).\\n\\n#### If your server runs Arch Linux....\\n\\nThen all it takes is one line:\\n\\n```bash\\npacman -Syu\\n```\\n\\nAnd your V2ray upgrades to the latest version.... zero drama.. super effortless....\\n\\nI really hate how VPS providers never offer Arch as a default OS option... so annoying.\\n\\n## Client Selection\\n\\nAs for clients, honestly the one I use most heavily is V2ray on Arch Linux\u2014best compatibility (since it\'s the exact same binary as the server) and high stability. Still, as mentioned before, other clients need to be considered too.\\n\\n#### Android\\n\\nLately I\'ve really taken a liking to **V2RayNG**, though it\'s also the only client I ran into where the 4.33 core couldn\'t connect to a 4.23 server.... Between the previous release (1.33) and the newly updated version, there\'s a real difference in VLESS support and speed. In the future, whenever the server\'s V2Ray-Core gets upgraded, we might have to upgrade this immediately too for things to stay smooth..... (I hope it won\'t be like that...)\\n\\n#### Windows\\n\\nSince I use QV2Ray + V2Ray-Core on Linux Desktop, it works smoothly with no issues. On Windows, I ~~have to switch to something else~~ just use QV2ray as well... (this thing is cross-platform.. works on Windows/Linux/macOS).\\n\\nDownload links: [QV2Ray](https://github.com/Qv2ray/Qv2ray/releases), paired with [V2Ray-Core](https://github.com/v2fly/v2ray-core/releases).\\n\\n#### iOS\\n\\nFolks in the China region, just go find a kind-hearted soul\'s US region account to borrow...."},{"id":"2020/11/27-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/11/27-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-11-27-01.md","title":"Ladders and Their Accessories","description":"Still more V2Ray-related information","date":"2020-11-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"V2ray","permalink":"/en/tags/v-2-ray"},{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/en/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":8.34,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Ladders and Their Accessories","date":"2020-11-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["V2ray","VPN","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/11/27-01","description":"Still more V2Ray-related information"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The Ladder, Part II...","permalink":"/en/2020/12/01-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Note taking tools","permalink":"/en/2020/11/16-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nThe day before yesterday, I picked up another cheap VPS from **RackNerd**\'s Thanksgiving sale\u2014getting two years of usage for around 18 USD.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## VLESS\\n\\nI mainly bought this one to test out VLESS configuration and usage, so as soon as I got it, I went straight to setting up VLESS. As usual, the detailed installation notes will go into the Wiki, so I won\'t specifically go into the installation and setup details here\u2014let\'s just talk about my initial impressions.\\n\\n### Motivation\\n\\nEven though I know VLESS was created because VMess has issues that make it 100% detectable, the \\"ladders\\" (proxies) I currently have all use the WS+TLS architecture, so they aren\'t actually that risky (and if that\'s considered risky, wouldn\'t VLESS be just as risky?). So it wasn\'t strictly necessary to switch to VLESS. Still, after seeing some recommendations from Chinese users saying VLESS performs much better (since removing all the encryption and authentication from the original VMess should theoretically make it faster),\\n\\nI couldn\'t resist... (a classic example of impulse buying).\\n\\n### Differences\\n\\nWhen using VMess+WS+TLS, to save myself the trouble, I basically always recommended using Caddy to handle the WS+TLS part. However, with VLESS, because TLS has to be wrapped inside V2Ray itself, you need to specify the path to the SSL key. That turns Caddy\'s convenient automatic SSL certificate handling into a bit of an issue (actually it shouldn\'t really be a problem\u2014it\'s just that because it\'s automatic, you still have to track down where it stores its keys and verify it yourself). On top of that, Caddy v2\'s configuration file became more complex compared to v1, and I\'m not as familiar with its configuration logic and documentation as I am with Nginx or Apache (at the end of the day, it still comes down to my own limitations...).\\n\\n### Testing Platform\\n\\nBecause Caddy was no longer needed, thanks to my bizarre habit, I naturally wanted to reinstall the entire OS instead of just uninstalling packages (I guess to some extent this is a bad habit spoiled by VMs\u2014whenever I\'m too lazy to make big changes, I just reinstall). So at first I started working on the Arch Linux machine at home that runs my backup V2Ray. Since I was also rewriting my Arch Linux automated install script recently, I got carried away and started debugging the script while reinstalling the system at the same time (speaking of which, I have to say it\'s really annoying that the installation process changed slightly again, forcing me to rewrite core parts of the script...). So the day before yesterday, most of my time was spent tweaking the script and reinstalling the OS...\\n\\nLater, when I got the RackNerd VPS, I took the opportunity to deploy directly on it. These VPS providers basically only offer Debian/Ubuntu or CentOS-based distros, and none of the Arch Linux I like to use...\\n\\nAfter looking at some tutorials online, I felt using Arch Linux might actually be simpler\u2014at the very least, `pacman` can handle the installation without needing to run an external script, and you don\'t need to edit `crontab` for auto-updates (speaking of which, auto-updates on my other VPSes basically all broke because the install script path changed).\\n\\n### Server-Side Thoughts\\n\\nBecause V2Ray can handle the WS+TLS part by itself, I initially thought naively that I wouldn\'t need to install Nginx or Apache during setup. However, thinking about it afterward, it\'s still easier to handle SSL keys with Certbot if a web server is installed, so I installed one anyway...\\n\\nOverall, though, the fact that once V2Ray is configured you can run WebSocket without even starting Nginx/Apache is pretty convenient\u2014it saves you from configuring an extra web server. That said, in the end you probably still need to set up the web server anyway, otherwise its fallback won\'t work properly.\\n\\nYou can see this trend aligns with the V2Ray team\'s vision: from the beginning, V2Ray never intended to be just a SOCKS server. What they want is more like a comprehensive, extensible network management Swiss Army knife\u2014with DNS, routing tables, communication protocols, encryption, and so on. If one day it includes a full web server module rather than just WS functionality, I probably wouldn\'t be surprised at all.\\n\\nRight now it\'s set up with VLESS+WS+TLS in the most minimal way following a template. Once the XTLS licensing issue is sorted out later, I might also try VLESS+XTLS to see if it\'s really as powerful as people say (everyone says it\'s as fast as running unencrypted...).\\n\\n### Client-Side Thoughts\\n\\n##### Gateway V2ray client\\n\\nYep, I switched my room\'s Gateway over to the VPS running VLESS+WS+TLS. It seems to work pretty normally. As for speed, it\'s probably bottlenecked by China Unicom\'s terrible network, so I can\'t tell at all whether it got faster... (so basically, my original goal wasn\'t achieved at all\u2014well, comparing speed and efficiency without a decent network route is kind of a joke anyway, right??)\\n\\nRight now I\'m basically doing stability testing to see if there are any surprises over the next few days (hopefully pleasant ones, not scary ones).\\n\\n##### Linux Client - QV2Ray+V2ray\\n\\nBecause my Linux Desktop (the one I\'m writing this blog post on right now) runs Manjaro, installing V2Ray is super easy, which makes me very grateful to the Arch team.\\n\\nAs for how it feels to use... well, I can only say I only tried it for a short moment before stopping, mainly because there\'s currently no WiFi at the office to connect to (so this post probably won\'t be published on time on the day I wrote it).\\n\\nHowever, it seemed to perform about the same as the Gateway\u2014no noticeable difference, probably still bottlenecked by the network route.\\n\\n##### Android Client - V2RayNG\\n\\nSince there aren\'t many clients that support VLESS yet, I had no choice but to newly install **V2RayNG** on my phone. I have to say, it feels more stable and nicer to use than **BifrostV**\u2014honestly, this app is the main reason I wanted to write this post (wait, so everything before this was just filler?!).\\n\\n###### Pros (Very Subjective)\\n\\n* QR code / barcode compatible with QV2Ray\\n\\n  First, this app supports the same barcode format as **Qv2ray**, which follows the \\"standard specification.\\" Compared to **BifrostV**, which uses its own proprietary format, this is much more convenient to use. That said, it\'s not that **BifrostV** was being stubborn, rather that there wasn\'t a clearly defined V2Ray link barcode standard earlier\u2014but either way, being able to use the same barcode format is definitely much nicer.\\n\\n* No ads, and a GUI to my taste\\n\\n  Although I\'m not completely opposed to free apps having ads, having a clean app without unnecessary ads really makes you feel happy.\\n\\n  Maybe it\'s because the **Shadowsocks** app left such a good impression on me earlier\u2014its clean GUI style, responsiveness, and being the first app that let me reliably climb over the wall\u2014that I have a really high affinity for **V2RayNG**\'s interface, which looks a lot like **shadowsocks**.\\n\\n* Fast response and high stability\\n\\n  When I first switched to **BifrostV** for V2Ray, I often felt its responsiveness and stability weren\'t as good as **shadowsocks**\u2014it would frequently hang upon opening, or randomly disconnect mid-session, and things like that. At the time, though, I felt there wasn\'t any other alternative that covered so many protocols, so I just put up with it\u2014until I switched to **V2RayNG** for VLESS, and everything changed...\\n\\n  It brought back that feeling of using **shadowsocks** again. Though honestly, since I rarely wander around outside, I don\'t actually rely on proxy apps on my phone or tablet all that heavily\u2014and once I\'m back in my room, the WiFi is already behind a Gateway that handles the proxy, so I basically don\'t need to proxy again on the device...\\n\\n###### Cons\\n\\n* DNS issues\\n\\n  I\'m not sure whether it\'s a VLESS issue or a **V2RayNG** issue, but it doesn\'t handle DNS poisoning very well. With the exact same settings, connecting to VMess+WS+TLS works completely fine, but as soon as I connect to VLESS+WS+TLS, DNS errors occur and I can\'t connect. After randomly trying things out, only the following settings worked properly:\\n\\n  - Domain Strategy - Either `IPIfNonMatch` or `IPOnDemand` works\\n  - Predefined Rules - Bypass LAN and Mainland China\\n  - Enable Local DNS - Must NOT be enabled\\n  - Remote DNS - Change to Google\'s `8.8.8.8`\\n\\n  That\'s how it is for now, though logically there shouldn\'t be an issue. Maybe once I figure out what\'s causing it later, I\'ll come back and update this.\\n\\n## Conclusion\\n\\nIn short, proxy-related tech is probably something that only people living in ~~the Great Power~~ the Celestial Empire ever bother learning. Just like my master said, \\"That\'s a very niche skill tree\u2014among this group, probably only you who\'s stationed over there would put points into it.\\" So I could only dig through online resources, pick up a little superficial knowledge, and embarrass myself by sharing it here.\\n\\nFortunately, there are still plenty of resourceful folks in the Celestial Empire, so related info is still easy to find\u2014well, yes... it\'s easy to find once you\'ve scaled the wall and can use Google, whereas inside the wall you can\'t find a single article...\\n\\nSo when it comes down to it, a \\"ladder\\" is really just something where you need a tool to get over the wall the first time. Once you have that, building any other ladder afterward is relatively easy...\\n\\nThat said, building ladders is inherently a small act of defiance against the Celestial Empire\'s state apparatus\u2014it requires constant updates and refinement. If you truly want a permanent solution once and for all,\\n\\nphysically getting yourself out (\\"emigrating in person\\") is still the most practical option."},{"id":"2020/11/16-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/11/16-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-11-16-01.md","title":"Note taking tools","description":"Tools for taking notes","date":"2020-11-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"},{"inline":true,"label":"Tools","permalink":"/en/tags/tools"},{"inline":true,"label":"web","permalink":"/en/tags/web"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":1.21,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Note taking tools","date":"2020-11-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Life","Tools","web","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/11/16-01","description":"Tools for taking notes"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ladders and Their Accessories","permalink":"/en/2020/11/27-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Reopening! Major Revamp!","permalink":"/en/2020/11/15-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nNow that the Blog & Wiki are set up, it\'s time to start building a habit of writing regularly, as well as collecting related tools and refining how I use them.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\nAs for the tools I\'m using, I already have Typora\u2014a nice-looking, handy, cross-platform Markdown editor. I\'ll create entries for the finer details in the Wiki later (am I digging myself a hole here?).\\n\\nStill, it always felt like something was missing. After searching around (for about five minutes...), I found a Chrome extension web clipper:\\n\\n#### [MaoXian Web Clipper](https://mika-cn.github.io/maoxian-web-clipper/index-zh-CN.html): [Chrome Web Store Link](https://chrome.google.com/webstore/detail/maoxian-web-clipper/kjahokgdcbohofgdidndeiaigkehdjdc/related)\\n\\nHowever, I\'m still figuring things out myself\u2014beyond how to use the software itself, I also need to figure out how to mesh it with the habits I plan to build going forward.\\n\\nThis middle-aged guy is now a dad of two kids too (rambling starts.. =_=!).\\n\\nI\'d also like to show off in front of the kids someday and say, \\"Hey, your dad had good habits and built a personal knowledge base!\\" (puffing my chest out~~).\\n\\nSo while I\'m currently posted away from home on my own and have some spare time, I\'ll write as consistently as I can..."},{"id":"2020/11/15-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/11/15-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-11-15-01.md","title":"Reopening! Major Revamp!","description":"Simply an announcement about switching from Hugo to Hexo","date":"2020-11-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Archive","permalink":"/en/tags/archive"},{"inline":true,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life"}],"readingTime":1.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Reopening! Major Revamp!","date":"2020-11-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Old-Blog","Archive","Life"],"slug":"2020/11/15-01","description":"Simply an announcement about switching from Hugo to Hexo"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Note taking tools","permalink":"/en/2020/11/16-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Uncle\'s Ramblings - Windows & Linux Tool Inventory","permalink":"/en/2020/07/15-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article is reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\nOriginally, I wanted to use some Markdown note-taking software, such as [Obsidian](https://obsidian.md/), to take fragmented notes for collecting and organizing new things and new approaches I encounter later on.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\nHowever, after browsing around for a while, I felt that rather than using a local note-taking app, it would be better to build it in the form of a Wiki so I can easily look things up myself. As I kept exploring, I decided to switch my existing Hugo + GitHub Pages setup to Hexo + GitHub Pages paired with two different themes to create a new Blog + Wiki website.\\n\\nI\'m using GitHub Desktop so that Blog/Wiki articles can be conveniently synced and version-controlled through GitHub to achieve the desired workflow. For the detailed setup, you can refer to the already written <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[Wiki entry](https://kiwi0093.github.io/Wiki-site/wiki/Network/Hexo/1-introduce/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [Wiki entry](https://blog.kiwireich.com/docs/HomeLab/Network/Archive/w-0077))*.\\n\\nNext, I\'ll probably set up a web page clipping/recording feature to save things I come across, and slowly get used to writing short, fragmented Markdown posts to build a personal knowledge base of my own."},{"id":"2020/07/15-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/07/15-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-07-15-01.md","title":"Uncle\'s Ramblings - Windows & Linux Tool Inventory","description":"An inventory of the Windows and Linux tools I used in 2020","date":"2020-07-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Windows","permalink":"/en/tags/windows"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":15.8,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Uncle\'s Ramblings - Windows & Linux Tool Inventory","date":"2020-07-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Linux","Windows","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/07/15-01","description":"An inventory of the Windows and Linux tools I used in 2020"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Reopening! Major Revamp!","permalink":"/en/2020/11/15-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"An Uncle\'s Ramblings - Basic Git Usage & Essential Tools for Blogging","permalink":"/en/2020/07/11-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was archived from the old site.\\n:::\\n\\n# Foreword\\n\\nAfter years of using computers, the software I rely on has gradually changed over time. Looking back at the posts I wrote earlier about installing and using Manjaro or Linux gaming, I started thinking about writing a ~~hodgepodge~~ consolidated post to document the software I\'m currently using\u2014or have used for years without getting tired of\u2014along with my preferred setup methods, as a reference for anyone interested.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Basic Premises\\n\\nStarting this year, I\'ve gradually gotten used to working across multiple platforms (mainly Windows and Linux desktops), so when choosing software, I try to prioritize the following characteristics:\\n\\n* Freeware or Open-Source software\\n* Cross-platform support\\n* Ideally able to sync with each other, so switching platforms or devices doesn\'t interrupt my workflow\\n* Able to operate without depending on an internet connection\\n\\nBasically, these points help me avoid pirated software as much as possible and reduce reliance on web apps (such as tons of Chrome extensions).\\n\\n# Desktop Environment\\n\\n## Editor/Coding/Note tools\\n\\n### Markdown Editor\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Typora.jpg\\" alt=\\"Typora\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Typora](https://typora.io/) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade typora -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\n#None root account & yay installed\\nyay -S typora\\n```\\n\\nYou only realize how amazingly good this is once you\'ve used it. As I mentioned in <span style={{color: \\"#e06c75\\"}}><del>[another post](https://kiwi0093.github.io/post/git/)</del></span> *(Updated 2026-11-27: [another post](https://blog.kiwireich.com/2020/07/11-01))*, it can also integrate with GitHub as an image host to automatically upload images. Once you get used to the hotkeys, writing blog posts with it is super productive\u2014definitely a Markdown editor worth recommending.\\n\\nBy the way, as a quick note, if you want to embed a YouTube video in Markdown, [do it like this](https://gist.github.com/billthelizard/a632b6a6a79839cba1c0fd67f64b87ff):\\n\\n```r\\n\u57FA\u672C\u8A9E\u6CD5\\n[![alt text](http://example.com/exampl.png)](http://example.com/link \\"title\\")\\n\\n\u5C01\u9762\u7684\u5716\\nhttp://img.youtube.com/vi/{video-id}/0.jpg\\n\\n\u6240\u4EE5\u7BC4\u4F8B\u5C31\u662F\\n[![Audi R8](http://img.youtube.com/vi/KOxbO0EI4MA/0.jpg)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KOxbO0EI4MA \\"Audi R8\\")\\n```\\n\\nHere, *KOxbO0EI4MA* is the Video ID, which you can find from the video URL\u2014though playlists don\'t have cover thumbnails.\\n\\n### Editor for coding\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/notepad.png\\" alt=\\"Notepad\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Notepad++](https://notepad-plus-plus.org/downloads/) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade notepadplusplus -y\\n```\\n\\nThis editor is pretty handy on Windows. I mainly use it to edit HOI4 files (a prime example of slacking off from real work). Unfortunately, it\'s Windows-only; you can install it via choco, download and run the installer, or even run it as a portable app.\\n\\n### Note\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Notion_app_logo.png\\" alt=\\"Notion\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Notion](https://www.notion.so/) - Browser based\\n\\n* Windows & Linux & macOS & any device with a browser\\n\\n  Use your favorite browser and [click here](https://www.notion.so/).\\n\\n* iOS\\n\\n  Search for [Notion on the App Store](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/notion-notes-projects-docs/id1232780281).\\n\\n* Android\\n\\n    <a href=\\"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=notion.id\\"><img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/google-play-badge.png\\" alt=\\"google-play-badge\\" style={{ width: \\"150px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} /></a>\\n\\n  Ever since Notion changed its free-tier block limit to unlimited, it instantly became an absolute powerhouse. For anyone in the habit of taking notes, it\'s basically a must-sign-up (though I switched my note-taking style to blogging, so I don\'t use it much myself).\\n\\n  Fun fact: I actually wanted to learn Markdown only after watching Notion promo videos\u2014that shows just how tempting it is to use. Here\'s a related YouTube playlist link too; friends behind the Great Firewall can just pretend they didn\'t see it.\\n\\n  <a href=\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kI1JQaNpBks&amp;list=PLC5c1koJaYqucZwMGChO36IRPzOxXhXcn\\"><img src=\\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/8B23t_jqySU/0.jpg\\" alt=\\"Notion guide\\" style={{ width: \\"300px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} /></a>\\n  Note: YouTube Video - Hold Ctrl + Left Click to open in new window\\n\\n## Multimedia\\n\\n### Player - Video\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/k-lite-codec-pack-logo.png\\" alt=\\"K-Lite\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[K-lite codepack](https://codecguide.com/download_kl.htm) - Windows\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade k-litecodecpackfull -y\\nor\\nchoco upgrade k-litecodecpackmega -y\\nor\\nchoco upgrade k-litecodecpackbasic -y\\n```\\n\\n\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/vlc.jpg\\" alt=\\"VLC\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[VLC](https://www.videolan.org/vlc/index.html) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade vlc -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\nBuilt-in; on other distros you can easily install it with your package manager, or even through a GUI software center.\\n\\n### Player - Audio\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Foobar2000_logo.png\\" alt=\\"foobar2000\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[foobar2000](https://www.foobar2000.org/) - Windows\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade foobar2000 -y\\n```\\n\\nFor this program, though, I actually recommend not using the installer version\u2014use the portable version instead. The main reason is that with the installed version you have to tweak the UI every time, and honestly its software updates aren\'t really necessary since almost all its codecs rely on plugins, and those plugins barely ever update...\\n\\nWhen using it, remember to pair it with the [Lame MP3 Encoder](https://lame.sourceforge.io/) so you can easily convert and organize your music.\\n\\n## Game\\n\\n### Platform\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/steam.jpg\\" alt=\\"steam\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Steam](https://store.steampowered.com/) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade steam -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\nPre-installed out of the box\u2014no special tweaks needed.\\n\\n\\n\\nThis can only be called a godsend, especially on Linux where it is hands-down the single most important piece of gaming software.\\n\\nNow that I\'m older, instead of spending time and energy hunting down random ISOs or sketchy repacks online, it\'s much more practical and hassle-free to just whip out the credit card during a sale.\\n\\nWith games in your Steam library, not only can you wait until you want to play to install them, you can also verify file integrity to restore a heavily modded/broken game right back to a working state. Paired with Steam Workshop and Nexus Mods, the PC gaming experience and satisfaction (yeah, I\'m a modder + cheat lover, deal with it) are sky-high.\\n\\n\\n\\nOn top of that, Steam now has new features like Steam Link and Remote Play Together, which run smoothly on Linux too\u2014a great choice for Linux users following the teachings of AMD who want to stream games.\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/galaxy_logo.png\\" alt=\\"GOG Glaxy\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[GOG Galaxy](https://www.gog.com/galaxy) - Windows\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade goggalaxy -y\\n```\\n\\nThis is GOG\'s version of a ~~Steam~~ game download/install manager. GOG\'s biggest selling point is being DRM-free. Most casual players might not notice much difference, but simply put, the copy protection isn\'t as strict, making it super convenient for playing old games (it\'s literally called Good Old Games, after all).\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/lutris_logo.jpg\\" alt=\\"Lutris\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Lutris](https://lutris.net/) - Linux\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nsudo pacman -Sy lutris\\n```\\n\\nAlthough Lutris is also an awesome tool\u2014since it gathers tons of game install scripts written by community wizards and automates them (though I haven\'t personally succeeded in running any games with it yet)\u2014because a lot of it is built on Wine (yes, all sorts of different Wine versions and configurations), you have to get your hardware setup right beforehand. And when running it, in classic Linux fashion, you still need to keep an eye out for how to troubleshoot issues if something goes wrong. [Here is a simple Manjaro Lutris Install Guide](https://forum.manjaro.org/t/manjaro-specific-lutris-install-guide/143943). To sum up the key takeaways:\\n\\n1. If you use Linux, buy an AMD CPU & GPU\u2014Nvidia will make you question your life choices.\\n2. If your game is on Steam, just use Steam, kid. Gaming is supposed to be fun; don\'t torture yourself over non-game headaches.\\n\\n### GameSteam\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/stream_logo.jpg\\" alt=\\"moonlight\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Moonlight - Steam](https://moonlight-stream.org/) - Windows & Linux & macOS & Android & iOS\\n\\nAt the very bottom of [the link above](https://moonlight-stream.org/), there are clean and complete installation links, so I\'ll save some words here.\\n\\nThis is basically a third-party Nvidia Shield client-and-server architecture. The recommended way to use it is:\\n\\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/moonlight.png)\\n\\nGreat as this is, it has a few limitations:\\n\\n1. It can only discover servers on the same subnet. If you want to connect and play from outside your network, ~~you need to poke holes in your gateway~~ you can set it up via the [Moonlight Internet Hosting Tool](https://github.com/moonlight-stream/moonlight-docs/wiki/Setup-Guide#streaming-over-the-internet).\\n\\n2. Because it\'s streaming, one server can only accept one client connection at a time, and whatever you do on the client happens directly on the server\u2014to some extent, it can even work as a remote desktop.\\n\\n3. The server is basically limited to Nvidia GPUs, as rendering relies on Nvidia GeForce Experience\'s native streaming capability. The upside, though, is that host setup is super simple.\\n\\nJust like in the architecture diagram, if I ever lose my mind and decide to switch all my work consoles over to Linux in the future, this would be ideal for setting up a dedicated Windows gaming server tucked away in some corner without a monitor attached.\\n\\nPaired with WOL (Wake-on-LAN), you can power it on only when you want to game and shut it down remotely when you\'re done\u2014saving power and taking up no desk space. You can even hook it up to a TV box to play on a big-screen TV. If only in the future this could work more like X Window\u2014\\n\\ninstead of sending over the whole rendered screen, outputting directly to the client so one server could let multiple clients run different things, that would be amazing (keep dreaming).\\n\\nPS: AMD also made AMD Link which should be able to do something similar, I just haven\'t tried it yet.\\n\\n## Network\\n\\n### Browser\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/brave-logo.png\\" alt=\\"Brave\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Brave Browser](https://brave.com/) - Windows & Linux & Android\\n\\n* Windows installation\\n\\n```r\\t\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade brave -y\\n```\\n\\nOf course, you can also go the old-fashioned route, download it from the official site, and double-click to install\u2014though I think letting a package manager handle updates fits modern tastes a bit better.\\n\\t\\n* Linux(Manjaro) installation\\n\\n```r\\nsudo pacman -Syu brave\\n```\\n\\n* Android installation\\n\\n  <a href=\\"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.brave.browser\\"><img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/google-play-badge.png\\" alt=\\"google-play-badge\\" style={{ width: \\"150px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} /></a>\\n\\nThis browser is highly compatible with Google Chrome (since it\'s built on Chromium, after all). Its sync method doesn\'t use account-based sign-in; instead, it uses a passphrase string as a Sync Code to sync data. So far, there haven\'t been any special settings worth mentioning.\\n\\n### SSH Client\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/putty.jpg\\" alt=\\"Putty\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Putty](https://www.putty.org/) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows installation\\n\\n1. Use the Portable version\\n\\n\\t[Portable Version](https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/putty/latest.html)\\n\\n2. Install via Chocolatey\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade putty -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro) installation\\n\\n```r\\nsudo pacman -Syu putty\\n```\\n\\nOn Windows, not having PuTTY is painful because there aren\'t many good SSH clients. On Linux, however, the built-in terminal or Terminator (installed later) works great, so aside from socket features or private key compatibility, there isn\'t really much reason to use PuTTY.\\t\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/download.jpg\\" alt=\\"JuiceSSH\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[JuiceSSH](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.sonelli.juicessh) - Android\\n      <a href=\\"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.sonelli.juicessh\\"><img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/google-play-badge.png\\" alt=\\"google-play-badge\\" style={{ width: \\"150px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} /></a>\\n\\nEven though touchscreen keyboards on Android are universally painful for CLI work, having a client on hand for emergencies is still pretty useful in a pinch.\\n\\n### SFTP/FTP Client\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/1200px-FileZilla_logo.svg.png\\" alt=\\"Filezilla\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Filezilla](#) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade filezilla -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S filezilla\\n```\\n\\nThese days I don\'t use FTP/SFTP nearly as much as back in college, but to some extent you still need an SFTP client. This one also supports PuTTY keys, so it\'s quite practical. Otherwise, the alternative is *WinSCP*, though in practice there isn\'t that big of a difference between the two.\\n\\n### Mail/IM\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/large.png\\" alt=\\"Rambox\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Rambox](https://rambox.pro/#home) - Windows & Linux \\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade Rambox -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S rambox\\n```\\n\\nThis is actually quite handy. We live in the smartphone era, so most IM apps\u2014like LINE, WeChat, Telegram, etc.\u2014are mobile-first. Rambox is essentially a wrapper that brings all IM/mailboxes with web clients together in one place, saving your desktop from the clutter of having a dozen separate apps open. The downside is there\'s no LINE (since it doesn\'t have a web client).\\n\\n### Remote Desktop\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Remmina-Official-Logo.png\\" alt=\\"Remmina\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Remmina](https://remmina.org/) - Linux\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S remmina\\n```\\n\\nTo use RDP, make sure to install:\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S freerdp\\n```\\n\\nThis is a great tool if you want to use RDP on Linux. I\'m not a big fan of remote desktop apps like TeamViewer or VNC that rely on other proprietary/special protocols, so I prefer RDP (on Windows I just use the built-in RDP client). Apparently this can open an SSH tunnel directly without needing PuTTY, though I haven\'t successfully tried that myself.\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/teamviewer_logo.jpg\\" alt=\\"teamviewer\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Teamviewer](https://www.teamviewer.com/en/) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade teamviewer -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S teamviewer\\n```\\n\\nHonestly, this is a very powerful remote desktop tool\u2014and precisely because it\'s *too* powerful, I don\'t really like it. My biggest mental hurdle is that a machine sitting behind NAT, without any port forwarding configured on the NAT gateway,\\n\\ncan still be connected to using just a code or account. Its NAT traversal and connectivity are insanely strong... which is why I\'m a bit hesitant to use it.\\n\\n### VPN\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/qv2ray.jpg\\" alt=\\"Qv2ray\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[qv2ray](https://github.com/Qv2ray/Qv2ray) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade qv2ray -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S qv2ray\\n```\\n\\nNeeds to be used together with [V2ray-Core](https://github.com/v2ray/v2ray-core). It\'s basically just a GUI frontend for V2ray-Core, works smoothly and is easy to use. Short description, but a very important \\"ladder\\" (proxy tool).\\n\\n## System Tools\\n\\n### Launcher\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/orchis-51s.png\\" alt=\\"Orchis\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Orchis](http://www.eonet.ne.jp/~gorota/) - Windows\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\nThis program has been with me for a very long time. Basically, you can only download it from [its website](http://www.eonet.ne.jp/~gorota/)\u2014a very classic Japanese-style approach.\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/1200px-Gnome-Pie_Logo.svg.png\\" alt=\\"Gnome Pie\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Gnome Pie](http://schneegans.github.io/gnome-pie) - Linux\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S gnome-pie\\n```\\n\\nThis is what I ended up with while looking for a Linux alternative to Orchis. In actual use, it feels pretty nice.\\n\\n### Package Manager\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/download.png\\" alt=\\"Chocolatey\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Chocolatey](https://chocolatey.org/) - Windows\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nSet-ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Scope Process -Force; [System.Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol = [System.Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol -bor 3072; iex ((New-Object System.Net.WebClient).DownloadString(\'https://chocolatey.org/install.ps1\'))\\n```\\n\\nThis is super useful, provided your internet connection cooperates.\\n\\n#### [yay](https://github.com/Jguer/yay) - Archlinux & Manjaro\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nsudo pacman -S yay\\n```\\n\\nIn Manjaro, `yay` can be installed directly with the command above, whereas on Arch Linux that won\'t work\u2014you have to install it via git.\\n\\n### Terminal\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/WT.jpg\\" alt=\\"Windows Terminal\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Windows Terminal](https://github.com/microsoft/terminal) - Windows\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade microsoft-windows-terminal -y\\n```\\n\\nThis is easily Microsoft\'s best terminal app\u2014the old `cmd` and PowerShell don\'t even come close. If you have WSL installed, you can open a Linux terminal right inside it, which is super convenient.\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/terminator.jpg\\" alt=\\"Terminator\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Terminator](https://terminator-gtk3.readthedocs.io/en/latest/) - Linux\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S terminator\\n```\\n\\nHonestly, I don\'t have high demands for a Linux console\u2014basic features are plenty for me\u2014but after seeing Chris Titus recommend it as his favorite, I decided to jump on the bandwagon.\\n\\n### Virtual Machine\\n\\n#### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/vmware-workstation-logo-png.png\\" alt=\\"VMware\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[VMware Workstation](https://www.vmware.com/products/workstation-pro.html) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade vmware-workstation -y\\n```\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S vmware-workstation\\n```\\n\\nVMware\u2014the reigning king of virtual machine environments in my heart. Simple yet powerful VMs; I even run VMware ESXi on my server. The fact that it can be installed via Chocolatey on Windows was also a major reason I leaned completely into using Chocolatey.\\n\\n#### ![Qemu| 20](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/qemu_logo.jpg)[Qemu](https://www.qemu.org/) - Windows & Linux\\n\\n* Windows\\n\\n```r\\n#Powershell as Administrator with Chocolatey\\nchoco upgrade qemu -y\\n```\\n\\nFor a GUI, please use <a href=\\"https://qtemu.org/\\"><img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/QtEmu.jpg\\" alt=\\"Qtemu\\" style={{ width: \\"30px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} /></a>\\n\\n* Linux(Manjaro)\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S qemu\\n```\\n\\nFor a GUI, please install <a href=\\"https://virt-manager.org/\\"><img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/virt-m.png\\" alt=\\"Virt-manager\\" style={{ width: \\"40px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} /></a>\\n\\n```r\\nyay -S libvirt\\n```\\n\\nTo be honest, there\'s really only one reason I wanted to touch QEMU: it currently looks like the only program capable of creating a Portable Virtual Machine, whereas basically everything else requires installation.\\n\\nI\'ll write a dedicated note about using QEMU later on."},{"id":"2020/07/11-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/07/11-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-07-11-01.md","title":"An Uncle\'s Ramblings - Basic Git Usage & Essential Tools for Blogging","description":"Git and related tools for writing a Hugo blog","date":"2020-07-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Git","permalink":"/en/tags/git"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":5.26,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"An Uncle\'s Ramblings - Basic Git Usage & Essential Tools for Blogging","date":"2020-07-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Blog","Git","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/07/11-01","description":"Git and related tools for writing a Hugo blog"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Uncle\'s Ramblings - Windows & Linux Tool Inventory","permalink":"/en/2020/07/15-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Raising a Penguin - Arch Linux Gateway - Summary","permalink":"/en/2020/07/07-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was migrated from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nEver since this uncle switched to using GitHub + Markdown syntax to write blog posts, my efficiency has definitely improved a lot. However, because I wasn\'t familiar enough with Git commands, I did a lot of really dumb things (like nuking my own repo and starting over from scratch multiple times).\\n\\nThings like not knowing how to handle diverged branches when pushing\u2014stuff that looks hilariously stupid to experienced Git veterans. So I decided to spend a short post writing down the Git commands I actually use, along with turning the commands I frequently run for this blog into scripts for future use.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n\\n# Basic Writing Environment\\n\\nBasically, my workflow is very simple\u2014I just write on different machines and can\'t finish a post in one sitting, so I need a way to seamlessly pick up where I left off across machines (when I say \\"multiple locations,\\" it\'s really just the office and my dorm).\\n\\n1. A machine that can run Git \u2014 for me, that\'s basically Manjaro running on my work PC and a Manjaro VM inside Windows in my room.\\n2. Typora + PicGo \u2014 since Typora is cross-platform, there\'s zero issue there.\\n3. A GitHub account \u2014 based on what I learned in the previous post, you need one repo for the whole site, one repo to serve the public web pages, and another repo to act as an image host. Later on, I\'ll probably create one more repo to store reusable general-purpose scripts.\\n\\nIn short, as long as I\'m somewhere with Git, Typora, and an internet connection, I can write.\\n\\n# Tool Setup\\n\\n## Typora\\n\\nFirst, you need to install Typora (I used to write with Remarkable, but after getting hooked on Typora, I switched completely).\\n\\n### Windows\\n\\nWe can use Chocolatey to install it, which is super convenient:\\n\\n```\\nchoco upgrade typora -y\\n```\\n\\nI know the standard syntax is `choco install`, but `choco upgrade` not only upgrades existing packages, it also installs ones that aren\'t installed yet... so just using this one command is super convenient.\\n\\n### Linux (Manjaro)\\n\\n```\\nyay -Syu typora\\n```\\n\\nTypora is currently still in the AUR, so you need to install it using *yay*.\\n\\n\\n\\nOn a side note, although Typora supports a lot of Markdown diagramming syntax, the Hugo theme I picked doesn\'t support it\u2014so once Hugo generates the static site, those diagrams won\'t show up. To some extent, rather than trying to render diagrams directly with Markdown syntax, it\'s simpler to just draw an image and upload it to your image host.\\n\\n### PicGO\\n\\nOn Typora regardless of platform, you can go to:\\n\\n> File--\x3ePreferences...--\x3eImage and select PicGo-Core under Image Uploader\\n\\nIt will automatically install PicGo. As for the config file, although the official docs tell you to run:\\n\\n```\\npicgo set uploader\\n```\\n\\nto configure it interactively, PicGo installed this way is buried in a hard-to-find path, and when using GitHub as an image host, even the interactive setup configures it wrong... It\'s much easier to just click *Open Config File* and paste in a modified version of the config below.\\n\\n#### PicGo Config File for GitHub\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"picBed\\": {\\n    \\"current\\": \\"github\\",\\n    \\"github\\": {\\n      \\"repo\\": \\"<github_User_name/Repo_Name>\\",\\n      \\"branch\\": \\"master\\",\\n      \\"token\\": \\"<your Github Token>\\",\\n      \\"path\\": \\"<your path under your Repo>\\",\\n      \\"customUrl\\": \\"\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\"picgoPlugins\\": {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\n> `<github_User_name/Repo_Name>` - Fill in your username/repository name\\n> `<your Github Token>` - Your GitHub Token for accessing the GitHub API\\n> `<your path under your Repo>` - Optional subfolder path inside your repo\\n\\nBasically, this plugin is super powerful: it can upload your local images directly to your image host and automatically rewrite the link to point to the hosted URL, making it effortless to include and manage images in blog posts. One catch is that files with the same name cannot be overwritten, so if an upload fails, check whether there\'s a filename collision and rename it.\\n\\n## git\\n\\n### References\\n[TW git book](https://gitbook.tw/)\\n\\n[ihower\'s Git Classroom](https://ihower.tw/git/remote.html)\\n\\n\\n### Common Commands\\n\\n* git clone - Download an entire Git repo from scratch where nothing exists yet\\n\\n```bash\\ngit clone --recursive https://github.com/<user_name>/<repo_name>.git\\n```\\n\\nBecause using GitHub for a blog in this setup involves more than one submodule inside a single Git repo, you need to add the *--recursive* flag to make sure submodules are downloaded as well.\\n\\n* git config - Used to configure the Git environment; adding the *--global* flag applies settings globally\\n\\n```bash\\ngit config --global pull.ff only\\n```\\n\\nSince I write everything myself, branches shouldn\'t diverge in general; this specifies that merges should only proceed in fast-forward mode.\\n\\n```bash\\ngit config --global user.name \\"<github_account>\\"\\n```\\n\\nThis line defines your Git account name.\\n\\n```bash\\ngit config --global user.email \\"<your_Email_address>\\"\\n```\\n\\nThis defines your email address.\\n\\n* git branch - Used to create or list branches; for my use case, it\'s mainly to check whether any messy branches exist that might cause update issues\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch\\n```\\n\\nThis lets you see which branches you have. For details, check out the [Taiwan Git Book](https://gitbook.tw/).\\n\\n* git checkout - Used to switch branches\\n\\n```bash\\ngit checkout <repo_you_want>\\n```\\n\\nSwitches the current branch to `<repo_you_want>`.\\n\\n* git pull - Fetch and sync from remote\\n\\n```bash\\ngit pull\\n```\\n\\n  Syncs from `origin/master`, equivalent to *git fetch* + *git merge origin/master*.\\n\\n* git push - The opposite of `git pull`\\n\\n```bash\\ngit push origin/master\\n```\\n\\nPushing from local up to `origin/master` is the primary command for publishing and uploading.\\n\\n# Turning It Into Scripts\\n\\n## Auto-Download to Sync Local with Remote Repo First (Reduces Branch Conflicts)\\n\\n```bash\\n#!/bin/bash\\n\\necho -e \\"\\\\033[0;32mSync your Local git folder with github\\\\033[0m\\"\\n\\n#pull data from github\\n\\ngit pull\\n\\n#update theme\\n\\ngit submodule update --remote --merge\\n\\n```\\n\\n\\n\\n## Auto-Upload Website Updates and Back Up Source Files\\n\\n```bash\\n#!/bin/bash\\n\\necho -e \\"\\\\033[0;32mDeploying updates to GitHub...\\\\033[0m\\"\\n\\n# Build the project.\\n\\nhugo \\n\\n# Go To Public folder\\n\\ncd public\\n\\n# Add changes to git.\\n\\ngit add .\\n\\n# Commit changes.\\n\\nmsg=\\"rebuilding site `date`\\"\\nif [ $# -eq 1 ]\\n  then msg=\\"$1\\"\\nfi\\ngit commit -m \\"$msg\\"\\n\\n# Push source and build repos.\\n\\ngit push origin master\\n\\n# Come Back up to the Project Root\\n\\ncd ..\\n\\n# Commit source repository changes\\n\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"$msg\\"\\ngit push\\n```\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nGit is a very powerful version control tool\u2014paired with generous platforms, it becomes an unbeatable combo."},{"id":"2020/07/07-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/07/07-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-07-07-01.md","title":"Raising a Penguin - Arch Linux Gateway - Summary","description":"Explains how to use Arch Linux as a homelab Gateway","date":"2020-07-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"V2Ray","permalink":"/en/tags/v-2-ray"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":12.37,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"slug":"2020/07/07-01","title":"Raising a Penguin - Arch Linux Gateway - Summary","date":"2020-07-07T00:00:00.000Z","description":"Explains how to use Arch Linux as a homelab Gateway","tags":["Linux","Server","V2Ray","Old-Blog"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"An Uncle\'s Ramblings - Basic Git Usage & Essential Tools for Blogging","permalink":"/en/2020/07/11-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Escaping the GFW - V2Ray Server Setup Summary","permalink":"/en/2020/07/07-02"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nI switched to a new blog and haven\'t decided what to write yet, so I thought I\'d reorganize my previous notes into a streamlined and supplemented version (actually just removing or replacing parts that I later found didn\'t work well).\\n\\nI\'m also practicing using GitHub to manage the scripts I wrote myself. However, since many of them are private in nature, I\'ll probably keep them in a private repo and write a separate public version outside for anyone who needs it.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n\\n# Desired Environment\\n\\n## Basic Requirements\\n\\nThe biggest reason I use Arch Linux instead of FreeBSD\u2014which I have used for many years\u2014as the primary Gateway for a network environment is the V2Ray + iptables combo for bypassing the firewall (looking at V2Ray alone, FreeBSD can actually install it too, but it doesn\'t have iptables). Almost every guide I saw online used this exact combination. Since I\'m not a true expert either, I just went with the flow and picked a Linux distro that looked pleasing to use. Some might ask why I didn\'t just use Ubuntu like the guides\u2014all I can say is that I still have a bit of tsundere pride....\\n\\nActually, I originally wanted to go even more extreme and use Gentoo, but remembering the painful past where just installing the system took me three days (because of tons of compiling, though my machine back then wasn\'t powerful enough either), I settled for the next best thing and switched to Arch Linux, which installs almost everything via binaries.\\n\\n## Expectations\\n\\nBasically, the biggest expectation is to build a network environment similar to home in Taiwan without needing to do anything extra. Based on this, a large NAT internal network and bypassing the firewall right at the Gateway side are the most basic of the basic requirements.\\n\\n## Blueprint\\n\\n![Network_top](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/Network_top.png)\\n\\n# Preparation\\n## Basic Tools & Equipment\\n### Hardware\\n* A piece of server hardware capable of running VMware ESXi\\n* A Wireless AP that meets the network requirements\\n### Software Tools\\n* [VMware ESXi](https://www.vmware.com/products/esxi-and-esx.html) 5.5 or 5.0 - (Mainly because of the client, though you can actually use the latest version) or other types of VMs such as Xen, etc.\\n* [Archlinux install Image](https://www.archlinux.org/) - This is the main protagonist, like ~~MS-06F~~ RX-78 to the original Gundam\\n* [vSphere Client](https://players.brightcove.net/1534342432001/Byh3doRJx_default/index.html?videoId=2011143030001) - This is the client used to control VMware ESXi\\n\\nNow VMware has renamed ESXi to [vSphere Hypervisor](https://www.vmware.com/products/vsphere-hypervisor.html), which has more features, though I\'m not sure about its pricing and haven\'t actually used it myself, so I\'m just leaving it here for reference.\\nIf I have the chance to set up a new VMware server in the future, I might give it a try.\\n\\n## References\\n\\nOut of respect, I\'m still listing all the documents I referenced when writing this earlier:\\n\\n* [Arch Wiki](https://wiki.archlinux.org/) - The very detailed Arch Wiki has documentation for almost all Arch Linux configurations, and many pages have Chinese translations, which is super useful.\\n* [Archlinux Install Guide](https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/Installation_guide_(\u6B63\u9AD4\u4E2D\u6587)) - This is the official Chinese installation guide, considered the most fundamental reference for installation, and is actually part of the Arch Wiki.\\n* [Miro\'s Blog](https://mirokaku.github.io/Blog/2016/ArchLinux-install-notes/) - These Arch Linux installation notes are very well written.\\n\\nAnother reference webpage disappeared for some reason when I was organizing this... so I won\'t list it anymore, though I\'m still very grateful for the predecessors\' efforts and sharing.\\n\\n# Installation Steps\\n\\n## Basic Concept\\n\\nBecause I ~~am a lazy bum~~ mainly use this to install a Gateway, I need to avoid looking things up left and right during installation as much as possible. Even though I listed a bunch of key installation points earlier, in the end it still comes down to using simple installation scripts to handle the setup for me under poor network conditions, keeping everything as simple as possible.\\n\\n## Basic Workflow\\n\\nBecause the Arch installation process involves `chroot`, one script cannot finish all actions in a single run, and I\'m not skilled enough to automatically run another script inside `chroot` at the same time, so the scripts are entered manually in two stages.\\n\\nThe first script after booting:\\n\\n`zsh <(curl -L -s http://my_home.or.any.vps/arch-install-before-chroot.sh)`\\n\\nIt will automatically run up to `chroot` and stop, and then you use the second dose:\\n\\n`zsh <(curl -L -s http://my_home.or.any.vps/arch-install--after-chroot.sh)`\\n\\nPut bluntly, the way these two scripts run is by using the *curl* command to let the shell directly execute scripts hosted on another web server.\\nThis is a relatively convenient approach for running scripts in a Live environment, but it requires preparing a web server to host the scripts in advance and worrying about sensitive security issues inside the scripts\u2014so it\'s not a foolproof method. Therefore, my approach is to customize the scripts and store them somewhere private first, upload them to a VPS or another server when needed, and delete them right after running.\\nThis is a bit more troublesome.\\n\\n## Script Explanation\\n\\n### Color Definitions\\nASCII color definitions are as follows:\\n\\n|Color|Code|Color|Code|\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n|Black|0;30|Dark Gray|1;30|\\n|Red|0;31|Light Red|1;31|\\n|Green|0;32|Light Green|1;32|\\n|Brown/Orange|0;33|Yellow|1;33|\\n|Blue|0;34|Light Blue|1;34|\\n|Purple|0;35|Light Purple|1;35|\\n|Cyan|0;36|Light Cyan|1;36|\\n|Light Gray|0;37|White|1;37|\\n\\nIf needed, you can change the display colors in the scripts below according to your personal preference.\\n\\n### Script Content Explanation\\n\\nSome basic explanations of what\'s inside the scripts:\\n\\n`echo -e \\"<what ever you like>\\"`\\n\\nThis syntax is for strings you want to display on the screen while running the script; typing it as above will output `<what ever you like>`.\\n\\n```bash\\n\u8B8A\u6578=XYZ\\n${\u8B8A\u6578}\\n```\\n\\nThis syntax pairs with the variable definitions at the very top\u2014whenever something is used repeatedly, this saves typing. Below, I mainly set the colors as variables, so as long as you don\'t want rainbow text, you only need to change it right here.\\n\\nUpdate 7/12: I changed the non-generic parts into interactive inputs, so this script can later be placed on GitHub for anyone who needs it to use directly.\\n\\n#### arch-install-before-chroot.sh\\n\\n```bash\\n#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n#(\u5F9EIso boot\u5F8C\u76F4\u5230\u5B8C\u6210change root\u5167\u6240\u6709\u5B89\u88DD/\u8ABF\u6574\u52D5\u4F5C)\\n#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n#!/bin/zsh\\n#Parmeter Define\\nCOLOR=\'\\\\033[1;35m\'\\nNC=\'\\\\033[0m\'\\n\\n#start ntp\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Starting NTP Service${NC}\\"\\ntimedatectl set-ntp true\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Modify Mirrorlist to setting country\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Starting Modify mirrorlist to China servers${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter which Country you like(ie. United_State or China)${NC}\\"\\nread COUNTRY\\nsed -i \'/Score/{/$COUNTRY/!{n;s/^/#/}}\' /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Fdisk\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Partition your HDD please create 1 data as sda1 and 1 swap as sda2${NC}\\"\\nfdisk /dev/sda\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Format\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Format /dev/sda1 as EXT4 format${NC}\\"\\nmkfs.ext4 /dev/sda1\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Format /dev/sda2 as Linux Swap${NC}\\"\\nmkswap /dev/sda2\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Mount /dev/sda1 to /mnt${NC}\\"\\nmount /dev/sda1 /mnt\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Mount Swap${NC}\\"\\nswapon /dev/sda2\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Install\\necho \\"${COLOR}Starting Install Archlinux into /mnt${NC}\\"\\npacstrap /mnt base vim zsh curl\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#fstab\\necho \\"${COLOR}Starting Gernerate fstab${NC}\\"\\ngenfstab -U -p /mnt >> /mnt/etc/fstab\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Copy Zsh\\necho \\"${COLOR}Starting Copy ZSH setting file to new Archlinux${NC}\\"\\ncp -Rv /etc/zsh /mnt/etc/\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n# Change root\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Change root to new Archlinux${NC}\\"\\narch-chroot /mnt /bin/zsh\\n#------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n#(\u4EE5\u4E0B\u662F\u4EC0\u9EBC\u90FD\u5F04\u5B8C\u4E86\u53EA\u5269\u4E0B\u91CD\u958B\u6A5F)\\n#------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished Installation, will reboot, Good luck${NC}\\"\\n# Reboot\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Rebooting...${NC}\\"\\nreboot\\n```\\n\\nBasically, other than possibly adjusting the partitioning options, there\'s really nothing you need to touch in this script. Since I only use this machine as a Gateway on a VM, there\'s naturally no need to make the partitioning complicated. If you have other needs, you can refer to the official installation guide to adjust the partitioning layout as desired.\\n\\nThe default partitioning layout is:\\n\\n```bash\\n/dev/sda1\u70BA\u4E3B\u8981\u8CC7\u6599\\n/dev/sda2\u70BASwap\\n```\\n\\n#### arch-install-after-chroot.sh\\n\\nThis can be considered the main installation script. There are many things in it that need to be modified and configured in advance, and I\'ve tried my best to turn them into variables at the very top.\\n\\n```bash\\n#------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n#(\u6240\u6709\u52D5\u4F5C\u90FD\u662F\u5728change root\u5167\u5B8C\u6210\u7684)\\n#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n#!/bin/zsh\\n#Parameter Define\\nCOLOR=\'\\\\033[1;34m\'\\nNC=\'\\\\033[0m\'\\n\\n#update package\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Start Pacman Update${NC}\\"\\npacman -Syuu\\necho -e \\"$COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#locale-gen to add en_US & zh_TW\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Setting local file${NC}\\"\\necho \\"en_US.UTF-8 UTF-8\\" >> /etc/locale.gen\\necho \\"zh_TW.UTF-8 UTF-8\\" >> /etc/locale.gen\\necho -e \\"#{COLOR}Generate locale.conf${NC}\\"\\nlocale-gen\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Setting locale.conf${NC}\\"\\necho LANG=en_US.UTF-8 > /etc/locale.conf\\nexport LANG=en_US.UTF-8\\necho -e \\"$COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#change Timezone to CTS(Taipei)\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Change Time Zone to Asia/Taipei & Set Hardware time${NC}\\"\\nln -sf /usr/share/zoneinfo/Asia/Taipei /etc/localtime\\nhwclock --systohc --utc\\necho -e \\"$COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Network\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Setting \'Gateway\' as hostname${NC}\\"\\necho Gateway > /etc/hostname\\necho \\"127.0.0.1 localhost Gateway\\" >> /etc/hosts\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Define your NIC by Mac address${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter your MAC address for your outside NIC${NC}\\"\\nread OUTSIDE\\necho \'SUBSYSTEM==\\"net\\", ACTION==\\"add\\", ATTR{address}==\\"$OUTSIDE\\", NAME=\\"EXT0\\"\' > /etc/udev/rules.d/10-network.rules\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter your MAC address for your inside NIC${NC}\\"\\nread INSIDE\\necho \'SUBSYSTEM==\\"net\\", ACTION==\\"add\\", ATTR{address}==\\"$INSIDE\\", NAME=\\"INT0\\"\' >> /etc/udev/rules.d/10-network.rules\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Define your PPPOE Setting${NC}\\"\\necho \\"Description=\'EXT0 PPPOE SETTING\'\\" > /etc/netctl/EXT0.service\\necho \\"Interface=EXT0\\" >> /etc/netctl/EXT0.service\\necho \\"Connection=pppoe\\" >> /etc/netctl/EXT0.service\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter your PPPOE acount:${NC}\\"\\nread ISP\\necho \\"User=\'${ISP}\'\\" >> /etc/netctl/EXT0.service\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter your PPPOE password${NC}\\"\\nread ISPPW\\necho \\"Password=\'${ISPPW}\'\\" >> /etc/netctl/EXT0.service\\necho \\"ConnectionMode=\'persist\'\\" >> /etc/netctl/EXT0.service\\necho \\"UsePeerDNS=false\\" >> /etc/netctl/EXT0.service\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Enable EXT0{NC}\\"\\nnetctl enable EXT0.service\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Define your Private Gateway IP for INT0${NC}\\"\\necho \\"Description=\'INT0 IP SETTING\'\\" > /etc/netctl/INT0.service\\necho \\"Interface=INT0\\" >> /etc/netctl/INT0.service\\necho \\"Connection=ethernet\\" >> /etc/netctl/INT0.service\\necho \\"IP=static\\" >> /etc/netctl/INT0.service\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter your Gateway IP address:${NC}\\"\\nread GATEWAYIP\\necho \\"Address=(\'${GATEWAYIP}/24\')\\" >> /etc/netctl/INT0.service\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Enable INT0${NC}\\"\\nnetctl enable INT0.service\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Initramfs\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Initramfs your Linux${NC}\\"\\nmkinitcpio -p linux\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Root Password\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Set your root password${NC}\\"\\npasswd\\nchsh -s /bin/zsh\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#add User\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Add user account:${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"$What ID you want:${NC}\\"\\nread YOURID\\nuseradd -m -g root -s /bin/zsh ${YOURID}\\npasswd ${YOURID}\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Add $YOURID into sudo list${NC}\\"\\npacman -Syu sudo\\necho \\"$YOURID ALL=(ALL) ALL\\" >> /etc/sudoers\\n\\n#install Tools\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install Packages Microcode/Bootloader - grub/dnsutils/open-vm-\\ttools/v2ray${NC}\\"\\npacman -Sy intel-ucode grub dnsutils open-vm-tools v2ray screen\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#install Bootloader\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install grub Boot Loader into /dev/sda1${NC}\\"\\ngrub-install --target=i386-pc /dev/sda1\\ngrub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#V2ray config.json get\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Fetch V2ray config.json and replace${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter where you put the file:${NC}\\"\\nread link\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter the file name:${NC}\\"\\nread conffile\\nwget $Link/$conffile\\nmv -f  ./$conffile /etc/v2ray/config.json\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Set Nat\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Open package fowrading${NC}\\"\\necho \\"net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\\" > /etc/sysctl/30-ipforward.conf\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Create Iptable start script${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter your V2ray Server IP:${NC}\\"\\nread VPSIP\\necho \\"#Re-direction TCP\\" > /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -N V2RAY\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 0.0.0.0/8 -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 10.0.0.0/8 -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 127.0.0.0/8 -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 169.254.0.0/16 -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 172.16.0.0/12-j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 192.168.0.0/16 -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 224.0.0.0/4 -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d 240.0.0.0/4 -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -d ${VPSIP} -j RETURN\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A V2RAY -p tcp -j REDIRECT --to-ports 12345\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -p tcp -j V2RAY\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\" \\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"#Natd\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho \\"iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 192.168/16 -j MASQUERADE\\" >> /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\nchmod 750 /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Create Systemd Service${NC}\\"\\necho \\"[Unit] > /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\necho \\"Description=iptables rules for V2Ray Daemon >> /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\necho \\" \\" >> /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\necho \\"[Service]\\" >> /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\necho \\"ExecStart=/bin/sh /etc/iptables/iptable.sh\\" >> /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\necho \\" \\" >> /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\necho \\"[Install]\\" >> /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\necho \\"WantedBy=multi-user.target\\" >> /etc/systemd/system/iptables.service\\nsystemctl enable iptables.service\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Finished.${NC}\\"\\n\\n#Finished install\\nsync\\nsync\\nsync\\nexit\\n```\\n\\nThis script makes the following assumptions:\\n\\n1. This machine has an Intel CPU\\n2. V2Ray\'s `config.json` is already written in advance and uses the DNS feature of V2Ray 4.20, allowing you to skip installing and configuring Dnsmasq (making it much cleaner)\\n\\n# Conclusion\\nIn the original blog I wrote two posts about automating this Gateway installation, and I\'ve actually used and tested it several times in real life. You can basically take this script, adjust the variables, and use it directly."},{"id":"2020/07/07-02","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/07/07-02","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-07-07-02.md","title":"Escaping the GFW - V2Ray Server Setup Summary","description":"Summary of setting up V2Ray as a proxy ladder in the 2020s","date":"2020-07-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"V2Ray","permalink":"/en/tags/v-2-ray"},{"inline":true,"label":"Server","permalink":"/en/tags/server"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linux","permalink":"/en/tags/linux"},{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"}],"readingTime":14.12,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Escaping the GFW - V2Ray Server Setup Summary","date":"2020-07-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["V2Ray","Server","Linux","Old-Blog"],"slug":"2020/07/07-02","description":"Summary of setting up V2Ray as a proxy ladder in the 2020s"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Raising a Penguin - Arch Linux Gateway - Summary","permalink":"/en/2020/07/07-01"},"nextItem":{"title":"Uncle\'s Ramblings - Hugo + GitHub","permalink":"/en/2020/07/06-01"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info\\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\nV2Ray\u2014this is arguably the main reason I started writing this blog, because it is a specialized skill only needed when living inside the Great Firewall. Generally, technical documentation is in English, yet ironically, documentation related to bypassing internet censorship is almost exclusively in Simplified Chinese...\\n\\nBecause this area of knowledge is quite niche, I felt inspired to record the methods and results I researched so I wouldn\'t have to look everything up from scratch every time\u2014and, if possible, automate these steps to reduce the effort required.\\n\\nOriginally, the article under this title was meant to consolidate everything up to the \\"update IV\\" version of VPS V2Ray Server setup and configuration. However, thinking about it carefully, I hadn\'t really covered the finer configuration details (since the V2Ray official website explains them very clearly, and once I have a working config I rarely rewrite it from scratch and mostly just reuse it). So I\'ll include a bit about the configuration here as well, though the official website\'s documentation is still better.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# Prerequisites\\n## VPS\\nEven though many people rent VPS instances for general purposes, specializing one solely for bypassing the firewall is probably something only netizens behind the GFW do, so you can find plenty of reviews online. Personally, since I\'ve always thought online freedom should be a given, I tend to be stingy when choosing a VPS\u2014picking the cheapest, barely-usable option. In practice, I just pick the lowest price available; machine performance doesn\'t matter (though KVM does)...\\n* [Anynode](https://anynode.net/) - This is my main machine. I\'m on the $20 USD/year plan with 500 GB of monthly traffic, which is more than enough as a primary VPS for personal use.\\n* [Hostenos](https://hosteons.com/) - A cheap and generous option at $21 USD for two years, with unlimited traffic and only a 100M speed cap. Very suitable if you want to generously share with others or burn through bandwidth without worrying about traffic limits (though 500 GB for one person is practically unlimited anyway...).\\n* [Bandwagonhost](https://bandwagonhost.com/) - BandwagonHost is actually a very convenient choice. Their early VPS offerings already came with a *one-click S~hadow~S~ocks~ installation* service, and recently they also launched *[Just my socks](https://bwgjms.com/post/how-to-buy-justmysocks/)*. Though a bit off-topic, anyone who doesn\'t want to set things up themselves can consider this option to save hassle.\\n## Linux Distro for VPS\\nSince we\'re picking cheap VPS hosts, it\'s naturally unrealistic to run anything with a GUI or Windows\u2014we can only run a pure CLI OS. Among them, the easiest to get started with is probably ~~FreeBSD~~Linux. Everyone has their own preferences, so just pick one whose commands you\'re comfortable with from what your VPS provider offers.\\nBecause of my own preferences, I mostly choose distros that use the apt package manager, such as Ubuntu or Debian.\\nHowever, after being converted by Arch, I\'ve become especially fond of rolling-release systems (even though for server stability it really should be the other way around).\\nSo here I\'ll use Debian as the baseline. For those using a different distro... please mentally translate the commands yourselves.\\n\\n## Domain (Optional)\\n\\nYou\'ll need this if you plan to use WebSocket+TLS+Web for obfuscation. Strictly speaking, you also need to apply for an SSL certificate, but Caddy can handle the SSL part for you, making it very simple. You just need to prepare a domain\u2014either a free one or a purchased one will do.\\n\\n## Cloudflare Account (Optional)\\n\\nYou only need this if you plan to add another layer of obfuscation on top of WebSocket+TLS+Web, betting that the firewall won\'t block all routes to your VPS web port. The principle is using CDN forwarding to bypass blocks and keep the connection alive, mainly betting that a company as large as Cloudflare won\'t have all its IPs blocked. Of course, if you don\'t like Cloudflare, you can use another CDN, though Cloudflare\'s free tier is quite tempting.\\n\\nFinally, the more obfuscation layers you add, the slower the connection speed becomes\u2014there\'s no way around that. You\'ll need to balance speed and reliability according to your own needs.\\n\\n## Planned Tools\\n* Browser - Needed to perform initial VPS setup via the web control panel.\\n* PuTTY or Linux Terminal - Simply put, an SSH client you\'re comfortable with.\\n* vi, vim, or any editor you like - There will be a lot of file editing, and since we almost never download files to edit in a GUI environment, it\'s best to use a CLI text editor. I personally prefer the vi family...\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[freenom](https://my.freenom.com/) - A place where you can get a free domain.\\n\\n[New V2Ray Plain-Language Guide](https://guide.v2fly.org/advanced/wss_and_web.html#%E6%9C%8D%E5%8A%A1%E5%99%A8%E9%85%8D%E7%BD%AE) - Essentially the official V2Ray documentation site; the content is ~~simple and~~ easy to understand.\\n\\n[Caddy Documentation](https://caddyserver.com/docs/getting-started) - The official Caddy V2 documentation page. Compared to V1, it has much more detailed config file and CLI documentation. The only thing Caddy V2 lacks compared to V1 is that automatic install script.\\n\\n[Blog - sprov](https://blog.sprov.xyz/2019/03/11/cdn-v2ray-safe-proxy/)\\n\\n[Na Shu Yang Guang](https://eveaz.com/1094.html)\\n\\n[233boy](https://github.com/233boy/v2ray/wiki/\u4F7F\u7528Cloudflare\u4E2D\u8F6CV2Ray\u6D41\u91CF)\\n\\n\\n# Installation\\n\\n## Basic System Installation\\n1. In your browser\'s VPS management panel, select your preferred Linux distro and install the OS. I chose Debian.\\n\\n2. Once installed, configure your SSH (via web terminal or whatever method is available).\\n\\n3. Log into your VPS using your preferred SSH client.\\n\\n4. Change your password for future login management:\\n\\n```bash\\npasswd\\n```\\n\\n5. Update the system:\\n\\n  ```bash\\n  #Debain or Ubuntu\\n  sudo apt update && apt upgrade\\n  \\n  #Archlinux\\n  sudo pacman -Syu\\n  ```\\n\\n  \\n\\n6. Install the required programs - Vim, V2Ray, Caddy:\\n\\n   ```bash\\n   #Debain or Ubuntu\\n   sudo apt install vim\\n   bash <(curl -L -s https://install.direct/go.sh)\\n   echo \\"deb [trusted=yes] https://apt.fury.io/caddy/ /\\" | sudo tee -a /etc/apt/sources.list.d/caddy-fury.list\\n   sudo apt update\\n   sudo apt install caddy\\n   \\n   #Archlinux\\n   sudo pacman -Sy yay\\n   yay -Sy vim v2ray caddy2 \\n   ```\\n\\n   \\n\\nWhen I wrote the original blog post, Caddy hadn\'t officially transitioned to Caddy2 yet, but since the Caddy official site announced that Caddy V1 is now under limited support, updating to V2 was only a matter of time.\\n\\n  Arch definitely looks cleaner and neater (pure personal bias).\\n\\n### Auto-Install Script - Apt-based (Debian/Ubuntu)\\n\\n```bash\\n#!bash\\n\\n#\u8B8A\u6578\u5B9A\u7FA9\\nCOLOR=\'\\\\033[1;35m\'\\nNC=\'\\\\033[0m\'\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Update Ubuntu System${NC}\\"\\napt-get -y update\\napt-get -y upgrade\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install V2ray${NC}\\"\\nbash <(curl -L -s https://install.direct/go.sh)\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Set Auto Monthy Update V2ray${NC}\\"\\necho \'59 3    1 * *   root    bash <(curl -L -s https://install.direct/go.sh)\'  >> /etc/crontab\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install BBR${NC}\\"\\nbash <(curl -L -s https://github.com/teddysun/across/raw/master/bbr.sh)\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install Caddy${NC}\\"\\nwget \\"https://github.com/caddyserver/caddy/releases/download/v2.1.1/caddy_2.1.1_linux_amd64.deb\\"\\ndpkg -i ./caddy_2.1.1_linux_amd64.deb\\"\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Setup  Caddy${NC}\\"\\nmkdir /etc/caddy\\nchown -R root:www-data /etc/caddy\\nmkdir /etc/ssl/caddy\\nchown -R www-data:root /etc/ssl/caddy\\nchmod 0770 /etc/ssl/caddy\\nmkdir /var/www\\nchown www-data:www-data /var/www\\nufw allow http\\nufw allow https\\napt install libcap2-bin\\nsetcap cap_net_bind_service=+ep /usr/local/bin/caddy\\necho \'<h1>Hello World!</h1>\' | sudo tee /var/www/index.html\\n\\n#Making Caddyfile for Caddy2\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Configure your Caddy setting${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"Please Enter your Domain name:\\"\\nread domain\\necho \\"$domain\\" { > /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho tls { >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho \\"protocols tls1.2 tls1.3\\" >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho ciphers TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256 >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho curves x25519 >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho } >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho @v2ray_websocket { >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho path /ray >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho header Connection *Upgrade* >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho header Upgrade websocket >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho } >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho -n \\"Please Enter your V2Ray port\\"\\nread PORT\\necho reverse_proxy @v2ray_websocket localhost:$PORT >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\necho } >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Setup V2ray${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter where you put the file:${NC}\\"\\nread link\\necho -n \\"${COLOR}Please Enter the file name:${NC}\\"\\nread conffile\\nwget $Link/$conffile\\nmv -f  ./$conffile /etc/v2ray/config.json\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Please enter information as required, and press Ctl+c when Caddy running well${NC}\\"\\ncaddy run -conf=/etc/caddy/Caddyfile\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Rebooting your VPS for normal operation${NC}\\"\\nreboot\\n```\\n\\n### Auto-Install Script - Pacman/yay-based (Arch Linux)\\n\\nBefore using this, you need to install and configure `sudo`. I\'ll fold this step into the Arch Linux installation script later; the method is as follows:\\n\\n```bash\\npacman -Syu sudo\\necho <user_NAME> ALL=(ALL) ALL >> /etc/sudoers\\n```\\nThis script must be run as root. Midway through, it will ask you to enter a non-root account\u2014the one you added to `sudoers`.\\nIt\'s only this troublesome because Arch Linux doesn\'t allow running `yay` as root.\\n\\n```bash\\n#!bash\\n\\n#\u8B8A\u6578\u5B9A\u7FA9\\nCOLOR=\'\\\\033[1;35m\'\\nNC=\'\\\\033[0m\'\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Update Arclinux System${NC}\\"\\npacman -Syu\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install yay for Archlinux${NC}\\"\\ngit clone https://aur.archlinux.org/yay.git\\ncd yay\\nmakepkg -si\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Need None Root account to run yay${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"Please Enter your none root account\\"\\nread YOURID\\nsu $YOURID\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install V2ray, Caddy2${NC}\\"\\nyay -Syu v2ray caddy2\\nexit\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Install BBR${NC}\\"\\nbash <(curl -L -s https://github.com/teddysun/across/raw/master/bbr.sh)\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Setup  Caddy${NC}\\"\\nmkdir /etc/caddy\\nchown -R root:http /etc/caddy\\nmkdir /etc/ssl/caddy\\nchown -R http:root /etc/ssl/caddy\\nchmod 0770 /etc/ssl/caddy\\nmkdir /var/www\\nchown http:http /var/www\\ncurl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mholt/caddy/master/dist/init/linux-systemd/caddy.service -o /etc/systemd/system/caddy.service\\nsystemctl daemon-reload\\nsystemctl enable caddy.service\\nufw allow http\\nufw allow https\\nsetcap cap_net_bind_service=+ep /usr/local/bin/caddy\\necho \'<h1>Hello World!</h1>\' | sudo tee /var/www/index.html\\n\\n#Making Caddyfile for Caddy2\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Configure your Caddy setting${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"Please Enter your Domain name:\\"\\nread domain\\necho \\"$domain\\" { > /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho tls { >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho \\"protocols tls1.2 tls1.3\\" >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho ciphers TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256 >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho curves x25519 >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho } >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho @v2ray_websocket { >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho path /ray >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho header Connection *Upgrade* >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho header Upgrade websocket >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho } >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho -n \\"Please Enter your V2Ray port\\"\\nread PORT\\necho reverse_proxy @v2ray_websocket localhost:$PORT >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\necho } >> /etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Setup V2ray${NC}\\"\\necho -n \\"Please Enter where you put the file:\\"\\nread link\\necho -n \\"Please Enter the file name:\\"\\nread conffile\\nwget $Link/$conffile\\nmv -f  ./$conffile /etc/v2ray/config.json\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Please enter information as required, and press Ctl+c when Caddy running well${NC}\\"\\ncaddy run -conf=/etc/caddy/caddy.conf\\n\\necho -e \\"${COLOR}Rebooting your VPS for normal operation${NC}\\"\\nreboot\\n```\\n\\n\\n\\n## V2Ray\\n\\nHonestly, V2Ray is a great piece of software. Fundamentally, it is a framework rather than just a single communication protocol. Even though its configuration is relatively complex, once you understand how it works, even if VMess stops working later on, another useful protocol like VMess2 will likely be wrapped into the V2Ray framework so you can keep using it.\\n\\nCurrently, as a network traffic routing tool, V2Ray already includes the following capabilities:\\n\\n* Intercepting all local UDP port 53 packets and forwarding them to a specific IP and port to implement DNS functionality.\\n\\n* Routing all traffic entering a certain port via a chosen protocol to a designated IP machine to act as a proxy (a \\"ladder\\").\\n\\nEven if you don\'t use it as a proxy ladder, V2Ray could also serve as a firewall for your home network\u2014for example, preventing kids from accessing sites they shouldn\'t visit (though you can just do that directly with a firewall without going to all this trouble).\\n\\nFor V2Ray configuration details, you can refer to the [New V2Ray Plain-Language Guide](https://guide.v2fly.org/advanced/wss_and_web.html#%E6%9C%8D%E5%8A%A1%E5%99%A8%E9%85%8D%E7%BD%AE). It is very detailed and practical\u2014you can basically just copy it, tweak a few values, and start using it.\\n\\nIn the current environment, it is recommended to use WebSocket+TLS+Web paired with a CDN like Cloudflare. There are plenty of tutorials online for reference.\\n\\n\\n## Caddy\\n\\nComparing Caddy v2 installation methods, using `yay` on Arch-based distros is still much simpler. On Debian/Ubuntu-based systems, following the official instructions didn\'t allow installing via `apt` properly at the time, so I ended up installing via the `.deb` package from GitHub. The downside is having to update the package version in the auto-install script whenever a new release comes out.\\n\\nAnother difference is that on Arch-based systems the config file is */etc/caddy/caddy.conf*, whereas when installed via `.deb` it remains */etc/caddy/Caddyfile*.\\n\\nKeep this in mind during later use so you don\'t run into issues.\\n\\n### Caddy2 Configuration\\nThis configuration file is adapted directly from the [New V2Ray Plain-Language Guide](https://guide.v2fly.org/advanced/wss_and_web.html#%E6%9C%8D%E5%8A%A1%E5%99%A8%E9%85%8D%E7%BD%AE):\\n```json\\n# Caddy v2 (recommended)\\n\\n<mydomain.me> {\\n    log {\\n        output file /etc/caddy/caddy.log\\n    }\\n    tls {\\n        protocols tls1.2 tls1.3\\n        ciphers TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256\\n        curves x25519\\n    }\\n    @v2ray_websocket {\\n        path /<your_path>\\n        header Connection *Upgrade*\\n        header Upgrade websocket\\n    }\\n    reverse_proxy @v2ray_websocket localhost:<your_port_Number>\\n}\\n```\\n\\n> Replace `<mydomain.me>` with your domain without `<>`.\\n>\\n> Replace `<your_path>` with your V2Ray WebSocket path without `<>`.\\n>\\n> Replace `<your_port_Number>` with your V2Ray port number without `<>`.\\n\\nYou can copy everything else as-is.\\n\\nStrictly speaking, since I only just adopted Caddy v2 while writing this post, I\'m not too familiar with its config format myself\u2014I recommend checking the [Caddy Documentation](https://caddyserver.com/docs/getting-started).\\n\\n## Client-Side (Gateway) Setup Guide\\n\\n### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/qv2ray.jpg\\" alt=\\"Qv2Ray\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Qv2ray](https://github.com/Qv2ray/Qv2ray) - Windows & Linux & macOS\\n\\nThis is a Qt-based GUI for V2Ray. You generally use it together with [V2ray-Core](https://github.com/v2fly/v2ray-core/releases). V2ray-Core updates frequently, though it usually maintains compatibility with the previous three versions, so it\'s rarely an issue.\\n\\n#### Windows\\n\\n```\\nchoco upgrade qv2ray v2ray -y\\n```\\n\\nThis single line installs everything\u2014just open it and configure your settings.\\n\\n#### Linux (Arch Linux / Manjaro)\\n\\n```\\nyay -Syu qv2ray v2ray\\n```\\n\\nAnd that\'s it for installation.\\n\\n#### Linux (Others)\\n\\nThere is an AppImage file on the official GitHub releases page that you can download and run directly.\\n\\n#### macOS\\n\\nI can\'t afford one so I don\'t know, though there\'s a `.dmg` file on the official GitHub releases page\u2014you should just be able to download and install it directly.\\n\\nFor related instructions, please refer to the [Official Qv2ray Documentation](https://qv2ray.github.io/en/).\\n\\n### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/unnamed.png\\" alt=\\"BifrostV\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[BifrostV](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.github.dawndiy.bifrostv) - Android\\n\\n       <a href=\\"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.github.dawndiy.bifrostv\\"><img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/google-play-badge.png\\" alt=\\"google-play-badge\\" style={{ width: \\"150px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} /></a>\\n\\nJust tap to install. For those who can\'t access Google Play, you\'ll have to find a way to get the APK\u2014for instance, you can set up a V2Ray proxy on Windows and have your phone/tablet connect through it to install.\\n\\nAs for phones without Google Play Services... we\'ll save that topic for another day if the opportunity arises.\\n\\nFor usage instructions, check out [this guide written by a GFW-bypass expert](https://ssr.tools/342).\\n\\n### <img src=\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kiwi0093/graph/master/img/246x0w.png\\" alt=\\"Shadowrocket\\" style={{ width: \\"20px\\", height: \\"auto\\" }} />[Shadowrocket](https://apps.apple.com/jp/app/shadowrocket/id932747118) - iOS\\n\\nAs long as you don\'t use a Mainland China Apple Store account, you should be able to buy this app directly on the App Store (yes, it\'s the only paid one here, though iPhone users usually don\'t mind spending a few bucks).\\n\\nFor friends in China who have no other choice, you can only [~~use this unorthodox workaround~~ rely on kind-hearted folks](https://shadowsockshelp.github.io/ios/). However, third-party sharing methods easily lead to other installation issues that I\'m not an expert at troubleshooting.\\n\\nYou\'ll have to figure those out on your own, fellow seekers of the Way...\\n\\n\\n\\n# Conclusion\\n\\nIn conclusion, the most effective method is physically crossing the firewall, though even now I still haven\'t managed to do that myself. So this post serves as a reference for anyone who needs it (and mostly as my own notes). Hopefully one day the contents of this post become obsolete because they\'re no longer needed\u2014not because we lost the technical cat-and-mouse game..."},{"id":"2020/07/06-01","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/2020/07/06-01","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/b-2020-07-06-01.md","title":"Uncle\'s Ramblings - Hugo + GitHub","description":"Basic guide on how to set up a personal blog using Hugo with GitHub Pages","date":"2020-07-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Old-Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/old-blog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Markdown","permalink":"/en/tags/markdown"},{"inline":true,"label":"Blog","permalink":"/en/tags/blog"}],"readingTime":6.29,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Uncle\'s Ramblings - Hugo + GitHub","date":"2020-07-06T00:00:00.000Z","description":"Basic guide on how to set up a personal blog using Hugo with GitHub Pages","tags":["Old-Blog","Markdown","Blog"],"slug":"2020/07/06-01"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Escaping the GFW - V2Ray Server Setup Summary","permalink":"/en/2020/07/07-02"}},"content":":::info\\nThis article was automatically translated by Google \\"Gemini\\". The author does not guarantee its accuracy or wording.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info \\nThis article was reposted from the old site archive.\\n:::\\n\\n# Preface\\n\\nA few days ago, I watched [CTT](https://www.youtube.com/user/homergfunk/featured)\'s YouTube channel and discovered that Hugo + GitHub or Hugo + Netlify are also great options for building a blog. Considering that Blogger\'s editing mode really isn\'t that modern, and my hard work trying to switch over to Markdown syntax, starting with this post I plan to move this unread blog of mine from Blogger to GitHub\u2014and take the opportunity to learn the trendy Git way of managing my blog.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## Prerequisites\\n\\nThe new blog requires the following tools:\\n\\n* **[Hugo](https://gohugo.io/)** - A tool for generating static websites, available on Linux, Windows, macOS, FreeBSD, etc.\u2014a pure CLI tool.\\n\\n* **[Github](https://www.github.com)** - Arguably Microsoft\'s most generous service. We mainly use it because we can create our own projects for free and easily upload and manage them using `git` commands.\\n\\n* **[Remarkable](https://remarkable.com/)** or **[Typora](https://typora.io/)** or other Markdown editors - Mainly for comfortably editing `.md` files as the body of blog posts, which is also my primary reason for switching.\\n\\n* **[git](https://git-scm.com/)** - An ultra-powerful tool that basically comes pre-installed on Linux by default; if you\'re on Windows, you\'ll need to install it separately.\\n\\nSurprisingly, not many tools are needed, though since I\'m not familiar with it, I had to look up quite a lot of references. Actually, there are plenty of resources out there\u2014just search Google for *hugo tutorial* and tons of results pop up.\\n\\n## Installation Steps\\n\\n### Installing Hugo\\n\\nSince I mostly sneak in blog writing during work hours, my main machine runs Manjaro. Anyway, let\'s install Hugo first:\\n\\n```bash\\nsudo pacman -S hugo\\n```\\n\\n### Creating the GitHub Repos\\n\\nHere I recommend following [chanmitsu55](https://chanmitsu55.github.io/posts/2017/12/create-blog-by-hugo/)\'s approach.\\nFirst, you\'ll need a GitHub account\u2014it\'s free, just sign up with an email. Once registered, please create two repositories:\\n\\n1. A repo with the same name as your site; in my case, `Blog`.\\n2. A repo named `<username>github.io`.\\n\\nFirst, clone the repo with the same name as your local base directory:\\n\\n`git clone https://github.com/<username>/Blog.git`\\n\\n### Creating the Site Body, Copying to the Local Working Directory & Mounting the Public Submodule\\n\\nNext, create a new site. This step basically only needs to be done the first time:\\n\\n```bash\\nhugo new site _blog\\ncp -p -f -R _blog/* Blog/\\ncd Blog\\ngit submodule add -b master https://github.com/<username>/<username>.github.io.git public\\n```\\n\\nThe reason for copying it over first is that the Fuji theme I use is attached as a submodule, and you can\'t add a submodule outside a git directory.\\nAlso, the reason for attaching `public` before Hugo generates the site is that otherwise you\'d have to run *rm -rf public* later to attach it anyway, so you might as well attach it before anything is generated.\\n\\n### Installing a Theme\\n\\nNow we have a basic web framework. Next, let\'s install a theme so it actually looks like something.\\nI\'m pretty minimalist, so I picked a simple theme, [Fuji](https://themes.gohugo.io/hugo-theme-fuji/), though you can install whichever one you like.\\n\\n```bash\\ncd ~/Blog\\ngit submodule add https://github.com/amzrk2/hugo-theme-fuji.git themes/fuji\\n```\\n\\nThat installs the theme. Next, let\'s do some simple configuration for it:\\n\\n```bash\\ncp themes/fuji/exampleSite/config.toml ./\\ncp -R themes/fuji/exampleSite/content/archives ./content/\\ncp -R themes/fuji/exampleSite/content/search ./content/\\n```\\n\\nThe important part of this step is taking `config.toml` from `exampleSite` to modify; the other two are because I only plan to keep the Archives and Search menus, so I copied them over.\\n\\nNext, edit `config.toml`:\\n\\n`vim config.toml`\\n\\nThere are a few main items to change:\\n\\n```bash\\n#\u9019\u500B\u662F\u5B9A\u7FA9\u4F60\u7684\u4E3B\u9801\u7DB2\u5740,\u8ACB\u8DDF\u4F60\u7684\u8A2D\u5B9A\u4E00\u81F4\\nbaseURL = \\"https://Kiwi0093.github.io\\"\\t\\n#\u9019\u500B\u662F\u4F60\u7684\u7DB2\u9801\u4E3B\u8981Title\\ntitle = \\"\u4E2D\u5E74\u5927\u53D4\u7684\u81EA\u8A00\u81EA\u8A9E\\"\\n#\u9019\u500B\u662F\u5B9A\u7FA9\u4F60\u7DB2\u9801\u7684\u4E3B\u984C\\ntheme = \\"fuji\\"\\n\\n[params]\\n  #\u9019\u500B\u662F\u4F5C\u8005\u7684\u540D\u5B57\\n  author = \\"Kiwi.L\\"\\n  #\u9019\u500B\u662F\u7DB2\u9801\u7684\u526F\u6A19\\n  subTitle = \\"\u5C0D\u61C9\u8001\u5E74\u7661\u5446\u8207\u8A18\u61B6\u8870\u9000\u7684\u7B46\u8A18\\"\\t\\n\\n[taxonomies]\\n  #\u9019\u662F\u5B9A\u7FA9Tag\u6703\u4F7F\u7528tags\u4F86\u9032\u884C\u5206\u985E\\n  tag = \\"tags\\"\\n\\n[menu]\\n  #\u9019\u500B\u5B9A\u7FA9\u7684\u662F\u5C0E\u5411\u54EA\u908A\u800C\u975E\u7C21\u55AE\u7684link\\n  [[menu.nav]]\\n   #\u9019\u500B\u5B9A\u7FA9\u4E86\u53F3\u908A\u9023\u7D50\u7684Home\\n   name = \\"Home\\"\\n   #\u9023\u7DDA\u662F\u76F8\u5C0D\u4F4D\u7F6E*/*\u5C0D\u61C9\u7684\u662F*./content*\\n   url = \\"/\\"\\n   #\u9019\u500B\u5B9A\u7FA9\u4E86\u6392\u5E8F\\n   weight = 1\\n[[menu.nav]]\\n   name = \\"Archives\\"\\n   url = \\"/archives/\\"\\n   weight = 2\\n[[menu.nav]]\\n   name = \\"Search\\"\\n   url = \\"/search/\\"\\n   weight = 3\\n #\u9019\u500B\u5B9A\u7FA9\u7684\u662F\u7C21\u55AE\u7684link\\n[[menu.link]]\\n #\u9019\u500B\u662F\u4F60\u60F3\u8981\u8868\u73FE\u7684\u5B57\u4E32\\n   name = \\"R2d Blog\\"\\n   #\u9019\u500B\u662F\u4ED6\u7684\u9023\u7D50\\n   url = \\"https://roidintw.kaienroid.com/\\"\\n   #\u9019\u500B\u4E00\u6A23\u662F\u6392\u5E8F\\n   weight = 1\\n```\\n\\n### Creating and Writing a New Post\\n\\nWith that set up, the basic website framework is ready. Next, let\'s write a simple post:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd ~/Blog\\nhugo new post/what-ever-you-like-to-name.md\\n```\\n\\nThen open this file with your favorite editor and you will see:\\n\\n```bash\\n#\u9019\u500B---\u4E0D\u8981\u52D5\\n---\\n#\u9019\u500B\u662F\u9019\u500B\u6587\u4EF6\u7684\u4E3B\u984C\u6703\u51FA\u73FE\u5728Post\u7684\u6A19\u984C\u4E0A\\ntitle: \\"what-ever-you-like-to-name\\"\\n#\u9019\u500B\u662F\u6642\u9593\u6233\u8A18\\ndate: 2020-07-06\\n#\u9019\u662F\u6A19\u8A18\u662F\u5426\u70BA\u8349\u7A3F,\u5BEB\u597D\u8981\u6B63\u5F0F\u767C\u51FA\u53BB\u7684\u6587\u7AE0\u628A\u9019\u884C\u53BB\u6389\\ndraft: true\\n#\u589E\u52A0tag\u7684\u5BEB\u6CD5\\ntags: [ \\"A-tag\\", \\"B-tag\\", \\"C-tag\\" ]\\n#\u9019\u500B---\u4E0D\u8981\u52D5\\n---\\n```\\n\\nEdit the section above as the header of your file, and then write your blog content directly below using Markdown syntax. At this point, the core essentials are done; next comes generating the site and putting it on GitHub.\\n\\n### Generating the Site & Local Testing\\n\\nBefore pushing it live, please generate the site and preview it locally to make sure it looks the way you want:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd ~/Blog\\nhugo && hugo server\\n```\\n\\nRemember to follow the prompt to view it locally and see if anything else needs adjusting; if not, it\'s time to push it to GitHub!\\n\\n### Auto-Deploy Script\\n\\nFor this part, it\'s easy to forget the manual commit message part in the official recommended script, so here I\'m using [chanmitsu55]\'s version, which basically just appends the update timestamp at the end:\\n\\n```bash\\n#!/bin/bash\\n\\necho -e \\"\\\\033[0;32mDeploying updates to GitHub...\\\\033[0m\\"\\n\\n# Build the project.\u6211\u5011\u5DF2\u7D93\u5728config.toml\u88E1\u5B9A\u7FA9\u4E86theme\u6240\u4EE5\u7C21\u55AE\u7684hugo\u5C31\u5920\u4E86\\nhugo\\n\\n# Go To Public folder\\ncd public\\n# Add changes to git.\\ngit add .\\n\\n# Commit changes.\\nmsg=\\"rebuilding site `date`\\"\\n   if [ $# -eq 1 ]\\n   then msg=\\"$1\\"\\n   fi\\ngit commit -m \\"$msg\\"\\n\\n# Push source and build repos.\\ngit push origin master\\n\\n# Come Back up to the Project Root\\ncd ..\\n\\n# Commit source repository changes\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"$msg\\"\\ngit push\\n```\\n\\nI don\'t like making it an executable directly, so I put the above contents in `deploy.sh` and run it with:\\n\\n`sh ./deploy.sh`\\n\\nDuring the process, it will ask for your GitHub username and password twice\u2014once for pushing `Blog` and once for `Public`\u2014don\'t mistype them.\\n\\n### Working Remotely Across Machines\\n\\nWhat if you also want to edit on other machines? Basically, you just need the following command to pull everything down from scratch:\\n\\n`git clone --recursive https://github.com/<username>/Blog.git`\\n\\nOn machines that already have an existing git folder, you can just write new updates, because `git push`/`pull` compare diffs.\\nFiles that aren\'t updated won\'t be uploaded (of course, if you\'re paranoid and have unlimited bandwidth, you can re-clone every time).\\nThat way, we can ensure our data is basically all stored on GitHub."}]}}')}}]);